1 OCCU hertog van Gelre, hertog van Gelre en graaf van Zutphen
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ==Links:==
2 CONT *[http://www.thepeerage.com/p760.htm#i7597 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://geneall.net/de/name/402902/carl-van-egmond-herzog-von-geldern/ Geneall]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_II,_Duke_of_Guelders Wikipedia]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Stamboom nr. : 52.298.
2 CONT >
2 CONT Generatie : 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT At the battle of Nijmegen the six year old Charles was captured by Charles the Bold and taken to the Burgundian court in Ghent were he was educated.
2 CONT
2 CONT While fighting in the Burgundian army, Charles of Gelre was captured during the battle of Béthune (near Calais, France). After a five year imprisonment and a paying large ransom he was released in 1492 and became the official Duke
2 CONC  of Gelre, ending the bitter internal conflict.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Maximilian subsequently managed to acquire the Burgundian lands for the Habsburgs by marriage. In 1492, the citizens of Guelders, disenchanted with Maximilian's rule, ransomed Charles and recognized him as their Duke. Charles
2 CONC  was supported by the French King, but in 1505, Guelders was regained by King Maximilian's son Philip the Handsome. Charles had to accompany Philip to Spain to attend Philip's coronation as King of Castile but at Antwerp, Charles 
2 CONC managed to escape. Shortly afterwards, Philip died in Spain and by July 1513 Charles had regained control over the whole of Guelders. In his conflict with the Habsburgs, Charles also became a major player behind the scenes of the 
2 CONC Frisian peasant rebellion and at first financially supported the rebel leader Pier Gerlofs Donia. After the tides turned against the rebels, Charles stopped his support and switched sides together with his military commander Maart
2 CONC en van Rossum. In the Treaty of Gorinchem (1528), Emperor Charles, son of Philip the Handsome, proposed to recognize Charles of Egmond as Duke of Guelders under the condition that he would inherit the Duchy should the Duke die wit
2 CONC hout issue. The Duke, who at the time did not have any children, delayed signing the treaty. Another battle ensued, after which the passage was removed from the treaty. In 1536 there was finally peace between Guelders and Burgundy
2 CONC  with the Treaty of Grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles died at Arnhem, and is buried in the St. Eusebius Church there
2 CONT
2 CONT His only legitimate (twin) sister, Philippa (1467–1547), survived him and died during the reign of her great-grandson, Charles III, Duke of Lorraine (1543–1608).
2 CONT
2 CONT Maarten van Rossum (c. 1478 – June 7, 1555) was a Dutch military tactician who became field marshal in the service of Charles, Duke of Guelders.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/Maarten-van-Rossem/6000000021482227244 Sources ::
2 CONT
2 CONT http://paulbuddehistory.com/europe/gelre/
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_II,_Duke_of_Guelders
