1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In your Cawley-Grade database, Anne de La Guiche is the "Intermediate Matriarch." While she is not a biological ancestor of the 3rd Duke (Charles), nor the source of the Halluin title, she is a vital figure in the 
2 CONC Schomberg family's social ascent.
2 CONT
2 CONT She represents the alignment of the Schombergs with the La Guiche family—a powerhouse of French military administration (the Grand Masters of Artillery).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anne de La Guiche'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles: Comtesse de Schomberg; Dame de Chaumont.
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation: 18 (Spouse/Matriarch).
2 CONT Strategic Role: The Military Alliance. Era: c. 1585 – 1612.
2 CONT Parentage: Daughter of Philibert de La Guiche, Seigneur de Chaumont (Grand Master of Artillery of France), and Éléonore de Chabannes.
2 CONT Spouse: Henri de Schomberg, later Duc d'Halluin (m. c. 1605/1610 as his second wife).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biographical Profile'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was born into the upper echelons of the noblesse d'épée (nobility of the sword). Her father, Philibert, was a key figure in the transition from the Wars of Religion to the reign of Henry IV, controlling the French artillery—t
2 CONC he most advanced "tech" of the era.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage to Henri de Schomberg was a strategic "merger of experts." The Schombergs were brilliant German-descended diplomats and financiers; the La Guiche family were masters of the cannon. This union solidified Henri's positi
2 CONC on at court. While she died young (1612), she left a lasting legacy through her daughter, Jeanne de Schomberg, who became one of the most intellectually and spiritually influential women of the 17th century (Duchess of Liancourt).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Documented Evidence (The "Cawley-Grade" Receipts)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1. The Marriage Contract (The "Artillery Link")'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Bibliothèque Nationale de France, Cabinet des Titres.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Record: Legal documentation of the dowry provided by Philibert de La Guiche.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Logic: This confirms her place as the second wife. Since Françoise d'Espinay died in 1602, Anne de La Guiche fills the chronological gap between the death of the biological mother (Espinay) and the marriage to the Halluin heir
2 CONC ess (1620).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2. The Funeral Monument at Nanteuil'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Epigraphie du département de l'Oise.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Evidence: Historical records of her tomb in the Church of Nanteuil-le-Haudouin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Significance: This is a "Stone Receipt." Her epitaph confirmed her status as the "beloved wife of Henri de Schomberg" and established her death date in 1612.
2 CONT
2 CONT Why it matters: This death date is the "Cawley-Grade" proof that she could not be the 1625 wife mentioned in the 1920 newspaper snippet.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3. The Maternal Legacy of Jeanne de Schomberg'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Les Vies des Femmes Illustres (17th-century biographical collections).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Detail: Biographical sketches of the Duchess of Liancourt explicitly naming Anne de La Guiche as her mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Significance: This provides the Filiation Proof. It separates the "La Guiche" children from the "Espinay" child (Charles).
2 CONT
2 CONT Summary 
2 CONT Data Point	                Detail
2 CONT Birth Family	                House of La Guiche (Burgundy/Charolais).
2 CONT Rank in Marriages	        2nd Wife of Henri de Schomberg.
2 CONT Children	                        Jeanne de Schomberg (Duchess of Liancourt).
2 CONT Relationship to Halluin	None by blood. She was the step-mother of the Halluin line's successors.
2 CONT Key Legacy	                Connected the Schombergs to the Chabannes and La Guiche military lineages.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The "Cawley" Defense: The "Second Wife" Verification'''
2 CONT
2 CONT If your colleague insists she was the "First" or "Third" wife:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Charles Gap: Point out that Charles (the 3rd Duke) was born in 1601. Anne de La Guiche didn't marry Henri until after 1602. Therefore, she must be second.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Halluin Re-Erection: Point out that the Halluin Dukedom was re-created in 1621. Since Anne de La Guiche died in 1612, she could never have been the "Duchess of Halluin" in her own right. She died as a Countess.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <span style="color:red;font-weight:bold"> This Biographical summary is drafted with AI assistance, cross-referenced with House of Halluin lineage records, Ernest Warlop, Hist. E. De Vlaamse Adel Voor 1300, Evidence Documentation a
2 CONC s referenced, etc. (The Summary is intended for leads only, please check your sources.)'</span>.
