1 NOTE Maximilian II of the Hapsburg- Spanish-Austria line, son of Ferdinand I, succeeded his father as Emperor of Germany in 1564.  He was succeeded in 1576 by his son Rudolph II.
1 NOTE HOLY ROMAN EMPORER 1564-1576
1 NOTE Maximilian II (1527-76), German emperor, the son of Ferdinand I, was born in Vienna, and succeeded to the throne in 1564. His reign was peaceful and uneventful, except that he kept the Turks in check in Hungary (1566-8). See Life,
2 CONC  by Holtzmann (1903). [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
1 NOTE A Hapsburg ruler who failed
2 CONT
2 CONT EMPEROR MAXIMILIAN II
2 CONT By Paula Sutter Fichtner
2 CONT Yale University Press, $30, 344 pages, illus.
2 CONT REVIEWED BY BRIDGET HEAL
2 CONT
2 CONT "By virtually all standards, including his own, Emperor Maximilian II (1527-1576) was a failure." So begins Paula Sutter Fichtner's account of the life and times of one of 16th-century Europe's least effective rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a Hapsburg, Maximilian was a member of one of Europe's leading dynasties. His uncle, Charles V, had built up a massive empire, uniting the Netherlands, Spain, the New World, Naples, Sicily and Germany under his leadership. Char
2 CONC les' son, Philip, inherited the Spanish part of this empire and the Netherlands, while Germany passed to Charles' brother, Ferdinand, and thence, in 1562, to Ferdinand's son, Maximilian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles V and Philip II are two of the great figures of European history and have been the subjects of numerous studies, but "Emperor Maximillian II" is the first full biography of Maximilian II in English. Perhaps not surprisingl
2 CONC y, previous scholars have been deterred by Maximilian's inadequacies &#8212; his inability to achieve any of his stated aims and his failure to make any lasting impression on Europe's political and religious landscape. As the auth
2 CONC or points out, however, history is not just about great achievements. It is about the totality of past experience, in which failure and defeat have inevitably played a central role.
