1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY: Note that the ancestry of Jean Claude is highly disputed.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: From, "Acadian Genealogy Exchange", Vol. XXII, No. 2, pp. 39-42 , 'Steve White's Corner' - Response to letter from Dr. Donald J. Landry of Metarie, LA:
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: "Some researchers are under the impression that the ancestor of the LANDRYS was Jean Claude LANDRY, spouse of Marie SALLE, but no one of this name appears in any document concerning Acadians from the beginning.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: "In the censuses of 1671 and 1678, the deceased husband of Marie SALLE is simply called Jean (or even Jehan according to the 1671 census) Claude. A researcher with a lot imagination added LANDRY to the name of Jean Clau
2 CONC de in order to explain why Marie SALLE lived between Rene LANDRY the younger and his son Antoine LANDRY on the census of 1686. It was long presumed that this Marie SALLE was the same as the one who was married to Martin AUCOIN at 
2 CONC LaRochelle in 1632, which could be true and that she was related, by the first marriage to Michelle AUCOIN, to the daughter at whose home she lived in the censuses of 1671 and 1678, which is also possible. The only way the researc
2 CONC hers could prove the association of Marie SALLE to Rene LANDRY the younger was in transforming her husband Jean Claude to Jean Claude LANDRY and in presuming moreover that this would be the father of Rene the younger. We add that 
2 CONC this reasoning doesn't hold water. It isn't logical to suppose that the family name of Jean Claude was omitted by two distinct census takers. we note that there are those who have already rejected the faulty idea that the second s
2 CONC pouse of Marie SALLE was a LANDRY and have even advanced the hypothesis that he was an Amerindian. This is possible, but we mention that it is also possible he was a Frenchman because CL AUDE was a distinguished family name in Fre
2 CONC nch households in the 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: "Censuses give us no other indication of the origin of Jean Claude so his origin as all the French origins of the LANDRYS, too, remain to be determined."
1 NOTE Be sure and read the fourth and fifth internal textfiles which make it very difficult to understand this family, which, as I have here is totally wrong.  Since it is apparent that even the best researchers of the Landry family see
2 CONC m to disagree I will let it stand as is until further information becomes available.
2 CONT A short bio of Antoine Bourg acquired from the internet (copy filed L-13-30).
2 CONT <http://www.geocities.com/ronald_gaudet2001/wga106.html>  (copy filed L-37-2).
2 CONT <http://www.ultranet.com/~arthurl/WC31/WC31_323.htm> (copy filed L-14-31).
2 CONT <http://home.attbi.com/~sodapop68/webtree/pafg52.htm> (copy filed L-13-30).
2 CONT <http://home.attbi.com/~sodapop68/webtree/pafg52.htm> (copy filed L-14-31) show born in LaVentrouze, Mortagne, France.
2 CONT <http://membres.lycos.fr/ancetre/LandryJC.htm> (copy filed L-14-31) also in his internal file.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://216.239.39.104/translate_c?hl=en&sl=fr&u=http://membres.lycos.fr/ancetre/LandryJC.htm&prev=/search%3Fq%3D%2522jean%2Bclaude%2Blandry%2522%26hl%3Den%26lr%3D%26ie%3DUTF-8>:
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE:  The following gives you two versions of Jean Claude Landry's bio.  It is confusing, like many of our ancesters.  An article with greater definition of the Landry Genealogy is located in the following two (2) internal textfi
2 CONC les.  Be sure to read this article, which is in english, and most informative.
2 CONT
2 CONT JEAN-CLAUDE LANDRY
2 CONT Version # 1 :
2 CONT J ean-Claude Landry, is originating in Ventrouze , in Mortagne, with the Pole. It arrives towards the end of 1640, in company of his two children, which one is unaware of the name of the wife. Rene Landry, born in 1618, and Antoin
2 CONC ette Landry, born in 1620, in Ventrouze. Jean-Claude Landry also is accompanied by his new wife, married in 1625, Marie Sallé, widow of Martin Aucoin, and the Aucoin children, from of which Michelle Aucoin, born in 1618, which mar
2 CONC ries in 1641, Michel Boudrot , who has also a bond of descent in this genealogy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The elder Rene Landry, born in 1618, wife with Port-Royal about 1644, Perrine Borough, born in 1626, originating in Loudunois, sister of Antoine Bourg , born about 1609, married since 1636, with his/her sister Antoinette Landry, b
2 CONC orn in 1620. Of the five children of the couple Perrine Borough and Rene Landry, two girls weave us branches. The elder one, Marie or Marie-Henriette Landry, born in 1648, with Port-Royal, wife in 1667, Lawrence-Laurent Granger, s
2 CONC ailor, originating in Plymouth in England. Second Landry, Magdeleine, born in 1656, wife in 1680, with Port-Royal, Rene Richard, sior of Bowsprit, born in 1657, wire of the ancestor originating in Saintonge, Michel Richard , known
2 CONC  as Sansoucy and of Magdeleine Blanchard . After the death of Rene Richard, Magdeleine Landry marries in 1693, Pierre Dupuis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before arriving in Acadie, Jean-Claude Landry and Marie Sallé, had at least a son, also named Rene Landry, the junior, who differentiate it from Rene Landry, the elder one, his half-brother. Rene (the junior), born in 1634, wife a
2 CONC bout 1659, Marie Bernard, born in France, girl of Andre Bernard , man of the Tower, and Andree Guion. A girl of Rene Landry and Marie Bernard weaves us also another branch in Landry. Marie Landry, born in 1670, in Pre Grand, becom
2 CONC es the wife in 1686, of Martin Dupuis, born with Port-Royal, in 1665, wire of Michel Dupeux, known as Dupuis and of Marie Gautherot.
2 CONT
2 CONT The grandmother Marie Sallé dies between 1686-1693.
2 CONT
2 CONT The etymology of the name Landry is of origin Germanic, Landrik (nation or ground - powerful). There are several variations of the names like: Landre, Landru and Landron.
2 CONT
2 CONT Version # 2 :
2 CONT
2 CONT Professor Stephen White of the Center of acadian studies of the University of Moncton, indicates that there is no proof that Jean-Claude Landry and Marie Salle are the parents of Rene Landry. The name of Jehan Claude (John Clause)
2 CONC  is registered with some recoveries with the registers of Port-Royal as a amérindien Micmac.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen White is remaking the complete genealogy of acadian and does not name aucuns parents, nor origins for the Rene Landry. Moreover, it would seem that they are not even parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT It explains there why there is no specific documentation to prove that the acadian Landry ancestors are originating in LaChaussée, even if it recognizes that a majority of the first colonists of Acadie, came from Loudunois and tha
2 CONC t lived many Landry in the surroundings of LaChaussée at the 17th century, but there, no certainty which Landry d' Acadie do not come from this area.
2 CONT
2 CONT The task to establish the descent of Landry d' Acadie is very difficult to establish. The researchers established that there were two Rene Landry established in Acadie. Some research found a Rene Landry native of Lachaussée as a f
2 CONC ounder of the clan in Acadie. It was born in 1618 and arrived in 1640 and there, it married Perrine Bourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Research shows that the other Rene Landry, was born in 1634, probably in Martiaze or Loudon, in France. His/her parents are unknown. One believes that it married a Marie Bernard in 1659, in France. It arrived in Acadie, the same y
2 CONC ear. Some researchers tried to join both Rene like father and wire. Other acadian recordings quote them like Rene the elder one and Rene the Young person or the Junior.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, it is necessary to be questioned on several facts. Rene the elder one was born in 1618, while Rene the Junior was born in 1634. If it is exact, it would be only only 16 years old of difference between the two. However, th
2 CONC e father Clarence d' Entremont, historian and genealogist of News-Scotland, declare "I have an example in Acadie, that a child was born with an old father from only 15 years". However, Rene the elder one arrived in Acadie in 1640,
2 CONC  while Rene the Young person arrived in 1659, suggesting that a separation existed between them. The father Archangel Godbout, one of the most exceptional genealogists of his time in French Canada, in his dictionary of Acadian, in
2 CONC dicates on page 423, that the second Rene Landry was born in 1634, arrives from France, with his wife a Marie Bernard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert C.West, in his "Atlas of the surnames of Louisiana of French and Spanish origin, on page 9 and 12, declares that Rene Landry, the young person, emigrated of France, with his wife Marie Bernard, in Acadie, about 1659. Other 
2 CONC researchers propose rather than Rene LeJeune married after his arrival in Acadie. Cece is based on the recordings "of Rene Of Heve Landry (- - - of France) Mr. to Port-Royal, about 1659, with Marie Bernard (- - - of France)" found
2 CONC  in the French Canadian public records, vol.III, where Victor Landry, on page 751 known as that Rene Landry the Young person emigrated in Acadie, in 1653, with a group which include Emmanuel Leborgne and Marie Bernard and that the
2 CONC  latter became the wife of Rene Landry. However, no documentation can prove to support this position.
2 CONT
2 CONT And moreover, some researchers désigé Rene LeJeune, like the son of a Jean-Claude Landry and of Marie Salle. The writings in the publication "Acadian Families" by the father Léopold Lanctot makes suggestions of the origins of Land
2 CONC ry. It declares that all started in 1640, when a group of 10 Landry families came in Acadie, of France. The Landry families arrived of Ventrouze, of Mortagne at the Pole, current department of the Flowering ash, to France. They ha
2 CONC ve been encouraged to come in Acadie, by Marguerite Landry, girl of Jean-Claude, and her husband, Robert Martin, were installed in Acadie for a few years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The goupe of 10 were composed of Jean-Claude Landry, and his three children of its first marriage. Twins; Rene and Antoinette (22 years) and Perrine (29 years) with her husband Jacques Joffriau, as well as second wife of Jean-Clau
2 CONC de, Marie Salle (40 years) and three children of its first marriage with Martin Aucouin. These three children were Michelle (22 years), François (18 years) and Jeanne Aucouin (8 years). Also in the group, there were Rene (6 years)
2 CONC , wire of Jean-Claude and Marie. The group was probably established close to the Midsummer's Day river, in the Cape-with-Sand area. Later they moved with Port-Royal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert  Rochon
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.landrystuff.com/landry.htm> and might help with the confusion in this genealogy.
2 CONT
2 CONT LANDRY
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the surnames of French origin in Louisiana, that of LANDRY is second only to Hebert in its' frequency. Like many other French names in the state, most of today's Landrys can trace their ancestory to the Acadian refugees who 
2 CONC came at various times during the last half of the eighteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT The actual progenitor of the Landry family in Acadia is somewhat ambigous between two settlers bearing identical names, Rene Landry. Rene Landry (l'aine or the elder) of LaChausee, France, arrived in Acadia around 1639. He married
2 CONC  Perrine Bourg that same year. Another Rene Landry (l'jeune or the younger) was born in France during 1634, parents unknown (some research indicates his mother was a Marie Sale'). Rene l'jeune married around 1659 in France to Mari
2 CONC e Bernard, the daughter of Andre' Bernard and Andree Guion. Rene and Marie arrived in Acadia in 1659. According to a 1678 census of Acadia, Marie Sale' had made her way to North America and was living with her son Rene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rene and Marie had fifteen children. Several generations of Landry's spread throughout Acadia; at the time of the 1755 Expulsion , the Landry's formed a large group living in various parts, mainly Grand Pre' and Pisiquit. Rene I d
2 CONC ied in 1692 at Port Royal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having first been exiled to various parts of the British colonies along the Atlantic seaboard, a sizeable number of Landrys reached Louisiana during the 1760's. During the antebellum times, a number of Landrys in Ascension Parish 
2 CONC became well known sugar planters with many slaves on large plantations along the Mississippi River.
2 CONT
2 CONT Less affluent lines of Landrys settled in other areas of Louisiana. The first Landry to migrate into the Teche country of the Attakapas District was Firmin Landry , son of Alexandre Landry and Marguerite Blanchard. A Maryland exil
2 CONC e and widower (married 1752 to Elizabeth Thibodeau), Firmin had taken up land on the upper Bayou Vermillion with his two grown sons, Joseph and Saturin , by 1770. In 1776 Firmin married Theotiste Thibodeaux. Later, Firmin obtained
2 CONC  a small tract of land on the Teche at Fausse Point near present Loreauville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Firmin's son Saturin apparently remained single, but Joseph settling on Bayou Vermillion married in 1775 to Marie- Ann Melancon, daughter of Paul Melancon and Marie Theriot. It is from Joseph and Marie-Ann that this line of Landry
2 CONC s continues.
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www-faculty.cs.uiuc.edu/~friedman/genealogy/Landry.htm>:
2 CONT
2 CONT LANDRY
2 CONT
2 CONT Five separate and, as far as is known, unrelated Landry families migrated to North America.  Two of these are ancestral to our family.  Both migrated from France and settled in Acadia in the middle 1600s, and curiously, both were 
2 CONC headed by men named René; they are therefore distinguished from each other as René Landry l'Aîné (the older) and René Landry le Jeune (the younger).
2 CONT
2 CONT Some authors in the past have asserted that the two René Landrys were half-brothers, sons of a Jean Claude Landry.  The mother of René l'Aîné was listed as unknown, and the mother of René le Jeune was listed as Marie Sallé.  Marie
2 CONC  Salle is known, and is found on Acadian censuses as widow of Jean or Jehan Claude; it was assumed that the census takers omitted his surname, Landry.  However, this entire scheme was based on guesswork.  There is no evidence for 
2 CONC it, and it is now discredited.  According to Fr. Clarence J. d'Entremont, Jehan or Jean Claude was a Micmac Indian, and no such person as Jean Claude Landry ever existed.  According to research by Stephen A. White into Catholic Ch
2 CONC urch marriage dispensations, René Landry le Jeune could not have been more closely related to René Landry l'Aîné and his sisters Perrine Landry and Antoinette Landry than cousins in the third degree.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to the destruction of records, the origins and parents of neither of these two families is likely ever to be known.  It may be conjectured that they originated in La Chaussée in the Loudun area of Poitou in west central France
2 CONC , because many Acadian families originated there, and because the name Landry is well known there but much rarer elsewhere in France.  However, the conjecture cannot be proven with records now known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our family is descended in the direct male line from René Landry le Jeune to Eleda Landry Friedman, and in the female line from Antoinette Landry, sister of René Landry l'Aîné.
2 CONT
2 CONT A third Landry family was that of Guillaume Landry, son of Mathurin Landry and Damiane Desavin (or Desavis), who migrated from France to Québec in the 1650s.  Guillaume is known to have been born at La Ventrouze-au-Perche, and man
2 CONC y Acadian families came from that area.  It is therefore tempting to assume that one or both of the René Landrys came from there.  Again, however, there is no evidence.  As far as is known, our family is not descended from Mathuri
2 CONC n or Guillaume.
2 CONT
2 CONT A fourth migration of a Landry family was that of two sons of Jean Jacques Landry and Susanne Catherine Frasse (or Fraze), Jean Jacques Henry Landry and Charles Henri Landry.  They migrated from Neufchatel, Switzerland, and settle
2 CONC d in Louisiana in the 1820s and 1830s, and they have many descendants today in the Lafayette, LA area.  As far as is known, our family is not descended from this Swiss branch of Landrys.
2 CONT
2 CONT A fifth Landry family migrated to Louisiana in the early 1800s in the person of Alexandre Charles Landry, son of Etienne Nicholas Landry and Marie Adelaide Lepen of St. Sulpice Parish in Paris, France.  Alexandre Charles married L
2 CONC ouise Antoinette Peytavin on April 1, 1811 at St. Martin's Church, and they settled in the Opelousas area.  Again, as far as is known, our family is not descended from this branch of Landrys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information cannot be copyrighted.  A particular collection of information in a particular format can be.  This collection is copyright ? 2001, 2002, 2004 by H. George Friedman, Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.   ______ LANDRY.
2 CONT 1.   Perrine LANDRY b. c. 1611 in France; m. c. 1640 in France Jacques JOFFRIAU.
2 CONT No children.
2 CONT 2.   René LANDRY l'Aîné [the older] b. c. 1618 in La Ventrouze, France, d. between 1678 & 1686; m. c.
2 CONT 1645 in Port Royal, Acadia Perrine BOURG (BOURC), her 2nd m., b. c. 1626 in Martaize, France, d.
2 CONT after 1693; her 1st m. c. 1640 to Simon PELLETRET (PELLERET).
2 CONT 5 children of René and Perrine, 2 children of Simon and Perrine.
2 CONT 3.   Antoinette (Anthoinette) LANDRY b. c. 1618 in France; m. c. 1642 at Port Royal, Acadia Antoine
2 CONT (Anthoine) BOURG b. c. 1609 in Martaize, France, d. 10/5/1687 in Acadia.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.kateritekakwitha.org/ancestry/landry.htm> (copy filed L-14-31).
2 CONT
2 CONT The following article is taken from Landry Family Genealogy.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Professor White, there is no specific documentation to show that the ancestors of the Acadian Landrys actually originated from LaChaussee. Even though it is known that a majority of the first colonists in Acadia came 
2 CONC from Loundunais [Genevieve Massignon, in her linguistic analysis] and there were numerous Landrys in the vicinity of LaChaussee in the 17th century, there is no certainty that the Landrys to arrive in Acadia were from that area.
2 CONT The task of establishing the progenitor of the Landry's of Acadia is problematical to say the least. Researchers have established that there were two Rene Landrys to settle early in Acadia. Some investigators have named one Rene L
2 CONC andry, native of LaChausee, as the founder of the clan in Acadia. He shows to be born ca 1618. He arrived ca 1640 and there married Perrine Bourg. The other Rene Landry shows to be born ca 1634, probably in Martiaze or Loudon, Fra
2 CONC nce. His parents are unknown. It is believed he married a Marie Bernard in 1659 while still in France. He arrived in Acadia that same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some researchers have attempted to link the two Renes as father and son. Some Acadian records list them as Rene L'aine [the elder] and Rene le Jeunne [the younger]. However, that is questionable due to several facts. Rene L'aine w
2 CONC as born in 1618 while Rene le Jeunne was born in 1634. If this is correct, there would only be 16 years difference between the two. However, Father Clarence d'Entremont, historian and genealogist from Novia Scotia, states
2 CONT
2 CONT "I have an example in Acadia of a child when he was born had a father 15 years of age".
2 CONT However, Rene L'aine arrived in Acadia ca 1640 whereas Rene le Jeune arrived in 1659, suggesting a seperation would have existed between the two. Father Archange Godbout [who was in his time one of the most outstanding genealogist
2 CONC s in French Canada] in his "Dictionanaire des Acadiens" say plainly on page 423 that the second Rene Landry, born 1634, "came from France with his wife". [Venu de France avec sa femme, marie vers 1659 a Marie Bernard].
2 CONT Robert C. West in his "Atlas of Louisiana Surnames of French and Spanish Origin" on p.9 and 12 states: Rene Landry, le jeunne, emigrated from France, with his wife Marie Bernard, to Acadie about 1659". Some researchers suggest tha
2 CONC t Rene le Jeunne married after arriving in Acadia. This is based on records "Rene De La Heve Landry (---de France) m. at Port Royal vers 1659 Marie Bernard (---de France)" found in the National Canadiens Francais , Vol. I-II, p.75
2 CONC 1.
2 CONT
2 CONT Victor Landry claims that Rene Landry le Jeunne emigrated to Acadia in 1653 with a group that included Emmanuel Leborgne and Marie Bernard, who latter became Rene's wife. [Victor Landry...MY FAMILY-THE LANDRY GENEALOGY (as given b
2 CONC y an older member of the family).] However, no documentation can be found to support this position.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthurmore, some researchers have shown Rene le Jeune to be the son of a Jean-Claude Landry and Marie Sale'. Writings in the publication" Familles Acadienne", Father Leopold Lanctot makes suggestions to the beginnings of the Land
2 CONC rys in the New World. He states
2 CONT
2 CONT "It all began in the year 1640 when a group of 10 from the Landry family came to Acadia from France. The Landry family were originally from La Ventrouze, near Mortagne-au-Perche, Department of Orne, France. They were encouraged to
2 CONC  come to Acadia by Marguerite Landry, daughter of Jean-Claude, and her husband Robert Martin. They had been in Acadia for several years.
2 CONT The group of 10 consisted of Jean-Claude Landry, and three children from his first marriage; twin Rene (22) and Antoinette (22), and Perrine (29) with her first husband Jacques Joffriau. Also was Jean-Claude's wife by second marri
2 CONC age, Marie Sale'(40) and three children from her first marriage to Martin Aucoin. These children were Michelle (22), Francois (18), and Jeannne Aucoin (8). Also in the group was Rene (6), son of Jean-Claude and Marie. The group pr
2 CONC obably settled near the river Saint-John in the Cape Sable area. They later moved to Port Royal. Please note that there were two named Rene in this group; Rene (the elder) , son of Jean-Claude by his first marriage, and Rene (the 
2 CONC younger), son of Jean-Claude and Marie. Rene (the elder) married Perrine Bourg in 1645."
2 CONT
2 CONT This infomation was also found in the "Histoire et Geneologic des Acadians" by Arsenault on pp 623-624. "Jean-Claude Landry (born 1593) and Marie Sale' (born 1600), daughter of Jean Denys Sale' and Francoise Arnaud, were married i
2 CONC n Orne Department, France, in 1633. This was a second marriage for both. One child was born from this marriage, Rene, born in 1634. Jean-Claude died in 1671 in Mortagne-Au-Perche, France.
2 CONT The name of Marie Sale', age 86, appears in the 1686 Census of Acadia living with her son "Rene." This is somewhat based on the information obtained in the 1686 census where Marie Sale' (widow of Jean Claude) is shown residing nex
2 CONC t door to Rene Landry and beyond Rene's oldest son, Antoine. In discussing Marie Sale's relationship to Rene Landry, Father d'Entremont states the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The listing of Marie Sale', age 86, in close proximity to Rene Landry and his family in the 1686 census, for some time caused confusion, amongst early genealogists, concerning the parents of Rene "le Jeune" Landry." Father d'Entr
2 CONC emont continues "that Bona Arsenault, in his 1978 publication, p.624..footnote71, says that according to Father Godbout, Rene Landry [husband of Marie Bernard] would have had as a mother Marie Sale'.
2 CONT This prompted a series of errors, one of which was to create a fictious Jean-Claude Landry, because Marie Sale' is said to be the widow of Jean Claude, who is in fact believed to have been a Micmac Indian. NOWHERE in any census or
2 CONC  other documents is there to be found an Acadian by the name Jean-Claude Landry."
2 CONT In a recent letter dated July 30, 1997, to Dr. Don Landry, Father d'Entremont reinforces this by the following "Jehan (or John) Claude was an indian, of the Micmac tribe. His name occurs twice in the Port Royal Church registers, A
2 CONC LWAYS as Jehan Clause, NEVER given as family name Landry. As a matter of fact, if "Clause" had not been his family name, it would mean that a register gives him his first and SECOND name. Moreover, the registers of old ALWAYS give
2 CONC  the WHOLE name of persons; but the fact is that Jehan Clause has his name given thus, NEVER with another name added to those two. If the family name had been omitted in the registers, it would be the only time that such a thing o
2 CONC ccurs in any register. Thus CLAUDE was the family name.
2 CONT
2 CONT I do not have Father Leopold Lanctot's work "Familles Acadiennes". But I have of him L'ACADIE DES ORIGINES-1603-1771. I just took it out of my library and all along the margin of the eight first chapters of this work, I have writt
2 CONC en (in the past) all along the margins FALSE and ERROR, etc. While reading it, I must have put the book aside after the 10th chapter on the account of the amount of errors I found in that book. I must say that in the work, he give
2 CONC s again, Landry as the family name of Jehan Clause. I must add that up to about 50 years ago, there were indians living in this vicinity of the Pubnicos. Some of these families had as a family name "Glaude". I always asked myself 
2 CONC if this "Glaude" was not a corruption of "Clause" Father d'Entremont continues
2 CONT
2 CONT "So, who was the father and mother of Rene Landry? I do not know nor does anybody know....Thus the descendants of Rene Landry....in my humble opinion, cannot go furthur up in genealogy on the Landry side, as we do not know who the
2 CONC  parents of this Rene Landry were!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Bernard was born ca.1645 probably in Beauvoir-Sur-Mer, France. There is some question also as to who her parents were. There is considerable proof that her mother was "Marie" Andree Guyon (Guidon). Professor White in his "Di
2 CONC ctionaire genealogique des families acadiennes, partie I (1636-1714) " states ....."Marie Bernard d/o (?? Bernard) & Andree Guion , born 1645". However, Bona Arcenault's "Histoire et genealogie des Acadians", Vol 2, p428-429, stat
2 CONC es that Marie Bernard's father was Andre Bernard:
2 CONT
2 CONT ANDRE BERNARD, born 1620, Macon, originating in Beauvoir-sur-Mer, arrived in Acadia in 1641, "pour aller servir en l'habitation" for Charles de Latour, a Jemseg, on the river Saint-Jean in New Brunswick. He is the father of Jeanne
2 CONC , born 1643; Marie, born 1645; Nicholas, born 1662; and Rene, born 1663. "Apres la prise du fort de Jemseg par D'Aulnay", in 1645, he returned to France to return to Acadia "apres la rehabitation de Charles de LaTour, par la cour 
2 CONC de France." According to footnotes on page 428, Andre Bernard "Lors de la prise du fort de Jemseg, par Charles d' Aulnay, gouverneur d'Acadia, le 17 avril 1645, plusieurs compagnons de Charles de LaTour, faits prisonnies, ont ete 
2 CONC pendus sur place, apres deliberation du Conseil, pour servir d'exemple et de memoire a la poste-rite, d'une si obstinee rebellion. Andre Bernard est epargne et il en signe l'attestation, le 15 mai 1645. (Couillard, Charles de Sain
2 CONC t-Etienne de La Tour, p.489).
2 CONT
2 CONT Disputing the fact that Andre Bernard was Marie Bernard's father is the following from Bona Arcenaults publication quoting "Father Godbout indicates that the mother of the child Marie Bernard is Andree Guion. Her father is not And
2 CONC re Bernard......."
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite all the confusion to this point, it is agreed upon that Rene Landry le Jeunne and Marie Bernard were married and in Acadia in 1659. Together, they had fifteen (15) children, all born at Port Royal. They were Antoine, born 
2 CONC 1660; Claude, 1663; Cecile, 1664; Jean, 1666; Rene, 1668; Marie, 1670; Marguerite, 1672; Germain, 1674; Jeanne, 1676; Abraham, 1678; Pierre, 1680; Catherine, 1682; Anne, 1684; Charles, 1688; and Isabelle, 1691.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rene Landry died ca. 1692. In the 1714 census, it is indicated that Rene Landry died sometime before this census and his widow, Marie Bernard, was living in Port Royal with one of her sons. Marie Bernard died on January 11, 1719 a
2 CONC s document by Winston DeVille's "Acadian Church Records" vol 4 p.124-125. He also makes reference in his writings that "On April1, 1718, Marie Bernard, widow of Rene Landry, baptised a baby because of the danger in transporting th
2 CONC e infant in cold weather."
2 CONT
2 CONT (continued on the following internal textfile)
2 CONT (continued from the previous internal textfile)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Landry clan grew over the years and spread in various parts of Acadia. Before the Great Derangement, the Landry family was established at Port Royal, Grand Pre', Beaubassin, Chipoudie, Petcoudiac, Port-Toulouse, Royal Island, 
2 CONC Port-Lajoye, and Isle St. John. Of particular  interest to the descendants of Rene Landry le Jeune, one Firmin Landry is of importance because he was the connecting factor between Acadia and Louisana. However, confusion and errors
2 CONC  also exists around this Firmin Landry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bona Arcenault has made claim that Firmin was "probably" the son of Pierre Landry and Marguerite Forest in his publication "Histoire et Genealogie des Acadians" vol 6, page 2522 "Firmin Landry, born 1728 probably the son of Pierre
2 CONC  and Marguerite Forest..." and in Vol 4 p.1406 "without doubt the son of Pierre and Marguerite....."
2 CONT
2 CONT M.E."Mitch" Conover believes that Firmin was the son of Charles Landry and Marie Leblanc. He bases his believe on succession records in the St. Martinville Book of Original Acts whereby certain birth, baptismal and marriage certif
2 CONC icates appear. John T. Harrell also believes that Firmin was the son of Charles and Marie. He bases his assumption on the close relationship of Marie LeBlance, widow of Charles Landry listed in the 1763 census of Oxford Maryland, 
2 CONC placing Firmin Landry and Amant Landry among the known children of Charles Landry and Marie Leblanc. Firmin is later listed in the Attakapas census of 1777 as living next door to Amant Landry. Amant is later proven to be the son o
2 CONC f Charles and Marie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many researchers believe that he was the son of Alexandre Landry and Ann Flan. This believe is based on Father Hebert's listings in his southwest Louisiana Records as follows: "Landry Firmin (Alexandre & ------, of Acadie) m. Theo
2 CONC tiste Thiboudau....." (SM ch: v4, #231)(Hebert vol 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter written to Dr. Donald Landry, Metairie, Louisiana, in response to a letter written about questions concerning Firmin Landry, Professor Stephen A. White states: "Regarding Firmin Landry, I am aware of the hypothesis tha
2 CONC t has recently been advanced to the effect that Firmin who married Francois Thibodeau and Theotiste Thibodeau was a son of Charles Landry and Marie Leblanc. The researchers who have reached this conclusion seem to have some reason
2 CONC  for rejecting what appears to be recorded in Firmin's burial record at St.Martinville [SM ch: v4 #231] [4Feb. 1801 ag age 67 yrs] which according to Father Donald Hebert [Southwest Louisiana Records, vol 1, p.344] shows that Theo
2 CONC tiste Thibodeau's husband was the son of Alexandre Landry. I do not know what their reason might be for this, and consequently I am not changed in my belief that Firmin Landry was the son of Alexandre Landry and Marguerite Blancha
2 CONC rd. Insofar as I know, Marguerite Blanchard's husband was the only Alexandre Landry who was fathering children in Acadia around the time of Firmin Landry's birth. Please note that Alexandre Landry who married Anne Flan could not h
2 CONC ave been Firmin's father because he and Anne were only married in 1732."
2 CONT
2 CONT To furthur support Professor White's opinion, the following is loosely translated from a Spanish document found in St. Martinville "1801-entry 231-Firmin Landry--Day four of February of the year eighteen hundred and one, I Don Mig
2 CONC uel Berndo Barriere priest of this church of St. Martin of the juristiction of Atakapas, buried in the 5th camp of the referred church, the body of Firmin Landry adult of age sixty and days and six years, legitimate son of Alexand
2 CONC re Landry and of ----------, natural of the Acadia, which was married with Theotiste Thibodau, did not receive the sacrements (the last rites), for having died suddenly, did not give testament an ( ): For the record was signed by 
2 CONC Migel Bernado Barriere, curator.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based upon this information, the assumption must be made that Firmin was indeed the son of an Alexandre Landry. The question now is who are the parents of this Alexandre? Professor White states that Alexandre was the son of Germai
2 CONC n Landry in this letter to B.R.Malkmus of Decatur, Alabama "Once we eliminate from consideration the Alexandre Landry who married Anne Flan, the only other Alexandre known who might have been Firmin's father was Germain's son. As 
2 CONC it happens that Alexandre Landry's other children, including Genevieve, were all in Oxford, Maryland, in close proximity to Firmin. There is certainly a certain amount of plausability to the identification I have proposed."
2 CONT
2 CONT Germain Landry was the son of Rene Landry and Marie Bernard. He was born in 1674 at Port Royal, Acadia. He married in 1694 Marie Melanson, the daughter of Pierre Melanson and Marie Mius d'Entremont.. Marie was born in 1673 at Gran
2 CONC d Pre'. The family was established at St. Famillie Parish, Pisiguit. The census of 1717 shows them having 6 boys and 6 girls; Alexandre, Abraham, Germain, Paul, Pierre, Marie, Marguerite, a missing boy and a missing girl. Germain 
2 CONC died after the census of 1714.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now that some sort of ancestory has been established from Rene Landry to Firmin Landry, it is possible to move on with Firmin's history. He was born ca. 1728 in Pisiguit, married Francoise "Elizabeth" Thibodeau in 1752 at Pisiguit
2 CONC . They had issue of four children: Joseph, born 1753; Saturin, born 1755; Helene, born 1757; and Marie Madeleine, born 1759. He is remarried by 1770 to Theotiste "Sally" Thibodeaux, daughter of Charles Thibodeaux and Brigette Brea
2 CONC ux. They have issue of ten children: Francoise (in memory of his first wife), born 1770; Hubert, 1773; Helene, 1774; Rosalie, 1776; Valentin, 1778; Marie, 1780; Alexandre, 1782; Agnes, 1784; Marguerite, 1789; and Madeleine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Firmin was deported to Maryland in 1755 with his first wife Francoise Thibodeau and three children. Proof of that is found in the FRENCH PRESENCE IN MARYLAND, p.193 "Acadians in the 1763 census--Oxford". "Landry Firmin, Elizabeth 
2 CONC his wife, Joseph and Saturin, Eleine and Magdeleine". In this record Francoise is called Elizabeth, but this is the only mention of her under that given name, and may simply be an error.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Expulsion began sometime in November, 1755, when the village of Grand Pre, consisting to two parishes St. Charles at Riviere-Aux-Canards and St. Joseph at Grand Pre and little houses spread out for a mile and a half was burned
2 CONC  to the ground. Of the 1600 people captured in that region, 178 were placed aboard the vessel Elizabeth and 242 aboard the Leopard, and were shipped to Maryland. These two vessels were joined by the Dolphin with 227 aboard and the
2 CONC  Ranger with 323 aboard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not knowing what to do with them, a decision was made by the authorities, to send two of the ships to the Eastern shore and two were kept on the western side on Chesapeke Bay. After this, the Acadians were to be scattered througho
2 CONC ut the colony, some to Oxford, which included Firmin and his family. A Maryland paper wrote at the time "Refugees, especially French Catholics without funds, found chilly reception here" and "Gruging charity cares for Acadian 'pes
2 CONC ts'. Because of the fierce winter storms, the Dolphin and the Ranger had to sail into the Boston Harbor and seek shelter for a number of days and finally reached Maryland on November 30, 1755, just as waives of Francophobia (Engli
2 CONC sh dislike for the French) and anti-Catholicism in the colonies had crested. Because of the dreadful overcrowding and the delay due to the storms, the ships supplies were depleted. The captain of the ships on December 8, 1755, unc
2 CONC eremoniously dumped 181 Acadians, including Firmin and his family, on the warf, gruffly telling them he had no more provisions to give them, and as far as he was concerned, they could starve. He then sailed away leaving them among
2 CONC st a population who hated everything they stood for. Rumors spread throughout the English colony of Oxford that the Catholic Acadians were plotting to stir up insurrection amongst the negro slaves, capture the colony and turn it o
2 CONC ver to the Catholic France. In the midst of all this, stood the Acadians. They were there through no fault of their own. Their only "crime" had been the refusal to take the oath of allegience to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Acadians were quartered virually as prisoners, not to leave town without a written permit of the selectmen under penalty of five days in prison or ten lashes, they depended on the cold hand of the public for food, clothes, and
2 CONC  lodging and were given some provisions. Governors of the colonies received the Acadians with varying degrees of hostility. While at Oxford, the Acadians petitioned the Maryland legislature for relief and on August 1756, at the Ta
2 CONC lbot County Court, a petition was presented "setting forth that Joseph Bujiale, Fermee and Charles Landree, French neutrals, have each three small children, the oldest of which is not over five years of age...that they cannot supp
2 CONC ort their families, and can hardly get bread for themselves". The justices of the court allowed each 500 pounds of tobacco, then used as money in Maryland.
2 CONT
2 CONT While in Maryland, the Acadians fell victim of malnutrition and disease. Between 1755 and 1763, the Acadian population in Maryland declined from 913 to 667! By the mid 1760's, the Acadians were anxious to leave Maryland and the na
2 CONC tive Marylanders were equally anxious to see them leave. In the late spring of 1765, one Landry (probably Joseph) addressed a petition to Governor Sharpe, on behalf of the Acadians in Maryland and Pennsylvania to be allowed to set
2 CONC tle in Novia Scotia, either on the Bay of Gaspe or Chaleur. Frustrated in their attempts to resettle in Acadia, they requested settlement in Louisiana. Therefore, joined by the surviving Acadian relatives and friends in Pennsylvan
2 CONC ia, they pooled their resources, chartered ships, and made preparations to sail to Louisiana. However, it wasn't until March 1767 that departure actually took place.
2 CONT
2 CONT The exact date that Firmin  left Maryland for Louisiana is unknown. Records show him still in Maryland on January 14, 1766, when he witnessed a wedding.  However, Firmin's first wife, Francoise, must have died prior to his leaving
2 CONC  Maryland. Theotiste "Sally' Thibadeau, Firmin's second wife,  must have preceeded Firmin to Louisiana  because she arrived before the1766 census of the Acadian Coast in Louisiana where she is listed  as the widow Godin along with
2 CONC  her 5 year old daughter Barbe. She had been married ca. 1757 to Bonaventure Godin. The date of Firmin's marriage to Thotiste is uncertain but it was prior to 1770 because he had a daughter named Francoise, "in memory of his first
2 CONC  wife", born October 22, 1770 and  the mother is listed as Theotiste Thibodeau (SMCH: V1 p.20). Giving the announcement of Banns at the church, the time of search to make sure of their kindred, plus a nine month pregnancy, would i
2 CONC ndicate that they were married probably  in mid-to-late 1769. The earliest listing found of Firmin Landry in Louisiana is in the Brand Book at St. Martinville dated 1769, "Records of Attakapas District, Louisiana 1739-1811". Bona 
2 CONC Arcenault shows on page 1063 of "Attakapas Et Opelousas" Firman appearing in the 1769 Opelousas Brand Book. Assuming that Firmin had to have been in Louisiana early enough to have developed a need to register his brand, one could 
2 CONC say he arrived as early as late 1766 or 1767. On June 20, 1771, he is shown to receive a land grant in the Attakapas District [Land Records of the Attakapas District by Glen R. Conrad] which would have been on the upper Bayou Verm
2 CONC illion, near Grand Pointe. Later he obtained a smaller tract on the Teche at Fausse Pointe near present Loreauville.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are lists that show when many Acadians from Maryland arrived in New Orleans, but Firmin Landry is not listed on any of them. "I beleive that Firmin Landry must have brought his family to Louisiana by ship, as there was no ot
2 CONC her practical method of making the trip at that time", Professor Stephan A. White.
2 CONT
2 CONT Firmin's son Saturin apparently remained single, but his other son Joseph settled on Bayou Vermillion and became the progenitor of what is today's Landry clan in that area.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://landrystuff.com/jeanclaude.htm> (copy filed L-34-31).
2 CONT
2 CONT JEAN-CLAUDE LANDRY --- MYTH OR PROGENITOR?
2 CONT
2 CONT For many of us researching the Landry family, "Jean-Claude Landry" is the focal point of much debate!  He represents our connection between the Old World and the New World.  Wishful thinking has many people believing accounts that
2 CONC  he is that connection while others insist on verification before accepting that claim.  The uncertainty is increased due to the lost of some Acadian church records kept back in the 1600's which were destroyed during a fire in the
2 CONC  early 1700's.  Mention has been given to this topic in one of our other sections, but we feel that the subject is important enough to merit its' own section!
2 CONT
2 CONT A recent newspaper article published in certain areas of Louisiana and Canada made reference to the fact that Jean-Claude Landry was indeed the progenitor of this particular line of Landrys.  Dr. Don Landry of Metaire, Louisiana, 
2 CONC Historian for the Landry Family Association, has written the following rebuttal to these articles in the hope of answering the question.....myth or progenitor?
2 CONT
2 CONT REBUTTAL OF THE JEAN-CLAUDE LANDRY MYTH
2 CONT AND THE SUPPOSED ORIGIN OF LANDRY FAMILY
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime during February and March, 1998, a two part series on the Landry Family appeared in the Lafayette, Louisiana "Daily Advertiser" and again on Sunday March 16th and Sunday March 23rd the same, or similar article appeared in
2 CONC  Damon Veach's column, "Louisiana Ancestors" which is a more widely spread genealogy column, and is published in the editions of the New Orleans TimePicayune, Baton Rouge Morning Advocate, the Lafayette and possibly the Lake Charl
2 CONC es,Alexandria and Shreveport newspapers. According to the articles, the information on the genealogy and origin of the Landry Family of Acadia was received from a Paul Surrette, historian and genealogist from Moncton, New Brunswic
2 CONC k; Brian Comeaux, of the committee for the Congres Mondial Acadiennes-Louisiana, 1999 and Ray Landry, a member of the Landry Family Association. Unfortunately the articles appear to be merely a paraphrasing of Father Léopold Lanct
2 CONC ôt, o.m.i.'s account of the "The Landrys in Acadia" in tomes I et II, Éditions du Libre-Échange ISBN 2-89412-003-6 and L'Acadie des origines Léopold Lanctôt, o.m.i. Éditions du Fleuve, Montréal, 1988, which unfortunately are fille
2 CONC d with errors, presented as documented facts.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the past 8 to10 years, since I have been doing genealogical research into the Landry family, I have run across researchers and documents written by researchers that hold to the theory that the parents of René Landry, le Jeune 
2 CONC married to Marie Bernard was Jean-Claude Landry and Marie Salé.
2 CONT
2 CONT On more that one occasions noted genealogists, including Stephen A. White, genealogist and historian at the University of Moncton's Centre d'Etudes Acadiennes in Moncton New Brunswick and Father Clarence J. d'Entremont, Middle Wes
2 CONC t Pubnico - Yarmouth Co., Nova Scotia, have more than adequately rebutted this theory. They and the others theorize that this error was caused by the early censuses of Acadia, which enumerated Marie Salé as the "widow of Jean Clau
2 CONC de" in the cenuses of 1671 and 1678, and then in the 1686 census, no mention was made of her deceased husband Jehan Claude, Marie Salé was enumerated as 86 years old and living between René Landry, le Jeunne and René Landry's olde
2 CONC st son Antoine Landry. This caused noted genealogist, Archange Godbout, to leap to the conclusion, that since Marie Salé was living in close proximity to René Landry, le Jeune, then she was the mother of René Landry, le Jeune. And
2 CONC  still a greater leap was made to conclude that if Marie Sale was the widow of Jean Claude, then Jean Claude was the father of René Landry, le jeune and Jean Claude, in fact was actually Jean-Claude Landry, father of René Landry, 
2 CONC le jeunne.  I am sure that most researchers understand the importance of having all of the information documented, and I am sure that they assume that, since the information they received was from credible sources, that it was doc
2 CONC umented and factual genealogical and historical data. What I am afraid of is that since this error was so widely published throughout Louisiana, especially in south Louisiana, where the majority of the Louisiana Acadian population
2 CONC  resides, these errors will be perpetuated for a long time to come. And just as the errors of Fathers Archange Godbout, Leopold Lanctot and Adrien Bergeron, Bona Arsenault and countless others, have been believed to be documented 
2 CONC facts, these errors will also be believed to be the documented facts, just because they were printed in a reputable column.
2 CONT
2 CONT Probably prompted by the above census entry, the writings of Adrien Bergeron in his "Le Grand Arrangement des Acadiens au Quebec" vol IV p.283, says that Marie Salé is married to Jean-Claude Landry and had two sons René Landry, th
2 CONC e elder and René Landry, the younger. And in a more elaborate extension of this error, Leopold Lanctot, o.m.i., in his publication "Familles Acadiennes", makes the following suggestions as to the beginnings of the Landrys in the N
2 CONC ew World, when he states on page 7: "It all began in the year 1640 or 1641 when a group of 10 from the Landry family came to Port Royal, Acadia from France. The Landry family was originally from La Ventrouze, near Mortagne-au-Perc
2 CONC he. Department of Orne, France. They were encouraged to come to Acadia by Marguerite Landry, daughter of Jean-Claude Landry and Marguerite's husband Robert Martin, who had been in Acadia for several years. The group of 10 consiste
2 CONC d of Jean-Claude Landry and his second wife, Marie Salée (40 years) with their son René Landry, dit le jeune (6 ans) and three children of Jean-Claude Landry from his first marriage: twins, René Landry dit l' aisne (22 years) and 
2 CONC Antoinette Landry (22 years), Perrine Landry (29 years) with her husband Jacques Joffriau. Also in the group were three of Marie Salée's children from her first marriage to Martin Aucoin. These children were: Michelle Aucoin (22 y
2 CONC ears), Francois Aucoin (18 years) and Jeanne Aucoin (8 years). The group probably settled near the Saint-John River in the Cape Sable area. They later moved to Port Royal. Please note that there were two named René in this group, 
2 CONC René Landry, the elder (son of Jean-Claude Landry from his first marriage) and René Landry, the younger (son of Jean-Claude Landry and Marie Salé). René, the elder married Perrine Bourg, widow of Simon Pelletret, in 1645. Perrine 
2 CONC had 2 children from her first marriage: Henriette Pelletret (4 years)and Jeanne Pelletret (2 years). "On page 9 Leopold Lanctot, in discussing the 1686 census, mentions" "Marie Salé age 61 ans, widow of Jean Claude" but he adds th
2 CONC e surname LANDRY in parentheses "(Landry)". He like all the others before him, suggests, on page 11, that Ren?? Landry, l'aine and Rene Landry, le jeune are half brothers, and again adds, in parentheses, "(le jeune, demi-frere de 
2 CONC René Landry, l'ainse)" behind René Landry, the younger's name.  And again adding, in parentheses "(mere de René Landry, le jeune)" behind Marie Sale's name.  Leopold Lanctot suggests, on page 15, in a chapter on René Landry, dit l
2 CONC e jeune, and Marie Bernard, again suggests that René Landry, le jeune is the son of "Jean-Claude Landry and Marie Salé" but notice that the hyphen between Jean and Claude has been added and the surname Landry is not placed in pare
2 CONC ntheses. The placing of the earlier assumptions such as the surname Landry and Marie Salé being the mother of René in parentheses, which were later presented with out the parentheses, and the addition of a hyphen between Jean Clau
2 CONC de's name, show the gradual progression of these errors into what is now believed by many to be documented facts. These errors are also found on pp 623-624 of "Histoire et Genealogie des Acadians" by Bona Arsenault, where Arsenaul
2 CONC t states:- "Jean-Claude Landry bn. 1593 and Marie Salé bn. 1600 daughter of Jean Denys Salé and Francoise Arnaud, were married in Department of Orne in France, in 1633. This was the second marriage for both. One child was born fro
2 CONC m this marriage, Rene, born 1643. Jean Claude died in 1671 in Mortagne-Au-Perche, France. The name of Marie Salé, age 86, appears in the 1686 Census of Acadia, living with her son René".
2 CONT
2 CONT (continued on the following internal textfile)
2 CONT (continued from the preceding internal textfile)
2 CONT
2 CONT However, throughout this time, Professor Stephen A. White, historian and genealogist with the Centre d'etudes Acadiennes at the University of Moncton in Moncton, New Brunswick, and his fellow historian, genealogist and author, Fat
2 CONC her Clarence d'Entremont from Middle West Pubnico, Yarmouth Co., Nova Scotia have steadfastedly held that the theory that René Landry's parents were Jean-Claude Landry and Marie Salé, was false. As Father Clarence d'Entremont stat
2 CONC es in a letter of November 23rd: "NOWHERE in any census or other documents is to be found an Acadian by the name of Jean-Claude Landry. So, who was the father and mother of René Landry? I do not know, nor does anybody know.... Thu
2 CONC s the descendants of René Landry, in my humble opinion, cannot go further up in their Landry genealogy, as we do not know who the parents of René Landry le Jeunne were, nor where in France he was born".
2 CONT
2 CONT "Jean-Claude Landry is effectively fictitious. There is no record showing that such a person ever existed. The husband of Marle Sallé is simply called Jean (or Jehan) Claude in the censuses of 1671 and 1678. According to archives,
2 CONC  Marie Salé was married to Jean Claude; if she is to be called the mother of René Landry, necessarily we have to give her husband a name of Jean Claude LANDRY. But, I repeat, the name Jean Claude Landry is not to be found anywhere
2 CONC  in the history of Acadia at the time; plus that the husband of Marie Salé was Jean Claude, PERIOD. He was a Micmac Indian. The Indians with the name Claude used to be quite numerous in Nova Scotia, The name became Glaude; in my y
2 CONC oung days I knew a number of them, who would write their name Glode (In French "au" is pronounced "o"). . His name occurs twice in the Port Royal Church Registers, ALWAYS as Jehan Clause, NEVER given as family name "Landry". As a 
2 CONC matter of fact, if Clause had not been his family name, it would mean that the register gives him his first and SECOND name. Moreover, the registers of Port Royal ALWAYS give the WHOLE name of persons; but the fact is that Jehan C
2 CONC lause has his name given thus, NEVER with another name added to those two.  If the family name had been omitted in the registers, it would be the only time that such a thing occured in any register. Thus CLAUDE was the family name
2 CONC ."
2 CONT
2 CONT An enthusiastic and overly imaginative researcher added Landry to this individual's name in an effort to explain why Marie Sallé resided between the younger René Landry and his son Antoine Landry in 1686.  He supposed that this wa
2 CONC s the same Marie Sallé who married Martin Aucoin at La Rochelle in 1632, which does seem quite possible, and through that marriage she was related to Michelle Aucoin, with whose daughter she resided in 1671 and 1678, which is also
2 CONC  possible. But the only way this researcher could connect Marie Sallé with the younger Rene Landry was in guessing that her Jean Claude was really a Landry and further that he must have been the younger René's father. This is mere
2 CONC ly wishful thinking. The other difficulty with the younger René Landry concerns his absence from the 1671 census. Some researchers have thought that this signified that he had not yet immigrated to Acadia by that time, but it can 
2 CONC be shown that the 1671 census is incomplete, and thus the omission of anyone from it does not prove that that person only arrived in Acadia after that date.  Indeed the records of the LeBorgne family in series E of the Archives de
2 CONC s colonies (dossier E 277) mention transactions involving the younger René Landry's wife's brother-in-law, Guyon Chaisson, between 1668 and 1674, so we know for certain that the Chiassons were at "Mouchecoudabouet" during those ye
2 CONC ars. It is my opinion quite likely that the younger René Landry and his family lived in close proximity with the Chiassons in  "Mouchecoudabouet", around that time. As Bona Arsenault has indicated, for the elder René Landry to hav
2 CONC e been called "l'Aine" in the 1671 census presupposes that another René Landry must have lived somewhere in Acadia at the same time.  As Father Archange Godbout mentions in his Dictionaire des Acadiens, the younger René "came from
2 CONC  France with his wife". This quotation is lifted from several of the depositions of the Acadians at Belle-Ile-en-Mer.  As Father Godbout pointed out in the Memoires de la Societé généalogie canadienne- française (vol. V. p. 5), th
2 CONC is expression on those depositions means simply that both the husband and wife were born in France, but does not necessarily mean that they came to Acadia together, much less already married to one another. So all we can say is th
2 CONC at  René Landry was born in France about 1634. We do not know whether he came to Acadia alone or with other relatives.  As I have explained above, however, we do know that he was not nearly related to any of the other Landrys in A
2 CONC cadia."
2 CONT
2 CONT It is further stated by both Father d'Entremont and Professor White that it is very doubtful that two different census takers at two different times would have omitted the last name Landry when referring to the deceased husband of
2 CONC  Marie Salé and if the family name had been omitted in the church and other public registers, it would be the only time that such a thing occured in any register. Therefore they both conclude that the addition of the surname Landr
2 CONC y to Jean Claude is an error.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter witten in early 1998, Stephen A. White, Genealogist, Centre d'etudes Acadiennes writes: "What can I tell you about "Jean-Claude Landry" that I have not already said? Not much, I can assure you. No one has brought forwa
2 CONC rd any new information to show that two different census takers, at two separate times, both forgot to put the name Landry in the entries pertaining to the widow Marie Sale.  No one has discovered a cache of passenger lists for an
2 CONC y of the vessels mentioned by Father Lanctot to show, as he maintains, that "Jean-Claude Landry" arrived in Acadia on a certain date, at the head of a group of a specific number of family members,  In these circumstances, serious 
2 CONC researchers must agree that nothing supports the contention that there ever was a "Jean-Claude Landry" in early Acadia." "No one really knows how the Landrys came to Acadia, how many of them came together, if indeed they did come 
2 CONC in a group, or if and how they were related, beyond the simple fact that Rene Landry l'aine and Antoinette Landry were brother and sister. We certainly have no documentation to show that Rene and Antoinette were twins!  Even thoug
2 CONC h Rene and Antoinette are said to have both been fifty-three years old in the 1671 census, no experienced genealogist would read that as meaning that they necessarily born at the same time, because such records are rarely strictly
2 CONC  accurate. After all, fifteen years later, in 1686. Antoinette is said to have been eighty!  And by 1693 she had regressed to seventy-six.  Such records are merely guides; they do not admit strict interpretation.  To go further, w
2 CONC ithout additional proofs, is to indulge in the creation of romantic fiction". "It is most regrettable that Father Lanctot chose to present his account of the history of our early Acadian families as though all of his points were b
2 CONC ased on documented facts. And it is reprehensible that a publisher saw fit to distribute such an admixture of truth and fantasy, as though it were serious history.  The result is particularly invidious insofar as those people who 
2 CONC have little or no means to consult the original records are concerned. They are left to suppose that Lanctot's work is a reliable piece of research, where as it is in fact treacherously misleading, because there are some extremely
2 CONC  good information mixed in with the bad."
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen A. White writes:  "Regarding the origin and parents of René Landry, le Jeunne there is probably no other Acadian family about whose background there has been so much speculation and wishful thinking. The result is that wha
2 CONC t we actually know about the Landry families who immigrated from France to Acadia, has come to be regrettably enshrouded in a dense fog of error and confusion."
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Donald Landry
2 CONT Metaire, Louisiana
2 CONT Don2717@aol.com
