2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/160042733/maximilian_iii-of_austria
2 CONT Maximilian III, Archduke of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to:navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT 	This article does not cite any references or sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (December 2009)
2 CONT
2 CONT Maximilian III
2 CONT
2 CONT Archduke of Further Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 	1612-1618
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor 	Charles II
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor 	Ferdinand III
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Maria of Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	12 October 1558
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	2 Novembar 1618
2 CONT
2 CONT Maximilian III, also known as Maximilian der Deutschmeister (12 October 1558 – 2 November 1618) was the Archduke of Further Austria from 1612 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Wiener Neustadt, Maximilian was the fourth son of the emperor Maximilian II and Maria of Spain. He was a grandson of Anna of Bohemia and Hungary, daughter and heiress of Ladislaus II of Bohemia and Hungary, who himself was
2 CONC  the eldest son of Casimir IV of Poland. He descended from the ancient Piast kings of Poland, and from Jogaila and his forefathers, Grand Dukes of Lithuania. The late Sigismund I the Old, himself a younger son, was Maximilian's gr
2 CONC eat-great-uncle, and Maximilian descended from Sigismund's eldest brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1585 Maximilian became the Grand Master of the Teutonic Order, and he was known by the epithet 'der Deutschmeister' for much of his later life.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1587 Maximilian stood as a candidate for the throne of Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, following the death of the previous king, Stefan Batory. A portion of the Polish nobility elected Maximilian king, but, as a result of the r
2 CONC ather chaotic nature of the election process, another candidate, Sigismund III Vasa, prince of Sweden, grandson of Sigismund I the Old, was also elected. Maximilian attempted to resolve the dispute by bringing a military force to 
2 CONC Poland – thereby starting the war of the Polish Succession. His cause had considerable support in Poland, but fewer Poles flocked to his army than to that of his rival. After a failed attempt to storm Kraków in late 1587, he was d
2 CONC efeated in January 1588, at the Battle of Byczyna by the supporters of Sigismund III (who had since been formally crowned), under the command of Polish hetman Jan Zamojski. Maximilian was taken captive at the battle and was only r
2 CONC eleased after the intervention of Pope Sixtus V. In 1589, he formally renounced his claim to the Polish crown. The inactivity of his brother, the emperor Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor in this matter contributed to Rudolf's poor re
2 CONC putation.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1593 to 1595 Maximilian as served regent for his young cousin, Ferdinand, Archduke of Inner Austria. In 1595 he succeeded their uncle Ferdinand II, Archduke of Further Austria in his territories, including Tirol, where he pro
2 CONC ved to be a solid proponent of the Counter-Reformation. He also worked to depose Melchior Khlesl, and to ensure that Archduke Ferdinand of Inner Austria, his former charge, succeeded as Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, Maximilian is perhaps best remembered for his baroque archducal hat, exhibited in the treasury of the monastery of Klosterneuburg and was used for ceremonial purposes as late as 1835.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at Vienna in 1618, and is buried in St. Jakobskirche, Innsbruck.
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 22 February 2010 at 16:08
