1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Anna of Tyrol
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna, Archduchess of Austria, was the daughter of Ferdinand, Archduke of Further Austria and Count of Tyrol, and Anna Caterina Gonzaga, daughter of Duke William of Mantua. She was born 4 October 1585 in Innsbruck, and married her 
2 CONC first cousin, Matthias, King of Hungary and Bohemia, on 4 December 1611. With his election as Emperor in 1612, she became Empress of the Holy Roman Empire. They had no surviving children. Anna of Austria died 14 December 1618 in V
2 CONC ienna, predeceasing her husband by less than four months.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_of_Tyrol
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna of Tyrol
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to:navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna of Tyrol
2 CONT
2 CONT Holy Roman Empress; Queen consort of the Romans, Hungary, and Bohemia; Archduchess consort of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure 	1608–1618
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Matthias, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT House 	House of Habsburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Ferdinand II, Archduke of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Anne Juliana Gonzaga
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	4 October 1585
2 CONT
2 CONT Innsbruck, Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	14 December 1618
2 CONT
2 CONT Vienna, Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Imperial Crypt, Vienna
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna of Tyrol (Innsbruck, 4 October 1585 – 14 Dec 1618, Vienna) was the wife of Matthias, Holy Roman Emperor. By this marriage she was Holy Roman Empress, German Queen, Queen of Bohemia and Queen of Hungary[1]. Her parents were Fe
2 CONC rdinand II, Archduke of Austria and Anne Juliana Gonzaga.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 4 December 1611 in Vienna, Matthias and Anna were married. Matthias was already over fifty at the time. Anna was a first cousin of Matthias; his father, Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor was an elder brother of Anna's father, F
2 CONC erdinand II of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthias and Anna had no surviving children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1617 Anna with her husband founded the Capuchin church, which is were they wanted to be buried so Matthias had the Imperial Crypt built. Anna died a year later and was buried there. Three months later, Mattias died and was buri
2 CONC ed with Anna in the Imperial Crypt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of the Holy Roman Emperors and Emperesses have since been buried at the Imperial Crypt.
2 CONT
