1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A9sar_of_Bourbon,_Duke_of_Vend%C3%B4me
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00003655&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT César de Bourbon, Duc de Vendôme (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #114501, b. 3 June 1594, d. 22 October 1665
2 CONT
2 CONT César de Bourbon, Duc de Vendôme was born illegitimately on 3 June 1594 at Château de Coucy, Picardie, France. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Henri IV, Roi de France and Gabrielle d'Estrées, Duchesse de Beaufort. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Françoise de Lorraine-Mercouer, daughter of Philip Emanuel de Lorraine, Duc de Lorraine-Mercouer and Marie de Luxembourg-Penthièvre, on 7 July 1609. (1) He died on 22 October 1665 at age 71 at Paris, France.1
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1595 his birth was legitimised. (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created Duc de Vendôme in 1598. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of César de Bourbon, Duc de Vendôme and Françoise de Lorraine-Mercouer
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Louis de Vendôme, Duc de Vendôme et de Mercoeur+ b. Dec 1612, d. 6 Aug 1669 (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Elizabeth de Vendôme+ b. Aug 1614, d. 19 May 1664 (3) 
2 CONT
2 CONT -3. François de Vendôme, Duc de Beaufort b. 5 Jan 1616, d. 25 Jun 1669 (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás:
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT César of Bourbon, Duke of Vendôme
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT César of Bourbon-Vendôme (César I, Duke of Vendôme, Beaufort and Etampes) (Château de Coucy, 3 June 1594 – Paris, 22 October 1665) was the son of Henry IV of France and his mistress Gabrielle d'Estrées.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was legitimized in 1595, and was created the first Duke of Vendôme by his father in 1598. The next year, he became Duke of Beaufort and Duke of Étampes upon the death of his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 July 1608, at the Château de Fontainebleau, he married Françoise de Lorraine, Duchesse de Mercoeur et de Penthièvre (d. 1669), the wealthy heiress of Philippe Emmanuel, Duke of Mercoeur. The couple had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis II, duc de Vendôme (1612–1669)
2 CONT
2 CONT François, duc de Beaufort (1616–1669)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth, Mlle de Vendôme (Paris, August 1614 – Paris, 19 May 1664), married in Paris on 11 July 1643 Charles Amédée, Duc de Nemours.
2 CONT
2 CONT César spent much of his life intriguing against Marie de' Medici and then his half-brother Louis XIII of France. Implicated in the conspiracy of Chalais against Cardinal Richelieu, he and his brother Alexandre, the Chevalier de Ve
2 CONC ndôme, were imprisoned in the Château de Vincennes in 1626. He was released in 1630 and exiled to Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1632, he returned to France and took part in the cabale des Importants against Cardinal Mazarin with his second son François. Exiled again, this time to England, he did not return until 1642. The marriage of his son Louis to La
2 CONC ura Mancini brought about his reconciliation with Mazarin, and he supported Anne of Austria throughout the Fronde. In 1651, he was appointed Admiral of France, and Grand Master of Navigation in 1655.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the head and founder of the House of Bourbon-Vendôme.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A9sar_de_Bourbon,_duc_de_Vend%C3%B4me
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A9sar,_Duke_of_Vend%C3%B4me
2 CONT
2 CONT César, Duke of Vendôme
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT César
2 CONT
2 CONT Légitimé de France
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Vendôme
2 CONT
2 CONT César by Moncornet
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Françoise de Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Detail
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis, Duke of Vendôme
2 CONT
2 CONT François, Duke of Beaufort
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth, Duchess of Nemours
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name
2 CONT
2 CONT César de Bourbon, Legitimé de France
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Henry IV of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Gabrielle d'Estrées
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	3 June 1594(1594-06-03)
2 CONT
2 CONT Château de Coucy, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	11 October 1665 (aged 71)
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT César de Bourbon, Légitimé de France (3 June 1594 – 22 October 1665), Duke of Vendôme, was the son of Henry IV of France and his mistress Gabrielle d'Estrées. Sometimes simply known as César de Vendôme. Through his daughter, Élisa
2 CONC beth de Bourbon, César was a great great great grandfather of Louis XV of France, and thus an ancestor of Prince Henri the Count of Paris, Orléanist pretender to the French throne, are among the male line descendants and heirs of 
2 CONC Philippe. He is also an ancestor of Juan Carlos I of Spain, of Albert II, King of the Belgians, of Henri, Grand Duke of Luxembourg, and of Vittorio Emanuele, Prince of Naples, a pretender to the Italian throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Titles
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Born at the Château de Coucy in the Picardy region of France; his parents had started their affair in 1591 and César had been the couples first child. He was legitimised in 1595, and was created the first Duke of Vendôme by his fa
2 CONC ther in 1598. The Duchy of Vendôme had been owned by the Bourbon dynasty since 1393, at that point being a County then being raised by Louis XII of France to a Duchy. In the same year, he was engaged to Françoise de Lorraine, who 
2 CONC at the time she was said to be the richest heiress in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1599, César became Duke of Beaufort and Duke of Étampes upon the death of his mother, who died as a result of a miscarriage in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, King Henry IV, was grief-stricken, especially given the widely-held rumor that Gabrielle had been poisoned. He wore black in mourning, something no previous French monarch had done before. He gave her the funeral of a 
2 CONC Queen; her coffin was transported amidst a procession of princes, princesses, and nobles to the Saint Denis Basilica for a requiem Mass. Known in French history and song as La Belle Gabrielle, she was interred at Abbaye Notre-Dame
2 CONC -la-Royale de Maubuisson, Saint-Ouen-l'Aumône (Val-d'Oise, Île-de-France).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was his father's first son but due to his illegitimacy, was not allowed to inherit the throne; his half brother, the future Louis XIII of France was born in September 1601 much to the joy of the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 July 1608, at the Château de Fontainebleau, he married Françoise de Lorraine (d. 1669), the wealthy heiress of Philippe Emmanuel, Duke of Mercoeur. Françoise was the legal heir to the large and separate duchies of Mercoeur a
2 CONC nd Penthièvre.
2 CONT
2 CONT César spent much of his life intriguing against Marie de' Medici and then his half-brother Louis XIII of France. Implicated in the conspiracy of Chalais against Cardinal Richelieu, he and his brother Alexandre, the Chevalier de Ve
2 CONC ndôme, were imprisoned in the Château de Vincennes in 1626. He was released in 1630 and exiled to Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1632, he returned to France and took part in the cabale des Importants against Cardinal Mazarin with his second son François. Exiled again, this time to England, he did not return until 1642. The marriage of his son Louis to La
2 CONC ura Mancini brought about his reconciliation with Mazarin, and he supported Anne of Austria throughout the Fronde.
2 CONT
2 CONT He reconciled with his half brother in December 1642, a year before his death and the beginning of the reign of his nephew Louis XIV. The reconciliation occurred after the death of Richelieu.
2 CONT
2 CONT César led the royal troops against the rebels in Burgundy, of which he was appointed governor in 1650; appointed Grand Admiral of France in 1651 he helped to capture the insurgent stronghold of Bordeaux in July 1653. Joining Frenc
2 CONC h forces in the ongoing war with Spain, he defeated a Spanish fleet off Barcelona in 1655.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in 1655 César was created the Grand Master of Navigation. He died later that year on 22 October, 1655 in Paris and was buried in the chapel of Saint-Georges at the Château of Vendôme.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the head and founder of the House of Bourbon-Vendôme.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis de Bourbon, Duke of Vendôme (1612–1669) married Laura Mancini and had issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * François de Bourbon, Duke of Beaufort (1616–1669) never married;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Elisabeth de Bourbon, Mademoiselle de Vendôme (Paris, August 1614 – Paris, 19 May 1664), married in Paris on 11 July 1643 Charles Amédée of Savoy, Duke of Nemours.
2 CONT
