1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * This child of Henri IV was never baptized with a first name. Historians have used the first names 'Nicolas Henri' for practical research reasons, but this is historically incorrect. This son was called 'Monsieur 
2 CONC Orléans', and was recorded as 'N., duc d'Orléans' on his tomb. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolas_Henri,_Duke_of_Orl%C3%A9ans en.wikipedia.org... ] ; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas Henri Duke of Orléans A ca. 1610 sketch of Nicolas Henri. House 	House of Bourbon Father 	Henry IV of France Mother 	Marie de' Medici Born 	13 April 1607 Died 	17 November 1611 (aged 4)
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas Henri de France, Duke of Orléans, was the second son and fourth child of Henry IV of France and his Italian queen Marie de' Medici.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas Henri was born at the Palace of Fontainebleau on 16 (or 13) April 1607 and died on 17 November 1611 at the Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye. He was known as Monsieur d'Orléans or the Prince sans nom (prince without name), 
2 CONC as it was customary in the royal house of France to name the children only at the time of their baptism.
2 CONT
2 CONT His youngest brother, Gaston de France, titled at birth as Duke of Anjou as was customary for the third son, was born in 1608. At a very young age, he had been engaged to Marie de Bourbon, Duchess of Montpensier, heiress of the Ho
2 CONC use of Bourbon-Montpensier. Marie was the greatest heiress of the age and was the future mother of la Grande Mademoiselle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas Henri died at the age of four as a result of an epileptic attack. The title of Duke of Orléans reverted back to the crown and was later given to his younger brother Gaston who outlived him for another four decades. His bro
2 CONC ther would also marry Marie in 1626.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried at the Royal Basilica of Saint Denis, outside Paris.
