1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Cecylia Renata Habsburżanka''' – królowa Polski, żona króla polskiego Władysława IV. [http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cecylia_Renata_Habsbur%C5%BCanka Wikipedia PL] [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A4cilia_Renata
2 CONC _von_%C3%96sterreich Wikipedia DE]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecilia Renata of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecilia Renata, Queen of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation 	On September 13, 1637
2 CONT
2 CONT in the St. John's Cathedral,
2 CONT
2 CONT Warsaw, Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of Arms 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents 	Ferdinand II, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Anna of Bavaria
2 CONT
2 CONT Consort 	Władysław IV Waza
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of Birth 	July 16, 1611
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of Birth 	Graz, Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of Death 	March 24, 1644
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of Death 	Vilnius, Lithuania
2 CONT
2 CONT Archduchess Cecilia Renata of Austria (de: Cäcilia Renata von Habsburg, Erzherzogin von Österreich; pl: Cecylia Renata Habsburżanka) (July 16, 1611 – March 24, 1644) was a daughter of Ferdinand II, Holy Roman Emperor, of the House
2 CONC  of Habsburg, and Maria Anna of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Gallery
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 6 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecilia Renata was Queen of Poland and consort to the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth's King Władysław IV Waza. In Poland, she is also known as Cecylia Renata Rakuszanka and Cecylia Renata Austriaczka, both names meaning Cecilia Re
2 CONC nata of Austria, or Cecylia Renata Habsburżanka, meaning Cecilia Renata of Habsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in 1611 in Graz, she married Władysław on 13 September 1637, and the same day was crowned at St. John's Cathedral in Warsaw. Young and energetic, she soon began organizing the royal court to her liking. She was popular, espec
2 CONC ially for her politeness — one nobleman wrote in his memoires that she insisted other women sit with her, even though she was queen. She advocated the Habsburg and pro-Catholic point of view and allied herself with the pro-Habsbur
2 CONC g faction of chancellor Jerzy Ossoliński and pro-Catholic Albrycht Stanisław Radziwiłł. Her political opponents at the court was the faction of Adam Kazanowski, whose influence over king Władysław, his childhood friend, diminished
2 CONC  after her marriage. Kazanowski was allied with Chancellor Piotr Gembicki, who thus became one of her opponents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her influence was strong for the first 2–3 years of marriage, and she had much to say about the royal nominations for important official positions. However after 1638/39 when Władysław realised that Habsburgs were prepared to give
2 CONC  him little assistance, her power waned, as he started to disregard her advice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her two children died in childhood (Zygmund Kazimierz: 1 April 1640 – 9 August 1647; Maria Anna Izabella: 8 January 1642 – 1643). Cecilia Renata died in 1644[1] in Vilnius.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A4cilia_Renata_von_%C3%96sterreich
2 CONT
2 CONT Cäcilia Renata von Österreich
2 CONT
2 CONT aus Wikipedia, der freien Enzyklopädie
2 CONT
2 CONT Wechseln zu: Navigation, Suche
2 CONT
2 CONT Königin Cäcilia Renata
2 CONT
2 CONT Cäcilia Renata von Österreich (* 16. Juli 1611 in Graz; † 24. März 1644 in Krakau) war Königin von Polen und Großfürstin von Litauen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sie war die Tochter von Kaiser Ferdinand II. und dessen Gemahlin Maria Anna von Bayern und wurde in Graz streng katholisch erzogen. Sie war lebenslustig und intelligent und wurde am 13. September 1637 in Krakau mit dem polnischen 
2 CONC König Władysław IV. Wasa verheiratet, dessen Mutter Anna von Österreich zugleich eine Schwester ihres Vaters, also ihre Tante, war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aus der Ehe gingen zwei Kinder hervor, die jedoch beide das Erwachsenenalter nicht erreichten:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prinz Sigmund Kasimir (* 1. April 1640; † 9. August 1647)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prinzessin Maria Anna Isabella (* 8. Januar 1642; † 7. Februar 1642)
2 CONT
2 CONT Der König hatte nicht viel Sympathie für Cäcilia Renate und er machte ihr das Leben am Krakauer Hof nicht leicht. Sie starb im Kindbett bei einer Totgeburt im Jahre 1644 und wurde in der Krypta der Wawel-Kathedrale beigesetzt. Bei
2 CONC  seinem Besuch in Krakau stiftete Kaiser Franz Joseph I. Geld für die Renovierung ihres Sarges.
2 CONT
2 CONT Literatur [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Brigitte Hamann: Die Habsburger, ein biographisches Lexikon. Verlag Carl Ueberreuter, Wien, 1988.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henryk Wisner, Wladyslaw IV. Waza, Wroclaw, 1995
2 CONT
