2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Notre Dame d'Annecy, Annecy
2 CONT
2 CONT Religion 	Roman Catholic
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Amadeus of Savoy (French: Charles Amédée de Savoie) (12 April 1624 – 30 July 1652) was a French military leader and magnate and Duke of Nemours. He was the father of the penultimate Duchess of Savoy and Queen of Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the younger son of Henri of Savoy and Anne de Lorraine. He was a brother of Louis of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in the Army of Flanders in 1645, and in the following year commanded the light cavalry at the siege of Kortrijk. In 1652 he took part in the war of the Fronde, and fought at Bleneau and at the Faubourg St Antoine, where 
2 CONC he was wounded. He was shot and killed the same year in a duel by his brother-in-law, François de Bourbon, Duke of Beaufort.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Amadeus' brother Henri, who had been archbishop of Reims, withdrew from orders in order to succeed him to the title of Duke of Nemours.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married on July 11, 1643 at the Louvre, Élisabeth de Bourbon, Mademoiselle de Vendôme, the daughter of the Duke of Vendôme, the illegitimate son of King Henry IV of France and his mistress, Gabrielle d'Estrées. Her mother was t
2 CONC he wealthy heiress, Françoise de Lorraine (1592–1669), the daughter of Philippe Emmanuel, Duke of Mercoeur and a cousin of the Duke of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles had five children, two daughters and three sons only his daughters survived;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Marie Jeanne Baptiste of Savoy, Mademoiselle de Nemours (1644–1724), who married Charles Emmanuel II, Duke of Savoy, in 1665, and had issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Marie Françoise Élisabeth of Savoy, Mademoiselle d'Aumale (1646–1683), who married king Alphonso VI of Portugal, in 1666 and afterwards Afonso's younger brother, Pedro, Duke of Beja, regent of Portugal, and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT His granddaughter Isabel Luisa of Portugal was heiress-presumptive to the throne of Portugal and Princess of Beira, and his grandson Victor Amadeus II of Savoy became in 1712 king of the island of Sicily and in 1720 instead king o
2 CONC f Sardinia. Descendants of Victor Amadeus II are represented in practically all Catholic royal families of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried at the Notre Dame d'Annecy in Annecy, the capital of the Duchy of Nemours.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 1 August 2010 at 19:34.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Charles Amadeus of Savoy, 6th Duke of Nemours
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Coats of Arms of the Dukes of Savoy after Emmanuel Philibert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Amadeus of Savoy, 6th Duke of Nemours (French: Charles Amédée de Savoie, Duc de Nemours; April 12, 1624 – July 30, 1652) was a French military leader and magnate.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the younger son of Henry of Savoy and a brother of Louis of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in the army of Flanders in 1645, and in the following year commanded the light cavalry at the siege of Kortrijk. In 1652 he took part in the war of the Fronde, and fought at Bleneau and at the Faubourg St Antoine, where 
2 CONC he was wounded. He was killed the same year in a duel by his brother-in-law, François de Vendôme, duc de Beaufort.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Amadeus' brother Henry, who had been archbishop of Reims, withdrew from orders in order to succeed him to the title of Duke of Nemours.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He married on July 11, 1643 at the Louvre, Elisabeth de Bourbon-Vendome, the daughter of the Duc de Vendôme, the illegitimate son of King Henry IV of France and his mistress, Gabrielle d'Estrées. Her mother was the wealthy heiress
2 CONC , Françoise de Lorraine, Duchesse de Mercoeur et de Penthièvre (1592-1669), the daughter of Philippe Emmanuel, Duke of Mercoeur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Charles had two daughters,
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie-Jeanne-Baptiste de Nemours (1644-1724), who married Charles Emmanuel of Savoy, ruler of Piedmont, in 1665,
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie-Françoise de Nemours (1646-1683), who married king Alphonso VI of Portugal, in 1666 and afterwards Afonso's younger brother, Pedro, Duke of Beja, regent of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT His granddaughter Isabel Luisa of Portugal was heiress-presumptive to the throne of Portugal and Princess of Beira, and his grandson Victor Amedee, Duke of Savoy became in 1712 king of the island of Sicily and in 1720 instead king
2 CONC  of Sardinia. Descendants of Victor Amedee are represented in practically all Catholic royal families of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
