2 CONT Maria Anna of Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Anna (18 August 1606 – 13 May 1646), also known as Maria Anna of Austria, Infanta of Spain, Archduchess of Austria, and after marriage, The Holy Roman Empress and Queen of Hungary, was the youngest daughter of King Philip II
2 CONC I of Spain and Margaret of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a younger sister of Anne of Austria, queen consort of Louis XIII of France, and Philip IV of Spain. She was also an older sister of Cardinal-Infante Ferdinand.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 1620s, James I of England envisioned Maria Anna as a possible bride for his son and heir, the future Charles I of England and Scotland. Charles even visited Madrid to meet the young Maria Anna. This romantic endeavor 
2 CONC of the future king of England is set forth in the fictional Captain Alatriste by Arturo Pérez-Reverte published first in English in 2005. However negotiations failed, and Charles eventually married Henrietta Maria of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married instead to her first cousin, the future Ferdinand III, then the titular king of Hungary, on 20 February 1631. They were parents to six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand IV of Hungary (8 September 1633–9 July 1654).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mariana of Austria (23 December 1634–16 May 1696). Married her maternal uncle Philip IV of Spain. They were parents to Charles II of Spain, degenerate last Habsburg monarch of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip August, Archduke of Austria (1637–1639).
2 CONT
2 CONT Maximilian Thomas, Archduke of Austria (1638–1639).
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor (9 June 1640–5 May 1705).
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Austria (1646).
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Thirty Years War, the imperial family moved to Linz, where she died of poisoning during her last pregnancy. The child she was carrying, Maria, was still alive, and was born by cesarean section, but died soon after. Moth
2 CONC er and daughter were buried together in a single coffin.
