1 NOTE NOMINALLY TSAR OF RUSSIA 1610-1613; KING OF POLAND (V, VII, "IV") 1632-1648
1 NOTE He waged a series of victorious wars abroad while striving to reinforce 
2 CONT his power with a distrustful Sejm (parliament) at home. Wladyslaw first 
2 CONT tasted war in the Polish campaign against Russia in 1610-12. While 
2 CONT Sigismund occupied Moscow (1610), he had Wladyslaw elected czar, but he 
2 CONT never assumed the Russian throne. When Sigismund died (1632), Wladyslaw 
2 CONT became king of Poland. The Muscovites at once besieged the frontier, but 
2 CONT he beat them off; in 1634 they paid him a large indemnity and renounced 
2 CONT claims on territory in Poland, and he conceded the title of czar to 
2 CONT Michael Romanov (1596-1645). When Wladyslaw moved against the Turks, they 
2 CONT quickly agreed to share the rule of Moldavia and Walachia with Poland. 
2 CONT After a short war with Sweden, he won an advantageous truce at Stuhmsdorf 
2 CONT in 1635. He failed, however, to regain the Swedish throne once held by his 
2 CONT father. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At home Wladyslaw had difficulty with the Sejm, which viewed him with 
2 CONT suspicion and withheld needed funds, and he could not establish his 
2 CONT authority over the Polish nobles.
