1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois_de_Montmorency,_Duke_of_Luxembourg
2 CONT
2 CONT François de Montmorency, Duke of Luxembourg (born in 1600 and decapitated in Paris on 22 June 1627), second son of Louis de Montmorency, Comte de Bouteville, vice-admiral of France under Henri IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1616, he succeeded his brother, Henri de Montmorency and became Duke of Luxembourg and Governor of Senlis. He served with distinction at the sieges of Saint-Jean-d'Angély, Montauban, Royan and Montpellier.
2 CONT
2 CONT After fighting a duel against the Comte de Pontgibaud, he killed the Marquis de Portes in 1625 and the Comte de Thorigny on 25 March 1626. He injured the Baron de la Frette in 1627 and then fled to Brussels to escape the anger of 
2 CONC Louis XIII. Despite the intercession of many people, including the archduchess of the Netherlands, Louis XIII would not pardon him. Furious, he vowed to fight a duel in broad daylight in central Paris despite the fact that Richeli
2 CONC eu had issued an edict saying that duelling would be punishable by death.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 May 1627 at the Place Royale in Paris, he killed Marquis Bussi d'Amboise in the course of a duel. Their seconds, François de Rosmadec, Comte de Chappelles and François d’Harcourt-Beuvron, Marquis de Beuvron, also fought one 
2 CONC another. While d'Harcourt took refuge in England, Montmorency and Rosmadec, despite their nobility, were decapitated at the Place de Grève on 22 June 1627.
