2 CONT * "<em>[http://www.westminster-abbey.org/our-history/people/henry-frederick,-prince-of-wales Prince of Wales, Henry Frederick, Prince of Wales]</em>", Westminster Abbey
2 CONT ________________________________________________________________
2 CONT Prince Henry Fredrick Stuart seemed destined to be king
2 CONT By Steven Brocklehurst
2 CONT BBC Scotland News
2 CONT Henry Frederick Stuart seemed destined to become one of the most powerful and influential kings in history but his name is now almost unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was the heir to James VI of Scotland, who became the king of England and Ireland when his son was just nine years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young prince was brought up by strict governors to be the perfect example of a Renaissance Protestant monarch and was looked on to secure the Stuart dynasty for generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the course of history was changed by a swim in the Thames when Henry was only 18.
2 CONT Prepared for the role
2 CONT Henry Stuart's life spanned an extraordinary period in British history.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Union of the Crowns, the Gunpowder Plot, colonial expansion and the age of Shakespeare were just some of the more notable episodes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was schooled to display all the outward signs of kingship, with his father even writing a book to advise him on his role - Basilikon Doron.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, when King James died it was Henry's younger brother Charles who took the throne and eventually led England into the turmoil of the Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Turbulent times
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was brought up at Stirling Castle
2 CONT A BBC Scotland documentary - The Best King We Never Had - looks at the life of the man who could have been England's Henry IX (His grandmother Mary, Queen of Scots was the great-niece of Henry VIII).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Stirling Castle in 1594 and, as the first child of James and his Danish wife Anne, Henry was celebrated as guaranteeing the succession of the Stuart bloodline.
2 CONT
2 CONT The slaughter of the Scottish Reformation was just a generation away and James VI had become the first Protestant monarch of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The country was still divided though and many Catholics longed for a return to the old faith.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations with England were also tense.
2 CONT
2 CONT Only seven years before Henry's birth, England's Tudor queen, Elizabeth, executed her cousin Mary, Queen of Scots.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enforced separation
2 CONT King James believed Henry needed to be kept safe and, at just days old, he was taken from his mother and fostered into the care of the Mar family.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were the hereditary keepers of Stirling Castle and historically the guardians of Scottish princes.
2 CONT BBC
2 CONT
2 CONT Janet Milburn 11/11/22
