1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From the Reid-Mellott family tree:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.reidmellott.com/Genealogy/getperson.php?personID=P3281738676&tree=metree
2 CONT
2 CONT *Birth  Feb 1624  Champagne, Dordogne, Aquitaine, France   [2, 3, 5, 8]  
2 CONT **(Ben notes: Roucy, more or less his accepted birth city, is located in Picardy at: 49°22'14.69"N, 3°48'52.58"E)
2 CONT *Gender  Male 
2 CONT *Arrival  New Jersey, USA   [7]  
2 CONT *Died  Feb 1683  Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, USA   [6]  
2 CONT *Person ID  P3281738676  Mellott - Eastman Family Tree 
2 CONT *Last Modified  20 May 2009  
2 CONT *Father  Josias Marles,   
2 CONT **b. 1598, Champagne, Dordogne, Aquitaine, France ,   
2 CONT **d. 20 Feb 1655, Champagne, Dordogne, Aquitaine, France   
2 CONT *Mother  Jeanne Robb,   
2 CONT **b. 1602, Champagne, Dordogne, Aquitaine, France ,   
2 CONT **d. 19 Sep 1660, Champagne, Dordogne, Aquitaine, France   
2 CONT **Married  1618  Champagne, Dordogne, Aquitaine, France   
2 CONT *Family ID  F48  Group Sheet 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family  
2 CONT *Margaret Martin,   
2 CONT **b. 1622, Limbourg, Liege, Belgium ,   
2 CONT **d. 25 Aug 1681, Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, USA   
2 CONT **Married  21 Aug 1644  Walloon Church, Leiden, Zuid-Holland, Netherlands   [3, 5]  
2 CONT
2 CONT Children    
2 CONT *1. Josias Merlette,   
2 CONT **b. 17 Sep 1645, Leiden, Zuid-Holland, Netherlands ,   
2 CONT **d. 1715, Woodbridge, Middlesex, New Jersey, United States  
2 CONT *2. Marie Merlet,   
2 CONT **b. 11 Sep 1646, Leiden, Zuid-Holland, Netherlands ,   
2 CONT **d. 1662, Leiden, Zuid-Holland, Netherlands  
2 CONT *3. Eseschias Paul Merlet,   
2 CONT **b. 16 Jul 1648, Leiden, Zuid-Holland, Netherlands ,   
2 CONT **d. 1662, Leiden, Zuid-Holland, Netherlands  
2 CONT *4. Esechias Merlet,   
2 CONT **b. 26 Jul 1648, Leiden, Zuid-Holland, Netherlands ,   
2 CONT **d. 1662, Leiden, Zuid-Holland, Netherlands  
2 CONT *5. Paul Paulus Merlet,   
2 CONT **b. 14 Sep 1653, Mannheim, Baden-Wuerttemberg, Germany ,   
2 CONT **d. 1699, Quebec, Canada  
2 CONT *6. Abraham Merlette,   
2 CONT **b. 26 Jan 1656, Long Island, Kings, New York, USA ,   
2 CONT **d. 1 Jun 1714, Pisacataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, USA  
2 CONT *7. Jean Pierre Mellott,   
2 CONT **b. 18 Apr 1658, Mannheim, Baden-Wuerttemberg, Germany ,   
2 CONT **d. 13 Jan 1702, Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, USA 
2 CONT ***OUR ANCESTOR
2 CONT *8. Anne Merlet,   
2 CONT **b. 1666, Staten Island, Richmond, New York, USA , 
2 CONT **d. 25 Aug 1681, Hellsgate, , New York, USA  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gideon Mellott, born 1620 in Roucy, Picardy/Champagne, France; died February 1682/83 in New Jersey. He married Marie "Marjin" Martin August 21, 1644 in Waloon Church Leyden, Holland. Marie "Marjin" Martin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Gideon Mellott: Gideon Merlitt or Mellott arrived in New Amsterdam on 12 October 1662 on the ship the Church Pumerland, or "De Purmerlander Kerck", with Captain Benjamin Barentsz in command. The passenger lists "Gideon M
2 CONC erlitt, wife and four children ages, 15, 8, 6 and 4 years old. Gideon brought along a nephew but the brother's and nephew's name is not known. This was copied from the Holland Society of New York's Yearbook of 1902, page 22. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This French Hugenot family had fled to Holland earlier because of religious persecution. Their ancestors had held high government positions in France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gideon was a constable at Dover Staten Island New Amsterdam in 1671. In 1674 he was a magistrate under Governor Colve. In 1683 he was on the list of early settlers in Piscataway and Woodbridge, New Jersey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Gideon Mellott and Marie Martin are: 
2 CONT *1. John Peterson Mellott, born April 18, 1658 in Manheim, Germany; died 1704 in Middlesex County, New Jersey; married Marie Bellmain. 
2 CONT *2. Paul Mellott. 
2 CONT *3. Josias Mellott. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gideon Merlet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gideon Merlet (also written Marles and Marlet), the son of Josias Merlet and Jeanne Robb, is believed to have been born in about 1625 at Roussy in Champagne Province, France. As a young man, Gideon fled the religious persecution t
2 CONC hat was taking place in France, and moved to Leiden in the Province of Zuid Holland in the Netherlands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gideon was later married in the Walloon Church at Leiden to a woman named Marguerite Martijn on 21 August 1644. According to their marriage record, Gideon had arrived at Leiden from Gouda in the Netherlands during the month of Dec
2 CONC ember 1643, while Marguerite had come from the Limburg region in the southeast part of Holland and northeast Belgium. Gideon was a carpenter by trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT In about 1650, Gideon and Marguerite left Leiden and moved to the City of Mannheim in the Palatinate of Germany (now the State of Baden-Wuerttemberg, Germany), where they remained for nearly 12 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gideon, his wife, Marguerite, four sons aged 15, 8, 6, and 4, Gideon's uncle, Philip Merlet, and his wife's cousin, Jeanne Martijn, later emigrated to America, arriving in the Dutch colony of New Amsterdam (now New York City, New 
2 CONC York) on 12 October 1662 aboard the ship "De Purmerlander Kerck." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly thereafter, on 19 March 1663, Gideon, along with a number of other emigrants, petitioned the government for "grants of land and seed grain, with provisions for six months." Gideon subsequently received a grant of land on S
2 CONC taten Island, and it is there that he and his family settled.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1671, Gideon was appointed Constable of Staten Island by Governor Francis Lovelace, and in 1674, he was appointed a magistrate by Governor Coive. Sometime later, Gideon and Marguerite appear to have moved to Piscataway in the P
2 CONC rovince of East Jersey (now within Middlesex County, New Jersey), where they resided for the remainder of their lives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ben notes: I believe that the move from Staten Island was probably for the safety of Gedeon and his family. Gedeon took on the position of Constable under English Governor Francis Lovelace. During Governor Lovelace's trip to Bost
2 CONC on to organize a postal route between the two colonies, the Dutch under Admiral Cornelis Evertsen the Youngest - nicknamed "Little Cornelis the Devil" because of his temper - retook New Amsterdam. Governor Lovelace was imprisoned 
2 CONC in the Tower of London by the Duke of York for failing to defend his colony. I speculate that when Gedeon accepted the position of Magistrate under Dutch Governor Captain Anthony Colve in what was apparently the last month of Dutc
2 CONC h occupation, he made it quite easy for the returning English to think of him as a collaborator with the enemy. He likely left with Captain Colve for New Jersey, where Colve was accepted by the English proprietors John Berkeley an
2 CONC d George Carteret as their governor, despite his former service to the Dutch.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite is believed to have died at Piscataway on 25 August 1681, while Gideon died there during the month of February 1682/83.
2 CONT
2 CONT The variation in the spelling of the surname MARLET (MARLE, MERLE) may stem from the inconsistency in transcribing and pronouncing the surname MARLET in a time and place (New York 1662-1700) where French and Dutch-speaking immigra
2 CONC nts were still being assimilated by the English-speaking majority. 
2 CONT
2 CONT New York before 1664 was Dutch-speaking Nieuw Amsterdam, and the French-speaking Calvinists or Huguenots who accompanied the Dutch to the New World began to have their names spelled many different ways, but usually dictated by the
2 CONC  English-speaking clerks who spelled names as they heard them. The British pronunciation of pall-mall ("pell-mell") and Derby ("Darby) can suggest how the name of Merlet (originally Marle or a diminutive or Merle or its diminutive
2 CONC ) came to be pronounced "MAR-LETT" by the English-speaking neighbors of Gedeon Merlet or Marlett of Staten Island and New Jersey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The connection between the two names MARLE and MERLE, and later Marlett, Marlatt, and Mellott, can be traced back to French language and custom. For example, the arms of the MARLOT family of Champagne show three "MERLETTES" (martl
2 CONC ets). Thus the merlette was the symbol in battle for the family named Marlot. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The French suffixes frequently changed to suggest a certain branch of the family. Both names may have been used  interchangeably, but the English would definitely have prounced the spelling Merlet as MAR-LETT. I believe this is wh
2 CONC y so many of Gedeon's descendants maintained that spelling. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Peter, evidently, dropped the "r" and was frequently referred to as John Peterson Melot, Mellat, or Mallat. Theodore's 1694 baptismal record shows the spelling MELOT, which became Mellott among his son John Mellott's descenda
2 CONC nts, mainly in Pennsylvania. Theodore himself signed his name MALOT, whereas most of his second wife's (Catherine de la Chaumette or Delashmutt) children carried on the Malott spelling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: In Leyden, Holland, in the Records of the Church of Leide, exist the following records for Gedeon Merlette/Merlet/Marlet.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Received into the membership of the Church of Leide, Decemeber, 1643, Merlette, Gedeon, "surnomme La Plante" (nicknamed La Plante), according to the records of the Church of Ter Gouda".
2 CONT
2 CONT "Received as member of the Church of Leide December, 1651, Merlet, Gedeon, and Marguerite his wife, according to the records of Paris."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Baptized in the Church of Mannheim the 7th of February, 1656, Merlette, Abraham, son of Gedeon and Marguerite Martin."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Baptized in the Church of Mannheim (The French Huguenot Church) May 2, 1658 Merlet, Jean Pierre, son of Gedeon and Marguerite Martin."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Member of the Church of Leide, Merlet, Gedeon, and his wife, part of the record of March 15, 1664."
2 CONT
2 CONT [Please note: Although Gedeon and Marguerite (Martin) Merlet had emigrated to New Amsterdam in 1662, while it was still in the control of the Dutch, they evidently were still considered members of the Church at Leyden, Holland.]
2 CONT
2 CONT !Betrothal: Wallon Church of Leyden, Holland, Betrothalbook N, p. 114: "Aenget. de Augusti 1644. Gedeon Merleth, timmerman, jongman van de Rousy, wonende op de Santestraet, vergesz. met Philip Merleth, sijn oom, wonende mede aldae
2 CONC r. Margriet Martijn, jongedr. van Limborch, wonende op de Langegraft, vergesz. met Jenne Martijn, haer nicht, wonende mede aldaer."
2 CONT
2 CONT Translation is as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Entered August 6, 1644. Gedeon Merlet, a carpenter from Rousy, dwelling in "Sand Street", accompanied by Phillipe Merlet, his uncle, also living there with Marguerite Martijn (Martin) from Limbourg, accompanied by Jenne Jeanne) Ma
2 CONC rtijn, her cousin, also living there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gedeon Merlet and Maguerite Martijn (Martin) were married in the Walloon Church of Leyden on August 21, 1644. There are baptismal reccords there for their children on these dates:
2 CONT
2 CONT "September 17, 1645: Josias (Josue), son of Gedeon Merlette. The witnesses were Claude Marcy, Jean Martijn, Jeanne Martijn, and Clasinne Tertelim."
2 CONT
2 CONT "November 11, 1646: Marie, daughter of Gedeon Merlette. Witnesses were Daniel Fromavesijn, Francois Charret, Jacquemine du Lon, and Catherine de la Tour."
2 CONT
2 CONT "July 26, 1648: Esechias, son of Gedeon Merlette and Marguerite Martin. Witnesses were Paul Merlette, Augustin Goftin, Marie Cordies."
2 CONT
2 CONT After living in Leyden from about 1643 until the 1650's, Gedeon, Marguerite and the first three children moved to Mannheim, in the German Palatinate, an area known as a haven for French Huguenots at that time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There two more sons were born and baptized, namely Abraham and Jean Pierre MERLET, MERLETTE, MARLETTE or MARLET.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the records of the French Huguenot Church of Mannheim, Germany:
2 CONT
2 CONT "On February 7, 1656, was baptized in the Church of Mannheim, Merlette, Abraham, son of Gedeon and Marguerite Martin."
2 CONT
2 CONT "On May 2, 1658 was baptized in the Church of Mannheim, Merlet, Jean Pierre, son of Gedeon and Marguerite Martin."
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidently the family returned to Holland between 1658 and 1662. They resumed their membership at the Walloon Church of Leyden. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gedeon and his uncle Philippe had been listed there in June 1643 as having come from Gouda. This uncle may be the same person as the "Phillipe Merlet, born at "Crum", widower of Magdalena Gilbert," who married at Amsterdam on 12 O
2 CONC ctober, 1647, "Jannete Pau, born at Sedan, a widow." 
2 CONT
2 CONT If "Rousy" is Roucy in Champagne, it is interesting to note that there is a very old town called "Craon" not far away.
2 CONT
2 CONT When I was in Paris in 1988, I visited the French National Archives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the section for documents concerning "fugitifs religionnaires" or those who were persecuted for their religious beliefs, I opened an old file containing a letter to the Count of Roucy and it was written about 1599. The letter d
2 CONC iscussed the Count's Calvinist religious beliefs and basically was a warning that prior to the Edict of Nantes, his estates would have been confiscated, but since the Edict, they would not be taken from him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A warning tone was evident. It was this Count, of the family de la Rochefoucauld, who held the chateau at Roucy, in Champagne, France, during the lifetime of Josue Marle or Merle and Jeanne Robbe, the parents of Gedeon Merlet. A l
2 CONC ocal historian told me that the Counts of Roucy were Calvinists (Huguenots), and it is perhaps for this reason that the Marle/Merle family was living in Roucy in the late 1500's. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Edict of Nantes was revoked by Louis the Fourteenth on __________, but many Huguenots had already left France for the Netherlands, for the Palatinate in Germany, and for New Amsterdam, where a much greater tolerance than in Lo
2 CONC uis XIV's France prevailed for the Calvinist religious beliefs of the Huguenots.
2 CONT
2 CONT From SHIP PASSENGER LISTS: NEW YORK AND NEW JERSEY (1600-1825), edited and indexed by Carl Boyer, 3rd (Self-Published: Newhall, CA, 1978), p. 130.
2 CONT
2 CONT "October 12, 1662. In De Purmerlander Kerck Gedeon Merlitt, wife and four children, 15, 8, 6, and 4 years old."
2 CONT
2 CONT [The four sons were Josue or Josias; Poulus or Paul; Jean Pierre or John Peter; Abraham.]
2 CONT
2 CONT [Note that Gedeon's children Maria and Eseschias Merlet had died before the family emigrated from Holland in 1662. One more child, Anne Merlet was born after arriving in New Amsterdam.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: NY ABSTRACTS OF WILLS: LIBER 19 B, p. 427.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Chirurgeon's report about the death of Mary Marshall and Anne Marlett, New York, August 25, 1681. These are to certify that I, being requested by John Collier, Coroner, to view and search the dead bodies of Mary Marshall and Ann 
2 CONC Marlett, doe declare that after strict search and enquiry, I find no other accident to have occasioned their sudden death, but being overset in a canoe and thus drowned.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Lockhart.
2 CONT
2 CONT Return of Coroner's Jury that they were accidentally drowned. Action in New York, this 25 August, 1681. John Lawrence, Jr., Paul Richards, Henry Filkin."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Marlett and her friend Mary Marshall drowned while trying to ride a canoe in Hellsgate, in New York's East River.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is an address made by C. L. Marlatt on Oct. 27, 1935 on behalf of the Huguenot Memorial Association on the occasion of the 250th Anniversary of the Edict of Nantes. The address took place at the Memorial Church of th
2 CONC e Huguenots at Huguenot Park, Staten Island, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Two additional Huguenot immigrants have recently been honored by having their names recorded in this edifice, namely Gedeon Merlet and his youngest son, Abraham. Gedeon Merlet was one of many Huguenots who found freedom from reli
2 CONC gious persecution in Holland during the first half of the 17th century. Under the promise of lands and aid in getting started in the New World, he with some 29 other refugees came over to the New Netherlands in October 1662, on th
2 CONC e ship "Purmerland Church". With him were his wife Marguerite Martin and four minor sons, namely Joshua, born 1647; Paul, born 1654; John, born 1656; and Abraham, born 1658.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in the year following his arrival, 1663, Gedeon Merlet, with six other Huguenot immigrants, joined in a petition to the Director General and the Council of New Netherlands for grants of suitable land, provision for
2 CONT
2 CONT temporary subsistence, and seed grain so that, as they stated, "the supplicants may exert their industry and zeal without obstruction in the cultivation of the land, not only for their personal benefit, but also for the welfare an
2 CONC d good of the whole country. They also promised ultimately to fully repay the Council for such advances.
2 CONT
2 CONT This petition, the original of which is now on file in the state archives at Albany, was approved, and in the instance of Gedeon Merlet resulted in the assignment of land to himself and his sons in Staten Island, much of it in the
2 CONC  vicinity and possibly covering the site of this church edifice. Here he lived the balance of his life, and his children grew to manhood."
2 CONT
2 CONT The fragments of the history of this period which have come down to us, in addition to records of land purchase and and transfers, indicate that Gedeon Merlet, during the first British occupancy of New Netherlands, was
2 CONT
2 CONT appointed by Governor Francis Lovelace as a constable in Staten Island (April 20, 1671). A little later, under the very brief re-occupancy of the Colony by the Dutch, he was appointed by Governor Colve as a magistrate (February 14
2 CONC , 1674). 
2 CONT
2 CONT We have no record of his death, which must have  occurred prior to 1683, as indicated by the inheritance and division of his land. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gedeon's youngest son, Abraham, through whom my family is descended, married about 1677 Christence Billieu, daughter of Pierre Billieu - a Huguenot immigrant of 1661 and prominent in the early settlement of Staten Island. Two chil
2 CONC dren came from this union, viz., Margaret, baptized March 31, 1678 and Abraham, May 16, 1680.
2 CONT
2 CONT We have little information as to Abraham, the youngest son of Gedeon, except that he was for a period collector for the southern part of Staten Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Merlet very soon lost its French pronunciation and spelling, changing to Marlet, Marlett and Marlatt, with many other spellings.
2 CONT
2 CONT From this beginning the Marlatt family has multiplied and spread widely in the United States and Canada. Mention may appropriately be made here of a descendant of Gedeon Merlet who made a long and earnest attempt to trace and reco
2 CONC ver the records of the family, namely Mrs. Jane Marlett Taft, wife of the late Chief Justice of the Vermont, Russell S. Taft. Unfortunately she did not live to complete her work, but her manuscript is now in my possession, and if 
2 CONC not utilized and published, will be filed for permanent record in the Library of Congress in Washington, to be available for future students."
2 CONT
2 CONT [Mr. C. L. Marlatt, who delivered the above address, referred to his ancestor, Abraham Merlet as "the youngest child" of Gedeon's family. Perhaps in 1935 the baptismal certificate of Jean Pierre Merlet had not yet been found at th
2 CONC e French Huguenot of Mannheim, in the Palatinate region of Germany. The baptismal record of Jean Pierre Merlet (born 1658) indicates he was born after Abraham Merlet (born 1656).
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: NY Colonial Manuscripts, Vol. 10, Part 2, p. 49. Translation from Dutch.
2 CONT
2 CONT 19 March, 1663: "To the Hon. Director General and Council of New Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Show with due reverence and respect to your Honorable Worships, Nicollas Dupuij, Gedeon Merlet, Arnold Dutroij, Jacques Cossart, Louijs Laakman, Jacob Kolf, and Jean Le Cancelier, that the supplicants while in Holland, by the advi
2 CONC ce of some gentlemen there as well as by the reading of the printed New Netherland conditions were urged and moved to betake themselves with their whole families to these regions, in the hope that your Hon. Worships agreeable to t
2 CONC he aforesaid New Netherland conditions would come to their assistance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The supplicants address themselves therefore to your Hon. Worships with the humble request that you may please to assigne and grant them suitable lands and also to furnish them with seed grain and necessary provisions for six mont
2 CONC hs, in order that they, the supplicants, may exert their industry and zeal without obstruction in the cultivation of the land, not only for their personal benefit, but also for the welfare and the good of the whole country, expect
2 CONC ing to behave and conduct themselves in such manner that they will hereafter be able to make good and repay with thanks all that has thus far been advanced to them and what they may expect from your Honors' usual benevolence.
2 CONT
2 CONT This doing they remain
2 CONT
2 CONT Your Hon. Worships' servants,
2 CONT *Nicolas du puis
2 CONT *gedeon merlet (with paraph)
2 CONT *Arnoult du toict [Note: a relative of Philippe du Trieux]
2 CONT *Jacque cossart
2 CONT *Louis Lacqueman
2 CONT *Jacob Kolver
2 CONT *Jean Le concilie"
2 CONT
2 CONT [Gedeon Merlet's request was granted. See section  above.]
2 CONT
