1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://resources.huygens.knaw.nl/vrouwenlexicon/lemmata/data/Cusance
2 CONT
2 CONT >CUSANCE, Béatrix-Marie-Françoise de , best known as Duchess of Lorraine (born Belvoir, Franche-Comté 27-12-1614 – d. Besançon 5-6-1663), known for her friendship and correspondence with Constantijn Huygens. Daughter of Claude-Fra
2 CONC nçois de Cusance (1590-1627), officer in the Spanish service, and Ernestine van Withem (1592-1642). Béatrix de Cusance married (1) Léopold-Eugène Perrenot de Granvelle (1615-1637) on 6-3-1635 in Brussels ; (2) on 2-4-1637 in Besan
2 CONC çon with Charles IV de Vaudémont (1604-1675), Duke of Lorraine. From marriage (1) 1 daughter was born, from (2) 1 daughter and 2 sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT > In 1634, Béatrix de Cusance had a love affair with Duke Charles IV of Lorraine, an army commander in Spanish service whose troops were notorious for their looting. When Karel courted Béatrix, he was already a married man – his l
2 CONC egal wife was Nicole of Lorraine, a first cousin. To avoid disgrace, Béatrix's mother quickly married her to Léopold-Eugène Perrenot de Granvelle, Prince of Cantecroix. From this marriage a girl was born on January 15, 1636, who o
2 CONC nly lived a few months. A year later, on February 6, 1637, Léopold-Eugène died. Only nine days later, Béatrix and Karel, still officially married, announced their intention to marry and were married on April 2, 1637. In the autumn
2 CONC  of 1637 Béatrix had a son, François. It was unclear who the biological father was, but the duke acknowledged the child. For more than 25 years, Léopold-Eugène's mother would conduct lawsuits about this before the Council of Fland
2 CONC ers and the Great Council of Mechelen.
