2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Vittoria Francesca di Savoia was the child of Victor Amadeus II, Duke of Savoy and his favourite mistress Jeanne Baptiste d'Albert de Luynes. Born in Turin on 9 February, 1690, her parents affair had began in early 1689 when
2 CONC  her mother Jeanne Baptiste had caught the eye of the Duke of Savoy. Her mother was originally unwilling to become the mistress of the Duke but was encouraged by her husband, mother in law and even the Dukes wife Anne Marie d'Orlé
2 CONC ans[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parents were soon in love and the reign of her mother Jeanne Baptiste had began at the Savoyard court; her mothers husband was horrified at Jeanne Baptiste having become the Dukes mistress and soon extracted many a favour from
2 CONC  the Duke. Her mothers popularity made her unpopular at the Savoyard court and at one point she was nearly poisened and it was none other then the Duchess of Savoy who helped Jeanne Baptiste with her illness and helped her recover
2 CONC . Maria Vittoria's father became obssessed with Jeanne Baptiste; he had her shut up from view of the court for some time; Jeanne Baptiste decided to flee Savoy in 1700 and seek refuge in France, ruled by Louis XIV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Vittoria had one full sibling Vittorio Francesco who died in 1762, aged 68 having had no children. When her mother fled Savoy in 1700, Maria Vittoria and her brother were left in Savoy and were thus cared for by a loving fat
2 CONC her. It was her father who organised Maria Vittoria's marriage to Prince Victor Amadeus of Savoy, son of the Prince of Carignan. The Princes of Carignan were members of the so called House of Savoy-Carignan and a cadet branch of t
2 CONC he House of Savoy. They were the most important people in the Duchy of Savoy after the Ducal family themselves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father had her legitimised and created the Marchesa di Susa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Engaged to the son of the dead Prince of Carignan and the beauty Maria Angela Caterina d'Este in mid 1714, she married Prince Victor Amadeus of Savoy at the Castle of Moncalieri on 7 November aged 23. Her father gave her husband a
2 CONC n annual income of 400,000 livres partly to appease the union; her father was fond of Prince Victor Amadeus but in 1717, her husband was deeply in debt and lost the Kings favour[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result Maria Vittoria's husband fled to France in July 1718 during the Regency of Philippe d'Orléans; her husband travelled as the Comte de Bosco and soon after in Maria Vittoria followed; her husband set about founding an It
2 CONC alian opera house with his own set designs, only one of the grand schemes for which Vittorio became notorious in Paris. He had engaged the best voices in Italy for the project, but sadly it folded within two months. It appears tha
2 CONC t Paris was not then ready for such an ambitious project.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple settled in Paris amongst the court of the infant Louis XV who lived at the Tuileries Palace. Her husband was created Intendant des Ménus Plaisirs - a sort of Master of Ceremonies by the Régent and the couple lived at th
2 CONC e Hôtel de Soissons which they claimed by right of the Soissons inheritance which had been confiscated when Savoy had become an enemy of France under Louis XIV during the War of the Spanish Succession. During the Regency, Maria Vi
2 CONC ttoria and her husband led a louche life at the Hôtel de Soissons turning it into one of the most dangerous for gambling in the capital. Maria Vittoria eventually developed a close relationship with Cardinal Fleury [3] However, sh
2 CONC e was also an intimate of the Duke of Bourbon, Louis XV's prime minister after the death of Phillipe d'Orléans.
2 CONT
2 CONT These connections were important as the Prince Victor Amadeus proceeded to incur massive debts in France to add to those already contracted in Savoy (he eventually died a bankrupt in 1744) so it was important to keep in with the g
2 CONC reat officers of the court of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of a dramatic nature, she happily intruiged with the Duke of Bourbon and reported all happenings to her father back in Savoy. She also reported first and last attempt of Queen Marie to try to influence Louis XV politically - which
2 CONC  involved the Duke of Bourbon's trying to dispose of Fleury, a move which ended very badly for Monsieur de Bourbon. However, the Maria Vittoria sided with Fleury, and when Monsieur de Bourbon suggested via an intermediary that if 
2 CONC she tried to mend the relationship between himself and the Cardinal that he would pay off her husband's huge debts in both France and Savoy and settle an income of half a million livres on her, she indignantly refused.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the Queen sought her advice as to how to reconcile with the King, who had been very offended by Marie's attempts to interfere between himself and Fleury, Maria Vittoria never became very intimate with the Queen and in 172
2 CONC 6 she and Fleury were speculating about who would replace Marie if she should die in childbirth.
2 CONT
2 CONT She saw her son marry Christine Henriette of Hesse-Rotenburg[4] in 1740.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband died at the Hôtel de Soissons in April 1740 heavily in debt; she lived quietly as a widow but successfully managed to marry her only surviving daughter Anna Teresa[5] to marry the once widowed Charles de Rohan, Prince 
2 CONC of Soubise. Anna Teresa had one child; Victoire Armande Josèphe de Rohan was the governess of Louis XVI's daughter Marie-Thérèse de France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1763 Leopold Mozart wrote in a letter that "today my little girl was given a small, transparent snuff-box, inlaid with gold, from the Princess Carignan, and Wolfgang a pocket writing case in silver, with silver pens with which 
2 CONC to write his compositions; it is so small and exquisitely worked that it is impossible to describe it".
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Paris on 8 July, 1766 aged 76. She was the paternal grand mother of the princesse de Lamballe, friend of Marie Antoinette.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Her daughter Anna Teresa
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Joseph Victor Amadeus of Savoy (1716 - 1716)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Anna Teresa of Savoy (Turin, 1 November, 1717 - Paris, 5 April, 1745) married Charles de Rohan and had issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Louis Victor of Savoy, Prince of Carignan (Paris, 25 September, 1721 - Turin, 16 December, 1778)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Victor Amadeus of Savoy (1722)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * A stillborn daughter (1729)
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maria Vittoria Francesca of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ daughter of Philippe de France (brother of Louis XIV) and Princess Henrietta Anne of England
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ |Victor Amadeus II, Duke of Savoy had become King of Sicily in 1713 due to the Treaty of Utrecht ending the War of the Spanish Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. ^ She pretends to be devout and makes money out of the transactions of the court through the Cardinal, with whom she is on good terms.
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. ^ whose sister Caroline of Hesse-Rotenburg had married the disgraced Duke of Bourbon in 1728
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. ^ known as Anne Thérèse in France
2 CONT
