2 CONT ----------------------------------------------
2 CONT Judith Rigaud, born in France about 1633/36.  On 22 June 1651 she contracted 5 years to Marguerite Gardeur (spouse of Jacques LeNeuf #894) at Trois-Rivieres, St Maurice, Quebec, Canada.  While still under contract, she married 6 M
2 CONC ay 1654 to Francois Lemaitre.  The breach of contract was settled by a payment to Marguerite Gardeur for the unserved time of the contract.  Her marriage to Francois Lemaitre produced 8 children, the last child born after the deat
2 CONC h of Francois, who was killed by the Iroquois.  His burial record dated 14 Jan 1666 is shown in the records of Immaculee Conception Church at Trois-Rivieres (have copy of this record).  Prior to the death of her spouse, who was pr
2 CONC one to heavy drinking, she left him and returned to France.  Unknown to her at the time, she was pregnant with child (Charles) who was born 15 Apr 1666 in France.  Judith returned to Canada in the summer of 1666 and was jolted by 
2 CONC the news that her spouse had died and was heavily indebted. This debt was settled in Aug of 1668.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tanguay Vol V pg 309 shows Judith remarried 26 Jan 1667 at Immaculee Conception Church, Trois-Rivieres (have copy of this record) to Jean Terrien (aka Jean Duhèmme).  Jean died about 1670.  Two children were born of this marriage 
2 CONC although "Jette" pg 1072 shows 3 children.  The children were: Dominique, bapt. 6 Nov 1667 and buried 6 Dec 1667 (have copy of these records); Jean, born 16 and bapt. 17 Mar 1669 (have copy of this record); married 1700 to Marguer
2 CONC ite Laspron.  These records are in the register of Immaculee Conception Church, Trois-Rivieres.  The third child (Louis Michel) was born 19 Mar 1671 and appears in the records of Immaculee Conception Church, Trois-Rivieres dated 5
2 CONC  Apr 1671.  This bapt. record indicates some confusion as to the name of the father.  It is assumed that the child was born out of wedlock but was known as Louis Michel Terrien.  According to "Our French Canadian Ancestors"-Lamber
2 CONC t, the father of this child was Jean Duhèmme (this name is the aka of Jean Terrien).  The name Jean Duhèmme does appear on the baptism register.  Just preceeding his name on the register there is an area that is heavily scratched 
2 CONC out and not legible.  Could this have been another name? (have copy of this record).
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith again remarried 6 Oct 1675 at Immaculee Conception Church, Trois-Rivieres (have copy of this record) to Jean de Laplanche who was the first resident surgeon of Riviere-du-Loup, Louiseville, Maskinonge, Quebec, Canada.  This
2 CONC  marriage is listed on pg 650 of "Jette".  In 1679 Judith was arrested and accused of deserting her family and living with another man named Pierre Cavelier.  Judith was banned from Montreal for 10 years because of this action.  H
2 CONC er husband, Jean de Laplanche returned to France, leaving his unfaithful wife in June of 1678.  Pierre Cavelier also abandoned her to take a legitimate wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that Judith returned to Louiseville and appears on the 1681 census as Judith Desauneaux. living with her son-in-law, Guillaume Christophe Gerbault, who married Judith's daughter Marguerite Lemaitre.  (see "Jette" pg
2 CONC  489 for this family.)
2 CONT
2 CONT A fourth marriage for Judith is shown on LDS Family Registry #39534 dated 6 Feb 1686 to Louis Gillet.  Intensive search was made to find a hint of this marriage, but nothing was found to confirm or deny same.  Tanguay Vol I pg 374
2 CONC  shows another marriage dated 30 July 1690 to Jacques Abelin at Ste Anne.   Could not locate any proof of this event.  However, LDS IGI of Quebec as of 1992 shows this Jacques Abelin married to Marie Gasnier (aka Gagnon) at Ste An
2 CONC ne on this date.  Could Judith have used another alias?
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith died 13 May 1703 according to LDS Ancestral File, group record of self (copy filed 50-2).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is to be noted, that in several of the articles about her, that she was apparently well educated and had a very good handwriting.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.google.com/search?sourceid=navclient&q=%22judith+rigaud%22> a translated version follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Live the love
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith Rigaud, a girl to be married, enters to the service of Marguerite LeGardeur, son of Jacques LeNeuf of the Pottery, about 1652. She is originating in Midsummer's Day d'Angely, in Charente-Maritime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Remaining at Three-Rivers, she succumbs to the charm, undoubtedly exceptional, of François Lemaistre aka Lamorille 23 years old from Picardy, which, with the quality of a fine talker, and a heavy drinker!
2 CONT
2 CONT Lemaistre marries the his beauty 6 May 1654, for best or worst. Seven children are born under the lenient sky from Three-Rivers while Lemaistre accumulates the debts and ruins his health. In 1665, Judith Rigaud must go to France, 
2 CONC to undoubtedly regulate there some business problems of succession. It is probable qu?au moment of its departure, Judith is unaware of qu?elle is "in an interesting state". What qu?il is, Charles, last child of the couple, is born
2 CONC  at Saint-Jean-de-la-Rochelle  where he is baptized, April 15, 1666.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between the diapers and the business of her husband, Judith Rigaud engages personally to refund the debts of which he was jusqu?àlors the only person in charge. She poses this generous gesture, "believing to find her husband livin
2 CONC g" and of the goods in sufficient quality and quantity to assume the expenditure of some effects bought with Amault Perré and intended to be resold here. The return in boat will be without pity for the herds and the goods bought i
2 CONC n France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another disappointment: Judith learns while going down from boat, with he summer of 1666, that Lemaistre is no more, having been buried on previous 14 January. But Judith, who is now the head of a family of eight children, still h
2 CONC as many charms: January 26, 1667, she convoles with Jean Terrien with whom she will have two other children. One does not know when this second husband dies, however, Judith contracts a third union, October 6, 1675, with the surge
2 CONC on Jean de LaPlanche, first doctor to be resided at the River-of-Wolf (today Louiseville).
2 CONT
2 CONT This marriage n?est not of happiest. In 1677, Judith Rigaud abondonné the marital home and emigrant in Montreal where, without contract nor blessing, she lives under the same roof as Pierre Cavelier. At the 15th century, in New-Fr
2 CONC ance, the cohabitation is rather badly carried. It is undoubtedly why the sovereign Council hesitated to stop Judith under the accusation of having abandoned the marital home. With the social vexations that the financial vexations
2 CONC  that the woman already knows well.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Robert-Lionel Séguin, "marked publicly that she was the concubine of Cavelier, Judith Rigaud will manage to be rehabilitated opposite the public opinion and to finish her days respected by her neighbors".
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Our p180 roots
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.genealogie.org/famille/therrien/Ancetres.htm> a translated version follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestors Jean and Pierre Terrien
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestors, Pierre and Gabrielle, Jean and Judith
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Terrien and Gabrielle Mignot
2 CONT
2 CONT April 5, 1656 front the Moreau notary of LaRochelle, Pierre begins to work in Quebec for one three years period with wages of 60 livres per annum (roughly 40,00$) all paid expenditure. After this period, Pierre decides to be estab
2 CONC lished on the Island of Orleans. May 17, 1670 he marries Gabrielle Minau and from this couple were born 12 boys and two girls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage certificate of Jean Terrien and Judith Rigaud
2 CONT
2 CONT The year of grace 1667, the twenty-sixth day of January, after the proclammation of the bans made by three consecutive Sundays, the first the 9th second 16th, the last 2é the day of the known as month, in the parochial mass; being
2 CONC  found any prevention canonical, me, Jean Frémont, priest of this parish of the Three-Rivers, day questioned Jean Terrien in the church of this parish, son of Jean Terrien and Marie Hélie, her father and mother, of the parish of S
2 CONC t-Jacques de Dieppe, and Judith Rigault, this same parish, widow of François LeMaistre, inhabitant of this place; and, having received their mutual assent, the married day, with the required ceremonies, in the presence of Maurice 
2 CONC Foal and of Louis Godefroy known as Normandville.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://iquebec.ifrance.com/leotherrien/article3.html>.  It contained a copy of the contract of marriage of Jean Terrien and Judith Rigaud and could not be trannlated.  Acopy of this contract is fil
2 CONC ed in 50-3.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://iquebec.ifrance.com/leotherrien/article2.html> a translated version follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Therrien ancestor and the fur trade
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of a meeting of the members, the autumn passed, we advanced that the consultation of the texts published in the reviews was significant to locate the bonds with the people surrounding our ancestors.
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time, we had spoken about Marguerite FLYING, girl of Claude STEALING sior of Saint-Claude, of unknown origin, then commercial with Three-Rivers ( Jetté , p.1131), deceased about 1690. It married about 1653 with Three-River
2 CONC s with Francoise RADISSON, girl of Pierre-Spirit and Madeleine HÉNAULT, Saint-Sulpice in Paris. The Jetté dictionary quotes nine children, of which this Francoise Radisson, sister of Élisabeth woman of Claude JUTRAS and half-siste
2 CONC r of Marguerite HAYET, woman of Jean MINNOW.
2 CONT
2 CONT By examining the names of the people present at the marriage of LAND Jean and Judith RUGAUD, we could find the ground of the ancestor who is located at the corner of theSaint-Maurice streets and Saint-Paul with Three-Rivers (1).
2 CONT
2 CONT Faithful so that we advance in the review Therrien (vol.1, no.1, p.14), we always affirm, and with more conviction still, that LAND Jean and Judith RIGAUD have bonds with the trade of the furs .
2 CONT
2 CONT The father of LAND Jean was a merchant-clothier : here are which predisposes with the trade of the fur in News-France. By consulting the dictionary of Rene Jetté and by raising the various ancestors and individuals from Europe, it
2 CONC  is easy to locate people according to their trade, and it is relevant also to gather them by area of origin; that leads us to outline the networks of relations and to look further into our study on the subject.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before leaning us on the people quoted with the marriage contract, we must also check the way in which the individuals wrote.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sailor TERRIE(R) - perhaps "N" - sior of Repentigny and Francheville, originating in Saint Martin's day of Large-Mesnil, is évêché of Lisieux in Normandy ( Jetté , p.1066); soldier killed by Iroquois with Three-Rivers on August 19
2 CONC , 1652, it married in Quebec on September 3, 1647 (ct. Tronquet) with Jeanne JALEAU says Ploumelle, of Fontenay-the-Count, Poitou. The latter was the maidservant of Pierre LEGARDEUR in 1647, it was remariée in Maurice POULIN in 16
2 CONC 54 and had the concession of theSaint-Maurice seigniory on August 14, 1676, yielded to his/her children on January 19, 1683. LAND sailor [? ] and Jeanne JALEAU were pilot with the marriage of LAND Jean and Judith RIGAUD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another fact concerning the LEGARDEUR: Marguerite LEGARDEUR (always according to Jetté, p.695) also came from Normandy. Pierre, the brother of Marguerite, is deceased in May 1648 while returning from the LaRochelle, and Ignace, hi
2 CONC s son, signs "sior of the Culvert": he was born with Our-Lady-of-Knocks (La Rochelle). To notice that LAND Jean also signs "sior of the Culvert".
2 CONT
2 CONT Which was not our astonishment, by raising the names of the witnesses to the promise of marriage of LAND Jean (Dieppe) and Judith RIGAUD (Saint-Jean-Angely), to find there a range of the high society of Three-Rivers...
2 CONT
2 CONT Names that several have as ancestors who are quoted in an article of the Memories of the French Canadian genealogical company (2) by Hubert Charbonneau, deputy manager of the Research Programme in Historical Demography .
2 CONT
2 CONT The BASTION family is another military and commercial family . The author says that BASTON took again the trade of merchant , it is also quoted like soldier. This last collaborates with Daniel BRAILLE, a merchant calvinist - anoth
2 CONC er bond which we seek for the baptism of LAND Pierre (La Rochelle) and Gabrielle MIGNOT [ MINEAU ] (Fontenay-the-Count).
2 CONT
2 CONT This same Simon BASTON is not found with the census of 1666, just like LAND Jean and Judith RIGAUD. Simon BASTON is seen on the incoming vessel of France in 1663. And at the beginning of October 1665, it sets out again in France w
2 CONC ith a great quantity of skins being worth several thousands of books; it dies in enigmatic circumstances with the autumn of 1673.
2 CONT
2 CONT Witnesses of the husband (LAND Jean)
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnoult of LOUBIAS, captain to the regiment of Carignan
2 CONT Pierre BUTCHER of Grosbois, governor of Three-Rivers
2 CONT "Damoiselle" Jeanne CREVIER, her wife
2 CONT Claude GROUND, sior Desmarais, merchant
2 CONT
2 CONT Witnesses of the wife (Judith RIGAUD).
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine the MAISTRE of Morel, his/her brother-in-law
2 CONT Pierre and François the MAISTRE, his sons
2 CONT Michel NINE, rider, sior of the Hedgehog, civil and criminal lieutenant of Three-Rivers
2 CONT Young lady Marguerite LEGARDEUR, woman of Jacques NINE of the Pottery
2 CONT Louis GODEFROY of Normanville, rider, tax prosecutor of this place
2 CONT Damoiselle Marguerite SEIGNEURET, her wife
2 CONT Étienne SEIGNEURET
2 CONT Madeleine BÉNASSIS, her wife
2 CONT Simon BASTON, merchant
2 CONT Louis PLONK, syndic, and Marie HERTEL her wife (ancestor on the side of my mother)
2 CONT Maurice POULIN of the Fountain, prosecutor of the king
2 CONT Jeanne JALLAUX, her wife
2 CONT Jean GODEFROY of Linctot, rider
2 CONT Francoise RADISSON, woman of Claude FLYING of Saint-Claude, father of Marguerite
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth RADISSON married Claude JUTRAS; she is the sister of Francoise and thus the girl of the famous Pierre-Spirit RADISSON, intermediary, whose name related to is closely discovered roads for the trade of the fur . Jean-Franç
2 CONC ois, wire of FLYING Claude and Francoise RADISSON is a merchant of fur .
2 CONT
2 CONT In the next article, we will try to highlight these bonds extremely invaluable and filling with enthusiasm for research.
2 CONT
2 CONT by Léo Therrien, M.G.A.
2 CONT -------
