1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://richard.rathe.org/2006/fort-zeller-pennsylvania
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is taken from a chapter in the book entitled Notable Women of Pennsylvania, edited by Gertrude Biddle and Sarah Lowrie (Philadelphia, 1942) and is from a short biography of a French woman named Clothilde de Valois:
2 CONT
2 CONT The history of Clothilde Zeller, born in De Reni of a cadet branch of the Valois family, a subject of France, but through the persuasion of the Governor Keith and the astute Secretary Logan of Philadelphia a citizen of Pennsylvani
2 CONC a in pioneer days, is a typical one of a European immigrant to these shores.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born during the liberalism of the Edict of Nantes when it was the fashion for ladies of high rank to be educated, Margaret de Valois, Countess d’Augergne, in her chatelaineship at LeMont Dore left a reputation for letters, while t
2 CONC he Countess Clothilde de Valois de Reni, her many-times-removed cousin, fell in love with a scholar. In her teens, she married Jacques de Sellaire, or, if one knew as the family across the border, Von Zeller of Castle Zellerstein,
2 CONC  Zurich, and fled from France with him and their children after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes to Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after their son’s marriage in Holland the Zeller’s journeyed to England and the Lady Clothilde, as she was recorded there, place herself, her children, and husband under the protection of Queen Anne, who made them welcome 
2 CONC not only in England but, should they choose, in her colonies across the sea. With many of their race and a considerable number of their own class and position, they gladly accepted the chance to begin again in a new world. Clothil
2 CONC de at this time was the sole head of her family, her husband having died, either in London or before their ship arrived at New York in June 1710.
2 CONT
2 CONT These French immigrants, together with many of the Palatinate Germans who were coming to the Americas for the same reason – freedom to worship God according to their own conscience, had a bitter experience of scant welcome and ind
2 CONC ifferent choice of lands along the Hudson River. The Zeller’s and the French group – which had placed themselves with the Lady Clothilde for protection and advice found even their second venture father north, in the Dutch-English 
2 CONC province at Schoharie, little to their comfort though they named their settlement L’Esperance! It was at this juncture that Governor Keith of Pennsylvania paid them a visit and, impressed with their character and worth, invited th
2 CONC em over to Pennsylvania by a well known and already much used route down the Susquehanna River. Being French and already twice warned, the Lady Clothilde was wary of promises. So Jean Henri, her eldest son, with an Indian guide wa
2 CONC s sent ahead to prospect. Perhaps it was she who stipulated rich lands as well as clear titles, or perhaps Jean Henri, now in his thirties, was already aware of the soil from a farmer’s standpoint. At all events he journeyed down 
2 CONC the Susquehanna below where the great river branches at what is now Sunbury, paddled up a creek of some dimensions still known as the Swatara, and so across what is now the Lebanon Valley to a fertile region watered by the Tulpeho
2 CONC cken, in which black walnut trees of great age and fine verdure flourished. In the country of Jean Henri Zeller’s mother’s people, the French tradition was that black walnuts meant fertility and a deep soil. So armed with this ass
2 CONC uring promise, young Zeller journeyed by foot and canoe back to the Schoharie in northern New York and made his report to his mother and her party.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth: 	1660
2 CONT * Paris, France
2 CONT * Death: 	Jan. 2, 1749
2 CONT * Stouchsburg
2 CONT * Berks County
2 CONT * Pennsylvania, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried at Reeds cemetery according to Herbert P. Reed's book ''The Tulpehocken Reeds''. Arrived in New York, New York in 1710. This is a credible account of her life and accomplishments:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The history of Clothilde Zeller, born in De Reni of a cadet branch of the Valois family, a subject of France, but through the persuasion of the Governor Keith and the astute Secretary Logan of Philadelphia a citizen of Pennsylvan
2 CONC ia in pioneer days, is a typical one of a European immigrant to these shores.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born during the liberalism of the Edict of Nantes when it was the fashion for ladies of high rank to be educated, Margaret de Valois, Countess d'Augergne, in her chatelaineship at LeMont Dore left a reputation for letters, while t
2 CONC he Countess Clothilde de Valois de Reni, her many-times-removed cousin, fell in love with a scholar. In her teens, she married Jacques de Sellaire, or, if one knew as the family across the border, Von Zeller of Castle Zellerstein,
2 CONC  Zurich, and fled from France with him and their children after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes to Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after their son's marriage in Holland the Zeller's journeyed to England and the Lady Clothilde, as she was recorded there, place herself, her children, and husband under the protection of Queen Anne, who made them welcome 
2 CONC not only in England but, should they choose, in her colonies across the sea. With many of their race and a considerable number of their own class and position, they gladly accepted the chance to begin again in a new world. Clothil
2 CONC de at this time was the sole head of her family, her husband having died, either in London or before their ship arrived at New York in June 1710.
2 CONT
2 CONT These French immigrants, together with many of the Palatinate Germans who were coming to the Americas for the same reason - freedom to worship God according to their own conscience, had a bitter experience of scant welcome and ind
2 CONC ifferent choice of lands along the Hudson River. The Zeller's and the French group - which had placed themselves with the Lady Clothilde for protection and advice found even their second venture father north, in the Dutch-English 
2 CONC province at Schoharie, little to their comfort though they named their settlement L'Esperance! It was at this juncture that Governor Keith of Pennsylvania paid them a visit and, impressed with their character and worth, invited th
2 CONC em over to Pennsylvania by a well known and already much used route down the Susquehanna River. Being French and already twice warned, the Lady Clothilde was wary of promises. So Jean Henri, her eldest son, with an Indian guide wa
2 CONC s sent ahead to prospect. Perhaps it was she who stipulated rich lands as well as clear titles, or perhaps Jean Henri, now in his thirties, was already aware of the soil from a farmer's standpoint. At all events he journeyed down 
2 CONC the Susquehanna below where the great river branches at what is now Sunbury, paddled up a creek of some dimensions still known as the Swatara, and so across what is now the Lebanon Valley to a fertile region watered by the Tulpeho
2 CONC cken, in which black walnut trees of great age and fine verdure flourished. In the country of Jean Henri Zeller's mother's people, the French tradition was that black walnuts meant fertility and a deep soil. So armed with this ass
2 CONC uring promise, young Zeller journeyed by foot and canoe back to the Schoharie in northern New York and made his report to his mother and her party.
2 CONT
2 CONT They accepted the good sign at once. Rafts were built, provisions were packed, utensils, canoes and baggage were assembled and the contingent set forth, arriving at the Mill creek region not far from the present Newmanstown and th
2 CONC e larger settlement of Womelsdorf in 1723.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here the Zellers built a fort to protect themselves and their neighbors and procured deeds for land from three Penns - John, Thomas and Richard. Somewhat later they helped to start the first church of that vicinity. Under pressure
2 CONC  of Indian raids, in 1745 they rebuilt their log fort with stone outside and plaster over wattles inside, placing over the door the carved emblem of their faith and the family crest as knights of the Holy Roman Empire. About this 
2 CONC time the Zeller name was Germanized - Heinrich Zeller - for the America all about them was German in speech and behavior.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clothilde de Valois, who saw all this and much else come to pass, lived to be past eighty and to remain a personage long remembered in her family, a tradition of dignity, of authority, land of exalted if shadowy backgrounds, remai
2 CONC ning extant generation after generation."
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: a chapter in the book entitled Notable Women of Pennsylvania, edited by Gertrude Biddle and Sarah Lowrie (Philadelphia, 1942)
2 CONT
2 CONT Inscription:
2 CONT
2 CONT "To honor the first Zeller Family in America/
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother/ Clothilde de Valois Zeller/Born 1660 Died Jan 2 1749/ was a member of the Reformed Church of Tulpehocken/
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband/Jacques Zeller/Died at sea on the way over/
2 CONT
2 CONT Son/John Henry Zeller/who built For Zeller in 1745/ was a member of the Reformed Church of Tulpehocken/
2 CONT
2 CONT Son/John George Zeller/ Born 1685 Died 1737/ Mother and both sons buried in this cemetery/
2 CONT
2 CONT They came from the Palatine in 1710 and settled on the Schoharie in New York. They moved to the Valley in 1723./
2 CONT
2 CONT The Zeller Family and Association has loyally and gratefully erected this memorial in 1948" 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In 1660, the Zellers moved from Zurich to Deux Ponts, birthplace of Lady Clothilde de Valois, whom James married in 1682. It is often reported that he was her tutor & they eloped. They fled the Hugenot persecution & lived in Londo
2 CONC n for a few years, where they were awarded British citizenship by an Act of Parliament in 1709. They sailed to America in 1710 with a Land Grant from William Penn, but, James died at sea. His wife & sons survived, moving from Livi
2 CONC ngston Manor to Newburgh/New Paltz and finally settled in Tulpehocken, PA, where they established Fort Zeller on the "frontier of Philadelphia." Lady Clothilde de Valois lived there with her family until her death in January 1749 
2 CONC at the age of 89.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Wife of Jaques Zoller (Zeller). Buried at Reeds cemetery according to Herbert P. Reed's book "The Tulpehocken Reeds". Arrived in New York, New York in 1710. This is a credible account of her life and accomplishments:
