2 CONT *'''Duke of Chartres''': 1703–1723 '''Duke of Orléan''': 1723-1752
2 CONT >Predecessor:''' [https://www.geni.com/people/Philippe-II-d-Orl%C3%A9ans-r%C3%A9gent-du-Royaume-de-France/6000000000846872251 Philippe II Charles] '''Successor:''' [https://www.geni.com/people/Louis-Philippe-I-d-Orl%C3%A9ans-Duc/6
2 CONC 000000115421114821 Louis Philippe]
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Louis d'Orléans, Duke of Orléans====
2 CONT Louis d'Orléans, duc d'Orléans (4 August 1703 – 4 February 1752) was the Duke of Orléans and a member of the '''royal family''' of France, the '''House of Bourbon''', and as such was a prince du sang. At his father's death, he bec
2 CONC ame the First Prince of the Blood (Premier Prince du Sang). Known as Louis le Pieux and also as Louis le Génovéfain, Louis was a pious, charitable and cultured prince, who took very little part in the politics of the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Biography====
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis d'Orléans was born at the Palace of Versailles in 1703 to Philippe II, Duke of Orléans and his wife, Françoise Marie de Bourbon, the youngest legitimised daughter of Louis XIV and of his mistress Madame de Montespan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The only son of eight children, his '''siblings''' were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Mademoiselle de Valois (17 December 1693 – 17 October 1694)
2 CONT # Marie Louise Élisabeth d'Orléans, Duchess of Berry (20 August 1695 – 21 July 1719)
2 CONT # Louise Adélaïde d'Orléans, Abbess of Chelles (13 August 1698 – 10 February 1743)
2 CONT # Charlotte Aglaé d'Orléans, Duchess of Modena and Reggio (20 October 1700 – 19 January 1761)
2 CONT # Louise Élisabeth d'Orléans, Queen of Spain (11 December 1709 – 16 June 1742)
2 CONT # Philippine Élisabeth d'Orléans, Mademoiselle de Beaujolais (18 December 1714 – 21 May 1734)
2 CONT # Louise Diane d'Orléans, Princess of Conti (27 June 1716 - 26 September 1736)
2 CONT
2 CONT At his birth, he was given the courtesy title of Duke of Chartres as the heir to the Orléans fortune and titles. His maternal grandfather, the king, in addition gave him the pension reserved for the First Prince of the Blood, a ra
2 CONC nk he was not yet eligible to hold.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was brought up by his mother and his grandmother, Elizabeth Charlotte of the Palatinate, and tutored by Nicolas-Hubert Mongault, the illegitimate son of Jean-Baptiste Colbert de Saint-Pouange, a cousin of Jean-Baptiste Colbert,
2 CONC  Louis XIV's minister. He was very close to his mother, the two remaining close till her death in 1749.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis was very close to his younger sister Louise Élisabeth d'Orléans, who was to become Queen of Spain for seven months in 1724.[1] He was not, however, close to his older sister, Charlotte Aglaé d'Orléans, the wife of Francesco 
2 CONC d'Este, Duke of Modena. They were in frequent conflict during her many return visits to the French court from Modena.
2 CONT ====Regency====
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of his maternal grandfather Louis XIV in 1715, his father (the old king's nephew) was selected to be the regent of the country for the five year old new king, Louis XV. The court was moved to Paris so his father cou
2 CONC ld govern the country with the young king close by his side. Louis XV was installed in the Palais du Louvre opposite the Palais-Royal, the Paris home of the Orléans family. During the regency, Louis was seen as the "third personag
2 CONC e of the kingdom" immediately after Louis XV and his own father, the Regent. He was formally admitted to the Conseil de Régence on 30 January 1718. Despite his father's wishes, though, Louis was never to play an overly public or p
2 CONC olitical role in France. The following year, he was made the governor of the Dauphiné. He was not forced, however, to move there in order to fulfill his new duties. Later, he gave the title back to the Crown. In 1720, he became Gr
2 CONC and Master of the Order of Saint-Lazare and Jerusalem. In 1721, under his father's influence, he was named Colonel général de l'Infanterie and held that post until 1730.
2 CONT ====Duke of Orléans====
2 CONT Louis' father Philippe d'Orléans, 1716, Jean-Baptiste Santerre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of his father on 2 December 1723, the twenty-year old Louis assumed the hereditary title of Duke of Orléans and became the head of the House of Orléans. He also became the next in line to the throne of France until 
2 CONC the birth of Louis XV's first-born son in 1729. This was because King Philip V of Spain, the second son of the Grand Dauphin and uncle of the young king, had renounced his rights to the French throne for himself, and his descendan
2 CONC ts, upon his accession to the throne of Spain in 1700. Although the Regent had hoped that his son would assume as prominent a role in government as he had, the post of prime minister went to Louis' older cousin, Louis Henri, Duke 
2 CONC of Bourbon, when the Regent died. Constantly trying to consolidate and maintain his power at court, the Duke of Bourbon was always suspicious of Louis' motivations and was frequently opposed to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1723, Louis was conspicuous for his hostility to the former prime minister, Cardinal Dubois. Louis also worked with Claude le Blanc and Nicolas Prosper Bauyn d'Angervilliers in the post of Secretary of State for War; Louis hims
2 CONC elf worked in this position from 1723–1730
2 CONT ====Marriage====
2 CONT
2 CONT The fifth child and only son out of eight children, Louis was still not married at the death of his father. In 1721, the ambassador of France to Russia suggested a marriage between Louis and one of the two unmarried daughters of P
2 CONC eter I of Russia: the Grand Duchess Anna Petrovna (known for her fluency in French) or her younger sister, Grand Duchess Yelizaveta Petrovna. But the idea of a marriage with a Russian Grand Duchess had to be abandoned as there soo
2 CONC n arose difficulties relative to religion and order of precedence. Louis was "only" a great-grandson of the king of France and as such was only entitled to the style of Serene Highness. A Russian grand duchess, however, as a daugh
2 CONC ter of the tsar, was entitled to the style as Imperial Highness. Anna Petrovna later married a duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Gottorp.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another possible bride who was considered for him was his first cousin Élisabeth Alexandrine de Bourbon. She was the youngest daughter of his mother's older sister, Louise-Françoise de Bourbon. Élisabeth Alexandrine was also, howe
2 CONC ver, the younger sister of his main rival, the Duke of Bourbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1723, a German princess was suggested. She was Johanna of Baden-Baden (1704–1726), the daughter of Louis William, Margrave of Baden-Baden and his wife Sibylle Auguste of Saxe-Lauenburg. The marriage was agreed upon by his mothe
2 CONC r, and the bride's small dowry set at 80,000 livres. The marriage by proxy took place on 18 June 1724 at Rastatt, in Baden-Württemberg, Germany,[3] then on 13 July in the town of Sarry (Marne), in France.[4] It was at Sarry that t
2 CONC he couple first met. They fell in love at first sight. At the French court, the new Duchess of Orléans was known as Jeanne de Bade.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ducal couple had two children, but only one survived infancy
2 CONT ==== Later life====
2 CONT
2 CONT On 5 September 1725, the court celebrated the marriage of Louis XV to the Polish princess, Marie Leszczyńska at Fontainebleau. Earlier, Orléans had represented Louis XV at the proxy marriage ceremony, which had taken place the pre
2 CONC vious 15 August at Strasbourg.[6] The young queen would later have a lot of sympathy for the quiet and pious Duke.[citation needed][clarification needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year, on 8 August 1726, the duke's young wife died three days after the birth of her second child, Louise Marie, at the Palais-Royal in Paris. After the early death of his wife, and until his own death in 1752, Louis
2 CONC  lived by strict rules.[clarification needed]
2 CONT His mother, Madame d'Orléans, by François de Troy.
2 CONT
2 CONT His aunt, Élisabeth Charlotte d'Orléans Duchess of Lorraine, proposed her two daughters Élisabeth Thérèse de Lorraine[7] and Anne Charlotte de Lorraine as possible wives; Louis refused outright.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1730, Cardinal Fleury secured the duke's dismissal from the position of colonel-general of the infantry, a post he had held for nine years. Afterwards, Orléans became increasingly religious. Around 1740, he ordered the employme
2 CONC nt of a priest at the Palais Royal to stay with him during ill health. He later decided to retire at the Abbaye Sainte-Geneviève de Paris.[8] From then on, he became known as Louis le Génovéfain.[9] As he retired into private life
2 CONC , Louis spent his time translating the Psalms and the Pauline epistles, protecting men of science and managing his wealth.[10] Like his cousin, the Duke of Penthièvre, he was praised for his charitable works. After the birth of hi
2 CONC s son, Louis was often preoccupied with the education of his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Louis Philippe would liked to have married Madame Henriette, the second daughter of Louis XV, but Louis XV refused. The king did not want the House of Orléans to be as powerful as it had been during the regency of Orléans
2 CONC ' father.[11] In 1737 he, along with his aunt the Dowager Duchess of Bourbon, were asked to be godparents of the kings son, Louis de France, Dauphin of France (1729–1765).
2 CONT
2 CONT On 17 December 1743, Orléans' son married Louise Henriette de Bourbon, the daughter of Louis Armand, Prince of Conti and his wife, Louise Élisabeth de Bourbon. The Condé and Orléans families had been at odds since the Orléans had 
2 CONC assumed the rank of First Prince of the Blood in 1709, and it was hoped that the marriage would settle their mésentente. Although passionate at first, the marriage soon proved unhappy because of the young bride's débaucherie.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Philippe d'Orléans would see the birth of his grandchildren Louis Philippe (1747–1793) and Bathilde (1750–1822) who, during the French Revolution of 1789, would be known respectively as Philippe Égalité and Citoyenne Vérité.
2 CONC  Because of the scandalous behaviour of their mother, he refused to acknowledge the legitimacy of his grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1749, his mother died.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1752, at the age of forty-eight, at the Abbaye de Sainte Geneviève, having lost most of his sanity. On his deathbed, on suspicion of Jansenist views, he was refused communion by the Abbé Bouettin of the Saint-Étienne-du
2 CONC -Mont church, but was given the last rites by his own chaplain.[13] Louis d'Orléans had outlived all his siblings apart from Charlotte Aglaé, the Duchess of Modena and Reggio.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried at the Val-de-Grâce in Paris.
2 CONT ====Legacy====
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis was praised as a very charitable man; in Versailles the now destroyed College d'Orléans was named after him due to his generous patronage of the college's construction. He also remodelled the gardens at the Palais-Royal as w
2 CONC ell as the Orléans country residence, the Château de Saint-Cloud (c.1735). Louis was also praised for giving generous financial aid to victims of floods in the Loire in 1731 and again 1740;
2 CONT ===Issue===
2 CONT # Louis Philippe d'Orléans,Duke of Orléans, 12 May 1725 –18 November 1785 	Born at Versailles, he was titled Duke of Chartres (duc de Chartres) at birth; became Duke of Orléans (duc d'Orléans) upon his father's death in 1752; marr
2 CONC ied Louise Henriette de Bourbon in 1743 and had issue;
2 CONT # Louise Marie d'Orléans, Mademoiselle Blason duche fr Orleans (moderne), 5 August 1726 –14 May 1728. Born at the Palais Royal, she was known as Mademoiselle till her early death at Saint Cloud.
