1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Moray House, Edinburgh, was the venue for the wedding reception:
2 CONT
2 CONT That day the house was the venue for the wedding reception of Lord Lorne, son of the Marquess of Argyll, and Lady Mary Stuart, daughter of the Earl of Moray. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Moray House became a teacher training college in 1848, and it remains in that use today as part of the University of Edinburgh. However it is regularly part of Doors Open Day, and that is a great opportunity to see another reminde
2 CONC r of the house’s grand past.  The Cromwell Room, named after one of its famous guests, still has its dramatic and highly ornate plasterwork ceiling, dating back over 300 hundred years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT according to http://www.familysearch.org; a possible birth date is 21 July 1629 or June 1629 however some of the other dates or data  don't quite " fit "
2 CONT
2 CONT http://histfam.familysearch.org/getperson.php?personID=I80173&tree=Nixon
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p2210.htm#i22093
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thevalleyfamily.org/getperson.php?personID=I17558564475&tree=fitzvalley
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Lady Mary Stuart''' (Darnaway Castle, Elginshire, 1628 - May 1668), married at Canongate, Edinburgh, Midlothian, on 13 May 1650 Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll (Dalkeith, Edinburgh, Midlothian, 26 February 1628/9 - Cross
2 CONC  of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, Midlothian, 30 June 1685), and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Stuart,_4th_Earl_of_Moray]
2 CONT
