1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>George, ninth Lord Seton and fourth Earl of Winton</strong> was born 4 May 1642, and succeeded his grandfather 17 December 1650. On 12 May 1653 he was served heir-male to him in the Haddingtonshire properties (including t
2 CONC he barony of Hailes), and lands in other counties ; ' and on 28 September 1655 he was served heir to him in certain lands in Banff, Elgin and Forres. He was fined 2000 sterling by Cromwell's so-called Act of Grace and Pardon, 12 A
2 CONC pril 1654; the amount was afterwards reduced to 800." He was bred a Protestant by his uncle and tutor Alexander, first Viscount Kingston. He finished his education in France. His uncle states that in 1666 he was ordered to take co
2 CONC mmand of the county force and join General Dalzell In his advance against the rebels in the Pentland Hills. A year or two later he seems to have been again in France, for he is said to have served with distinction at the siege of 
2 CONC Besancon in February 1668, probably as a volunteer under Conde. He had commission, dated 2 December 1669, to be colonel of a regiment of militia appointed by Parliament to be raised in the shires of Linlithgow and Peebles. He held
2 CONC  this command until a successor was appointed on his receiving commission, dated 8 January 1675, to be colonel of the Militia regiment of Foot in East Lothian. On the same day King Charles II. addressed the Privy Council of Scotla
2 CONC nd, requiring them to admit George, Earl of Winton as one of their number." His name was accordingly included in a new commission on 12 July 1676. In 1679 he ' went with his regiment upon his own charges, with all his vassals in n
2 CONC oble equipage ' to Bothwell Bridge, against the West Country rebels. He was saved along with H.R.H. the Duke of York when the Gloucester frigate, bound for Leith, which had the Prince and his suite on board, was wrecked with great
2 CONC  loss of life off Yarmouth, 6 May 1682. On 1 April 1683 he was appointed Sheriff of East Lothian. He served with his regiment 'upon his own expense with his vassalls ' under the Earl of Dumbarton during Argyle's rebellion in 1685,
2 CONC  and is said to have succeeded the latter as Great Master of the Household in Scotland the same year. On 31 July 1686 he had a charter of novodamus and new erection of his dignities, with remainder to the heirs-male of his body, w
2 CONC hom failing, to any person he should name with the like remainder, and failing such nomination, to his heirs-male, failing whom, to his nearest heirs and assignees, the eldest daughter always succeeding without division, etc. He b
2 CONC uilt the harbour called Port Seton at Oockenzie. He died 6 March 1704, and was buried at Seton ; but his remains were exhumed by order of his son the fifth Earl, and reinterred with great pomp beside those of his second wife in th
2 CONC e Collegiate Church of Seton, 18 December 1708. 
2 CONT
2 CONT George, fourth Earl of "Winton, married first, 4 September 1662, his kinswoman Mary Montgomerie, eldest daughter of the second marriage of Hugh, seventh Earl of Eglinton. She died in 1677. They had issue an only daughter : 
2 CONT * Mary, baptized 24 November 1666 ; 12 died at the age of three years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married secondly, in or before 1682, Christian, daughter and heir of George Hepburne, of Addinstoun jure uxoris (a younger son of John Hepburne of Eistcraig), by Margaret, daughter and heir of Robert Addinstoun of that Ilk (par
2 CONC . Tranent). The Earl had lived with Christian Hepburne for some years before their marriage, which was called in question long afterwards at the instance of Archibald, second Viscount Kingston, the next heir to the honours of Wint
2 CONC on, but without success. She died 18 November 1703, and was buried at Seton Church. The issue of the second marriage was : 
2 CONT * George, who succeeded as fifth Earl of Winton;
2 CONT * Mr. Christopher, who died unmarried at Seton. 5 January 1705."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun08pauluoft#page/600/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. VIII, page 601
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www2.thesetonfamily.com:8080/cadets/The_Extended_Nobility.htm
2 CONT Many families of great houses in this country of Scotland, derive their origins from the House of Seton.  Beginning during the time of Seier de Lens, the founder of the House of Seton, many of the subsequent offspring of the famil
2 CONC y either founded names of their own by taking that of their estate (though they were still recognized as being Seton's); or adopted the surname of a great house or family to which a son had married into and continued their family 
2 CONC name.
2 CONT
2 CONT "And if so be that a man's house has had beginning by gift of Princes or other great men, it will give occasion to their posterity to be true and thankful servants to their superiors, to that effect that they may augment their hou
2 CONC se. And if it has begun by just acquisition by a man's silver or goods, it will give occasion in like manner to their posterity to be virtuous and not wasteful, that they may add to their heritage. And if it has begun with marriag
2 CONC e of ladies of heritage, it will give like occasion to their posterity, when such a thing happens, to praise it. And if it has come by succession of family, it will give occasion to every man to ally his son and apparent heir with
2 CONC  the best and most honest house and party that he may win to, in hope that such chance may fall to his posterity".  George Seton, 4th Earl of Winton.
