2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on James Hamilton:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10597.htm#i105968
2 CONT
2 CONT Lt.-Gen. James Hamilton, 4th Duke of Hamilton [1]
2 CONT *M, #105968, 
2 CONT *b. 11 April 1658, 
2 CONT *d. 15 November 1712
2 CONT *Last Edited=29 Dec 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.93%
2 CONT
2 CONT Lt.-Gen. James Hamilton, 4th Duke of Hamilton was born on 11 April 1658.[1] He was the son of William Douglas-Hamilton, 1st Earl of Selkirk and Anne Hamilton, 3rd Duchess of Hamilton.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Lady Anne Spencer, daughter of Robert Spencer, 2nd Earl of Sunderland and Lady Anne Digby, on 5 January 1686/87.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Elizabeth Gerard, daughter of Digby Gerard, 5th Baron Gerard of Gerard's Bromley and Lady Elizabeth Gerard, on 17 July 1698 at Bushey Park, London, England.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 15 November 1712 at age 54 at Hyde Park, St. James's, London, England, killed in a duel with Lord Mohun, who was also killed.[3] He was buried on 29 October 1718 at Hamilton, Scotland.[4] He was buried at Westminster Ab
2 CONC bey, Westminster, London, England, temporarily.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was styled as Earl of Arran between 1658 and 1698.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was educated at University of Glasgow, Glasgow, Lanarkshire, Scotland.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Gentleman of the Bedchamber between 1679 and 1688.[1] He held the office of Envoy to Paris between 1683 and 1685.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the rank of Colonel in 1685 in the service of the 6th Horse.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Envoy to Paris in September 1686.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Knight, Order of the Thistle (K.T.) on 29 May 1687.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the rank of Brigadier-General in 1688.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Master of the Great Wardrobe from 1688 to 1689.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 December 1688, when King James II was elected from Whitehall, he was one of the four peers who accompanied him to Rochester, consequently being on the side opposed to his own father.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He and Barbara Fitzroy were associated circa 1690.[6] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the rank of Lieutenant-General in 1693.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded to the title of 4th Marquess of Clydesdale [S., 1643] on 9 July 1698.[1] He succeeded to the title of 4th Duke of Hamilton [S., 1643] on 9 July 1698.[1] He succeeded to the title of 3rd Earl of Lanark [S., 1639] on 9 
2 CONC July 1698.[5] He succeeded to the title of 3rd Lord Machansyre and Polmont [S., 1639] on 9 July 1698.[5] He succeeded to the title of 4th Lord Aven and Innerdale [S., 1643] on 9 July 1698.[1] He succeeded to the title of 4th Earl 
2 CONC of Arran and Cambridge [S., 1643] on 9 July 1698.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Gentleman of the Bedchamber between 1699 and 1702.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 May 1700 he took his oath and seat in the Scottish Parliament. While he was a strong opponent of the Union [Scotland], he took no part in the contemplated insurrection.[5] He held the office of Representative Peer [Scotland]
2 CONC  between 1708 and 1712.[5] In March 1707/8 he was suspected of abetting the abortive invasion of Scotland by the French in the Jacobite interest, was apprehended at Ashton, Lancashire, and brought to London, although no evidence c
2 CONC ould be obtained against him.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Lord-Lieutenant of Lancashire between 1710 and 1712.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) on 13 December 1710.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created 1st Duke of Brandon [Great Britain] on 10 September 1711.[5] He was created 1st Baron of Dutton [Great Britain] on 10 September 1711.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 August 1712 he was appointed as Ambassador to Paris, but never actually took up the position due to his untimely death.[3] He held the office of Master General of the Ordnance in September 1712.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Knight, Order of the Garter (K.G.) on 26 October 1712.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He has an extensive biographical entry in the Dictionary of National Biography.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cockayne writes that "the duel with Lord Mohun was occasioned by a dispute regarding the division of the Gawsworth estate, to which his wife and Lady Mohun were coheirs. In a contemporary letter, penned from London within a week o
2 CONC f the duel, the writer states of the Duke 'I assure that he has more friends at present than ever he had while alive'. Macky wrote of him 'on the Queen's accession he made strong efforts to get into administration, but has not yet
2 CONC  succeeded, though he is well received at Court; he is brave in his person, with a rough air of boldness; of good sense, very forward and hot for what he undertakes, ambitious and haughty; a violent enemy, has been very extravegen
2 CONC t in his manner of living, but not grown covetous; he is supposed to have some thoughts towards the Crown of England when the Queen dies, beign descended from the house of Stuart, &c. Has a great estate, is of a middle stature, we
2 CONC ll made, of a black coarse complexion, and a brisk look.1' " 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cockayne then quotes Dean Swift " 'he was made master of the ordnance; a worthy good natured person, very generous but of a middle understanding; he was murdered by that villain Macarney, an Irish Scot'. Bishop Burnet, who knew hi
2 CONC m well, says 'I will add no character of him; I am sorry I cannot say so much good of him as I could wish, and I had too much kindness for him to say any evil without necessity.[1]' "
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Lt.-Gen. James Hamilton, 4th Duke of Hamilton
