1 NOTE The first Swedish ruler of the Palatinate dynasty, he became king when his 
2 CONT cousin, Queen Christina, abdicated in his favor. Charles succeeded to the 
2 CONT throne of an almost bankrupt kingdom; nevertheless, he hoped to increase 
2 CONT the territory of the country at the expense of nations weakened by the 
2 CONT Thirty Years' War. In 1655, on the pretext of preventing his cousin, King 
2 CONT John II Casimir of Poland, from seizing the Swedish crown, Charles invaded 
2 CONT Poland. He formed an alliance with Frederick William, elector of 
2 CONT Brandenburg, and was victorious at Warsaw. Soon thereafter Russia, 
2 CONT Denmark, and the Holy Roman Empire all declared war on Sweden, and 
2 CONT Frederick William switched sides when the Poles agreed to recognize his 
2 CONT territorial claims. Charles retired from Poland, but in 1658, leading his 
2 CONT army across the frozen sea, he forced the Danes to capitulate. By the 
2 CONT Treaty of Roskilde, King Frederick II of Denmark ceded all his lands in 
2 CONT southern Sweden to Charles. Hoping for a total conquest of Denmark, 
2 CONT Charles attacked again later in the year but was rebuffed by a stout 
2 CONT Danish defense of Copenhagen. Charles died soon thereafter, and he was 
2 CONT succeeded by his son Charles XI.
