2 CONT
2 CONT Victor Amadeus I, Prince of Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Victor Amadeus, Prince of Carignan)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Question book-new.svg
2 CONT
2 CONT 	This article needs additional citations for verification.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please help improve this article by adding reliable references. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (August 2009)
2 CONT
2 CONT A portrait of Victor Amadeus
2 CONT
2 CONT Victor Amadeus of Savoy, Prince of Carignan, born in Turin on February 29, 1690, died in Turin on April 4, 1741, was Prince of Carignan from 1709 to 1741. He was the son of Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy, Prince of Carignan and his w
2 CONC ife, the beauty Maria Angela Caterina d'Este.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Turin, he was the third child of four and the eldest son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Made a Knight of the Annunciation in 1696, he married, at Moncalieri on November 7, 1714, Marie Anne Victoire France of Savoy (1690 - 1766), legitimised daughter of Victor Amadeus II of Sardinia, King of Sardinia and of Jeanne Bap
2 CONC tiste d'Albert de Luynes, Countess of Verrue. Their children were :
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Joseph Victor Amédée (1716 - 1716)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anne Thérèse of Savoy (1717 - 1745), married in 1741 to Charles de Rohan, prince de Soubise (1715 - 1787)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis-Victor of Savoy (1721 - 1778), Prince of Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Victor Amédée (1722, died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * a daughter, born in 1729
2 CONT
2 CONT His father-in-law showed affection for him but ended up depriving him, in 1717, of his 400,000 livres of annual income because of excessive spending. It was then that he ran away to France, at the end of 1718, in order to take pos
2 CONC session of his inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since he had lost the Château de Condé to Jean-François Leriget de La Faye when it was confiscated from his family by Louis XIV) on March 6, 1719, he established himself in the hôtel de Soissons, which he transformed, with his wif
2 CONC e who had followed him there, into a "sumptuous gaming house" which for a time sheltered the economist John Law. He died, ruined, and his hôtel was razed to construct in its place a grain-trading hall, now the site of the Bourse d
2 CONC e commerce de Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a passion for the Paris Opéra, and was named intendant of the Menus-Plaisirs by Louis XV, he brought about the disgrace of the tax farmer Alexandre Le Riche de La Poupelinière after he caught him in the company of his mistr
2 CONC ess, the actress Marie Antier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source
2 CONT
2 CONT     * translation of the French Wikipedia article.
2 CONT
