2 CONT Edward Woodman, the son of Edward, was baptized Dec. 27, 1606 at Corsham, Wiltshire, England. (pp. 516-522)[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Joanna ___ by 1628 and they came to New England from Christian Malford, Wiltshire about 1635, and together with Archelaus, his younger half brother, settled in Newbury, MA. Archelaus came from England in the ship James,
2 CONC  (p. 512)[1]the name of the ship which brought Edward is unknown. (p. 109-110)[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Woodman was one of the ninety one grantees who settled Newbury and one of fifteen of that number who was entitled to be called "Mr". [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a husbandman as well as serving many offices in Newbury. He served as executive officer or selectman from the beginnings of Newbury and for several years hence. He owned extensive lands including a house lot with 4 acres, a
2 CONC  house lot with 1 acre, 45 acres field and upland meadow, 20 acres of salt marsh, a 4 acre planting lot, as well as several other parcels of upland, salt and fresh marsh and meadow. These additional parcels ranged from a few acres
2 CONC , to 300 and 500 acre parcels. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT c1635: Admitted to the Church at Newbury. [1] 1636: May 25: Made a Freeman. [1] 1636/7: One of the first executive officers ie. selectmen of Newbury. [4] 1637: 38: 41: 42: 45: 46: 49: Re-elected Selectman. These men were elected q
2 CONC uarterly and Edward may have served in such position for other terms than noted, as the same group of men are noted as voting town issues.[4] 1638: Aug. 6: Edward and other executive officers of Newbury adopted the 1635 ruling of 
2 CONC the General Court, regarding building of houses without permission in any town. [4] 1637: April: Lieutenant in a company sent against the Pequots. (p. 258)[5] 1645: 46: 50-53: 56: 58: 60: 62-64: 71: 83: Commissioner at Newbury to 
2 CONC end small causes. [1] [4] 1637/:8: March 12: to 1644: Edward appointed by the General Court to be allowed to sell wine and strong waters at Newbury. [4] 1645/6: Jan. 2: Edward and others on a committee to settle the placement of t
2 CONC he meeting house on a Knoll near Abraham Toppans barn. [4] 1647: Sept: 28: Assigned as jury man for the Essex 'petit jury.' [1] [4] 1652: Nov. 29: On the committee to have a school house built and pay a schoolmaster. [1] 1656: May
2 CONC  5: On the committee to meet with men of Rowley and Ipswich for the division of Plum Island. [1] 1658: 59: Newbury commissioner to solemnize marriages. [1] 1659: 60: 64: 70: Deputy for Newbury to the Massachusetts Bay General Cour
2 CONC t. [1] [4] 1660: Oct. 16: Formally granted a lot of 300 acres between the Rowley and Newbury line, which he had apparently held for some twenty years. [1] 1665: May 23: Appointed to the committee on the boundary between Hampton an
2 CONC d Salisbury. [1] [4] 1655: Nov. 13: On the committee to regulate workmen's wages. [1] 1662: 63: Essex magistrate. [1] 1663: Apr. 14: Again on a committee regarding the boundary between Hampton and Salisbury. [1] 1669: 70: 71: He w
2 CONC ith others, opposed via petition to the Courts, the attempt made by the Rev. Thomas Parker to change the mode of church government from Congregationalism to something like Presbyterianism.[1] (p. 109-110)[6] 1665: 1676: Edward or 
2 CONC his son Edward Jr., assigned as jury man for the Essex 'petit jury.[1] 1677: Sept. 25: Edward or his son Edward Jr., assigned to the Grand Jury. [1] 1681: March 25: He conveyed his dwelling house, barns, pastures, orchards etc. to
2 CONC  his son Jonathan in return for maintenance of Edward and wife Joanna during their natural lives. [1][6] Edward died between August 1688, when he appears on the tax rolls for Newbury and July 3, 1692, when his son Jonathan sold la
2 CONC nd of his late father. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His memorial is in the First Burying Ground, Newbury, MA and the inscription reads: In memory of MR. EDWARD WOODMAN who came from England and settled in Newbury in 1635. "A man of talents, influence, firmness and decision." He ser
2 CONC ved faithfully for many years as Selectman Deputy to the General Court and Commissioner. He died about 1690.[7] [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward born about 1628; married Mary Goodridge on Dec. 20, 1653 at Newbury.[1][9] John born about 1630; married first on July 15, 1656, Mary Field [9]; married second on Oct. 17, 1700 at Dover, widow Sarah (Burnham) Huckins. [1] J
2 CONC ane, likely their daughter, buried at Corsham Wiltshire July 4, 1633. [1] Joshua born about 1637; died on May 30, 1703; married Elizabeth Stevens at Andover of Jan. 22, 1665. [1] [10] Mary born about 1639; married John Brown, Feb.
2 CONC  20, 1659. [1])[9] Sarah born Jan. 12, 1641; married John Kent Jr. March 13, 1665.[1] [9] Jonathan born on Nov. 5, 1643; married Hannah Hilton on July 2, 1668. [1] [9] Ruth born March 28, 1646; married Benjamin Lowle on Oct. 17, 1
2 CONC 666. [1] [9] Source notes
2 CONT
