2 CONT
2 CONT Family ID  F47  Group Sheet 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family  
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Bellemain,   
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 1662, Mannheim, Baden-Wuerttemberg, Germany ,   
2 CONT
2 CONT d. 1699, Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, USA  
2 CONT
2 CONT Married  1690  New York City, New York, USA   
2 CONT
2 CONT Children    
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Maria Mellott (named in will, married name Hooper)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. John Mellott (named in will)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Peter Mellott (named in will, inherited all of father's lands in West Indies)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Hannah Mellott,   
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 1691, Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, USA ,   
2 CONT
2 CONT d. 1750, Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, USA  
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Theodorus Mellott,   
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 27 January 1693, Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, USA ,   
2 CONT
2 CONT d. 19 Mar 1751, Hagerstown, Washington, Maryland, USA  
2 CONT
2 CONT OUR ANCESTOR
2 CONT
2 CONT Family ID  F46  Group Sheet 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Pierre Mellott Will 
2 CONT
2 CONT Page: 314
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: John Peterson Mellat, Mallat, Marlet 
2 CONT
2 CONT Date: 30 Jan 1702-3
2 CONT
2 CONT Location: Piscattaway blacksmith; will of. Wife Mary, who has had daughter Lidia Jegou (under age) by previous marriage. Children--Peter, John, Theodor, Hanna (under age), Maria Hooper. Land in the West Indies, farm in Piscattaway
2 CONC  Township. Personal property incl. debts due for work on the fort in N. Y. and from Capt. Nanfan, for the recovery of which James Emmot is to be employed; a silver spoon, two negro slaves, three gold rings. The wife executrix with
2 CONC  John Royse and Gabriel Boytoo1 both of Piscattaway, as overseers. Witnesses--John Langstaff, John Manning and Isaac Smalley. Proved April 18, 1704. Lib. 1, p. 32, and Middlesex Wills 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Pierre Marlet (also written Marlett and Merlet), the son of Gideon Merlet and Marguerite Martijn, was born on 18 April 1658 at Mannheim in the Palatinate, Germany (Mannheim is now within Baden-Wuerttemberg, Germany). He was s
2 CONC ubsequently christened in the French Church at Mannheim on 2 May 1658. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young boy of four, Jean Pierre accompanied his family on the trip to America, where shortly after arriving they settled on Staten Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Pierre was apparently married twice during his lifetime. His first wife, and the mother of our direct line ancestor, Theodorus Melott, was Marie Bellemain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Jean Pierre and Marie continued to reside on Staten Island for a number of years after their marriage, they eventually moved to Middlesex County, New Jersey, settling first in the vicinity of Perth Amboy, then later in th
2 CONC e vicinity of Piscataway. Although Jean Pierre purchased approximately 280 acres of land in Piscataway Township from the estate of Major James Giles during the month of April 1693, it does not appear that he settled there permanen
2 CONC tly until several years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime after moving to New Jersey, Jean Pierre's first wife passed away. Jean subsequently married for a second time to a widow named Mary Jabou. No issue are known from Jean's second marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Pierre, who was a blacksmith by trade, died in Piscataway Township in 1704. His last will and testament, which had been written on 30 January 1703, was probated before the Middlesex County Court on 18 April 1704. In his will,
2 CONC  Jean Pierre mentions his second wife, Mary, and her daughter, Lydia Jabou (underage), from her previous marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although some researchers believe Jean Pierre may have moved into Gloucester County, New Jersey just prior to his death, this has not been verified.
2 CONT
