0 @N6000000006444108132@ NOTE Jeanne d'Albret
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Jeanne d'AlbretJeanne d'Albret (January 7, 1528 – June 9, 1572) was Queen of Navarre from 1555 to 1572, wife of Antoine de Bourbon, duke of Vendome and mother of Henry IV of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jeanne was born in Pau, Aquitaine in 1528, the daughter of Henry II of Navarre and Marguerite of Navarre. Marguerite was the sister of Francis I of France, and Jeanne grew up at the French court. When she was thirteen, Francis mar
1 CONC ried her to the Duke of Cleves, but this political marriage was annulled four years later.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Francis and the accession of Henry II Jeanne was married to Antoine de Bourbon, "first prince of the blood," who would become heir to the French throne if the Valois line died out.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1555 Henry II of Navarre died, and Jeanne and her husband became rulers of Navarre.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the first year of her reign, Jeanne d'Albret called a conference of beleaguered Huguenot ministers which led to her declaring Calvinism the official religion of her kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT The power struggle between Catholics and Huguenots for control of the French court and France as a whole led to the outbreak of the French Wars of Religion in 1562. Antoine de Bourbon chose to support the Catholics, but was mortal
1 CONC ly wounded at the siege of Rouen. Jeanne's son Henry now became "first prince of the blood."
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1567 war broke out again, and Jeanne fled to the Huguenot city of La Rochelle. From here she conducted peace negotiations, and in 1570 official talks began to marry Henry to the king's sister Marguerite. She died in Paris two m
1 CONC onths before the wedding took place.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Titles 
1 CONT 1.1 by birth 
1 CONT 1.2 by marriage 
1 CONT 2 Marriages and Issue 
1 CONT 3 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Titles
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT by birth
1 CONT Queen of Navarra (1555-1572) 
1 CONT Duchess of Albret (1555-1572) 
1 CONT Countess of Limoges (1555-1572) 
1 CONT Countess of Foix (1555-1572) 
1 CONT Countess of Armagnac (1555-1572) 
1 CONT Countess of Bigorre (1555-1572) 
1 CONT Countess of Périgord (1555-1572) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT by marriage
1 CONT Duchess of Bourbon (1548-1562) 
1 CONT Duchess of Vendôme (1550-1562) 
1 CONT Duchess of Beaumont (1550-1562) 
1 CONT Countess of Marle (1548-1562) 
1 CONT Countess of La Fère (1548-1562) 
1 CONT Countess of Soissons (1550-1562) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriages and Issue
1 CONT In 1541 Jeanne married William, Duke of Cleves, annulled in 1545, with no issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT On October 20, 1548 she married Antoine de Bourbon and they had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Henri, Duc de Beaumont (1551-1553) 
1 CONT Henri IV, King of France (1553-1610) 
1 CONT Louis (1555- 1557) 
1 CONT Madeleine (1556) 
1 CONT Catherine de Bourbon (1559-1604). Married Henry I, Duke of Lorraine in 1599. 
0 @N6000000001956357407@ NOTE
1 CONT Jeanne d'Albret (January 7, 1528 – June 9, 1572) was Queen regnant of Navarre from 1555 to 1572, wife of Antoine de Bourbon, duc de Vendôme and mother of King Henry IV of France.
1 CONT Jeanne was born in Saint-Germain-en-Laye, Yvelines in 1528, the daughter of Henry II of Navarre and Marguerite of Navarre. Marguerite was the sister of Francis I of France, and Jeanne grew up at the French court. In 1541, when she
1 CONC  was thirteen, Francis married her to William "the Rich", Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg, but this political marriage was annulled four years later.
1 CONT After the death of Francis and the accession of Henry II Jeanne was married to Antoine de Bourbon, "first prince of the blood," who would become heir to the French throne if the Valois line died out.
1 CONT In 1555 Henry II of Navarre died, and Jeanne and her husband became rulers of Navarre.
1 CONT Jeanne d'Albret was protestant and the daughter of King Henri II of Navarre and Marguerite of Angouleme, Duchess of Alencon, who was the sister of King Francis I of France. The Kingdom of Navarre allowed for the daughter of a Mona
1 CONC rch to inherit the throne, but the Kingdom of France did not
1 CONT Navarre was a small Kingdom which straddled the Pyrenees. It had become even smaller by the time of Henri's birth, the southern part having been annexed into Spain (1512). However the family also possessed other territories includ
1 CONC ing Béarn, which adjoined Navarre. When looking south from the Chateau of Pau or from nearby Coarraze, Henri would have observed the river below the Chateau, then a green landscape edged in the distance with snowcapped mountains. 
1 CONC The Peak of Midi D'Ossau would have been one of the most prominent landmarks in the mountain range.
