0 @N6000000000337133204@ NOTE (Research):Good King Philip?  12/12/98  Economist     Magazine: The Economist; December 12th-18th 1998  Section: MOREOVER  Spanish empire ==============  GOOD KING PHILIP?  ----------------- Despite the Inquisition, Philip II of S
1 CONC pain really deserves a better press  HIS was the first global empire, and in time it became the world's largest dominion. When he died, 400 years ago in 1598, the Spanish king, Philip II, left behind an empire that stretched westw
1 CONC ards from the Mediterranean to the shores of the Pacific. He was a cautious and scrupulous man, possessed of a heavy sense of duty, and believing himself to be the one true defender of the Catholic church against the twin threats 
1 CONC of Protestantism and Islam. The external vestiges of his power were impressive and rigorously promoted. In truth, his monarchy was over-stretched and up to its neck in debt. And his personal renown never recovered from his associa
1 CONC tion with the Spanish Inquisition.  In terms of recognition due, his story has not been a happy one. A Spanish philosopher, Miguel de Unamuno, always to the point, summed up Philip's predicament as 
1 CONT
1 CONT a monarch poorly known and even less understood''. He was capable, pious, respected by his people. He eschewed the sort of personal cult enjoyed by Elizabeth I of England or Henry IV of France. But these qualities were eclipsed by
1 CONC  the 
1 CONT
1 CONT black legend'' promoted by his Protestant opponents for whom he was the bogey-man of Europe ruling a hostile foreign power, a cruel authoritarian despot and the protagonist of the Inquisition. Verdi and Schiller were fascinated an
1 CONC d revolted by him. A recent biographer, Henry Kamen, claims in 
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip of Spain'' (Yale; $35), however, that Philip was 
1 CONT
1 CONT denigrated in his own day by political foes abroad, by Protestants everywhere. [He] acquired a sinister reputation that the passing of time has only succeeded in blackening further. [For] the Protestant propaganda machine was unqu
1 CONC estionably superior.''  Philip himself did not help this situation. He remained implacably opposed to public communication as a way of explaining or justifying his causes and concealed the way decisions were made. He was a copious
1 CONC  writer of nearly illegible instructions and memoranda, but kept no diaries and never wrote a memoir. Nor did he allow any official biography. The reason, according to one of the ambassadors who wrote his obituary, was Philip's av
1 CONC ersion to vanity. But one result was that the first studies of Philip were undertaken by his enemies who set the tone for subsequent biographies.  Help may now, at last, be at hand. Several studies aimed at repositioning Philip II
1 CONC  in a more neutral and objective light have been published in the last few years. Most have been carried out outside Spain, by historians such as Mr Kamen and Geoffrey Parker, based on extensive original research among the tens of
1 CONC  thousands of manuscripts that exist around the world. This re-evalution of Spain's prudent but controversial king is now also beginning to gain ground within Spain. Fernando Checa, a leading art historian and director of the Prad
1 CONC o Museum in Madrid, recently published a study on Philip II as a Renaissance prince. Several exhibitions commemorating the anniversary of his death have been mounted this year in Castile, Philip's home base, and more will open ove
1 CONC r the next 18 months.  Curiously (or maybe not?) it is a government-backed initiative, run by the Sociedad estatal para la conmemoracion de los centenarios de Felipe II y Carlos V, that is spearheading Philip's refit, with the hel
1 CONC p of a budget totalling 1.2 billion pesetas ($8.4m). Why has a government run by the conservative Partido Popular (PP) decided to give Philip II such a boost? Many Spaniards believed state support for the arts would dry up when th
1 CONC e PP came to power. Quite the opposite; they seem as keen as mustard to show their cultural credentials. Philip II, with his association with religion, centralism and a strong focus on Madrid, stands not a million miles from the P
1 CONC P's own ideology. Long a darling of the right, Philip was made much of by the Francoists, and was correspondingly frowned upon by the left. Nowadays, to most Spaniards, he is regarded as a more neutral, purely historical figure.  
1 CONC The overall theme of the quadricentenary exhibitions has been 
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip II-A Monarch and his Time''. This is the 
1 CONT
1 CONT high-art'' approach-a demonstration of great artistic and cultural creations, underpinned by a hope that through a deeper understanding of the king and his circumstances the public will gain a better insight into one of the most d
1 CONC efining periods of Spanish and European history.  The sheer scale of the undertaking is astonishing. Already, there have been three major exhibitions. The show at the Escorial, Philip's megalomaniac palace-cum-monastery, focuses o
1 CONC n 
1 CONT
1 CONT The Hispanic Monarchy''. The Prado Museum, where most of the royal picture collections now are, features 
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip II-A Renaissance Prince''. Valladolid, where he was born, and coincidentally also the local constituency of the prime minister, Jose Maria Aznar, is the location of the third tour-de-force: 
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip II-The Lands and Men of the King''.  This broad-brush view deftly avoids trying to give Philip a fixed image or present him as a great national hero. It attempts a more didactic approach, organising material on a vast array
1 CONC  of topics and perspectives, and avoiding rather than encouraging their resolution. Nevertheless, there are several striking points about these exhibitions, when considered together. First is the reminder that propaganda and image
1 CONC -manipulation are not strictly 20th-century phenomena. Paintings were used to proclaim political and religious messages (often one and the same).  Second, the galleries of portraits reveal the extraordinary tentacle of the Habsbur
1 CONC g family and the overweening preoccupation with dynastic succession and the demands of strategic alliances. Philip was married and widowed four times: to Maria of Portugal, Mary Tudor of England, Elisabeth of Valois and Anna of Au
1 CONC stria, his niece. In-breeding was rife. His retarded son, Don Carlos, had only four (as opposed to eight) great-grandparents. Of Philip's ten children, only four survived to adolescence.  Third, religion was Philip's highest prior
1 CONC ity. Mr Parker in 
1 CONT
1 CONT The World is Not Enough: The Grand Strategy of Philip II'' (Yale; $35) describes the king's approach as a type of 
1 CONT
1 CONT messianic imperialism''-he felt he had a direct mandate to defend the Catholic Church against all enemies inside Europe and out. Visible proof of this is the huge number of images that exist of Philip II in direct communion with G
1 CONC od, for example El Greco's 
1 CONT
1 CONT Allegory of the Holy League'' and Titian's 
1 CONT
1 CONT Allegory of the Battle of Lepanto''.  Fourth was his formidable education and preparation for the task of ruling. His mother died when he was 11 and his father, Emperor Charles V, was often abroad, occasionally even for years at a
1 CONC  time. He executed his parental duties by remote control, issuing strict and precise instructions about the child's upbringing. Philip was left in the hands of a hierarchy of tutors. Luckily for him, they were a wise and learned g
1 CONC roup that included the humane Cardinal Siliceo, Cristobal Calvete de Estrella (who taught him Latin and Greek) and Juan GiDnes de Sepulveda (geography and history), as well as his rather more puritanical and severe personal tutor,
1 CONC  Juan de ZuDniga.  Philip's early reading included the complete works of Erasmus, and Copernicus's 
1 CONT
1 CONT De Revolutionibus'', which he obtained only two years after it was published. Many of these were later banned by the Inquisition. He bought thousands of books including works on music, mathematics, astronomy, history, geography, m
1 CONC agic, theology and philosophy. His visits to Italy and Flanders were also central to shaping his outlook, and his artistic tastes were strongly influenced by one of his aunts, Maria of Hungary, who owned a magnificent collection o
1 CONC f pictures including Van de Weyden's 
1 CONT
1 CONT Descent from the Cross'', later one of Philip's most treasured art works.  Philip grew up to have an enormous spread of interests, all of which come together in his main architectural project, the Escorial, home to one of the thre
1 CONC e exhibitions. Begun in 1563, the Escorial was the biggest building and artistic project in Europe, and modelled on an imaginary concept of Solomon's temple in Jerusalem. It combines a mausoleum for his family, with a palace, mona
1 CONC stery, cathedral and school. Everything about it affirms the validity of the Catholic doctrine. And it epitomises his conception of his dual role as king and man of God.  But is all this helping Philip II's cause? The exhibitions 
1 CONC portray a highly capable and interesting man. They underline his supremacy as a prince of the Renaissance. And they help us empathise with what it might have been like 
1 CONT
1 CONT to be in his shoes''. They are also, in their different ways, both awesome and magnificent; an opportunity to see the best of Spain's phenomenal collections of paintings, books and manuscripts together with some highlights such as
1 CONC  El Greco's 
1 CONT
1 CONT Portrait of a Cardinal'' from the Metropolitan Museum in New York. And in context. Together, these artefacts-maps, books, letters, medals, coins, armour as well as sculpture and pictures-provide vritually direct access to the 16th
1 CONC  century when Europe was in the grip of the ideological and religious struggle of the Counter-Reformation.  But there remains something historically frustrating, a truth that is still elusive. This is partly a result of trying to 
1 CONC convey the contradictions of such a complex person in a far-off age. But it stems also from an undertone of soft propaganda that needs to imply that Philip was a good guy. The organisers have tried to slip it in unnoticed, but it 
1 CONC is unmistakable, particularly in the over-worthy catalogues that accompany the shows.  It is a difficult fact to appreciate from the outside, but the Spanish still have great difficulty dealing with their own history. In addition 
1 CONC to being Philip's anniversary, 1998 is the centenary of a year known in Spain as 
1 CONT
1 CONT el disastre de '98'', marking the loss of Cuba and Puerto Rico to the United States, the start of the Spanish-American wars and the loss of the Philippines-the final toss of the Spanish empire. At the start of this year, it was th
1 CONC ought that 1998 would be a year of massive Spanish soul-searching reflecting on the defeat of the empire. Some believed it was not before time. But the Philip II bonanza has distracted Spaniards' attention and diverted them from a
1 CONC  far harder task.  PHOTO (BLACK & WHITE): Little big man  ****** Copyright of the publication is the property of the publisher and the text may not be copied without the express written permission of the publisher except for the i
1 CONC nprint of the video screen content or via the print options of the software. Text is intended solely for the use of the individual user.  Copyright of "Good King Philip?" is the property of Economist. Its content may not be copied
1 CONC  without the copyright holder's express written permission except for the print or download intended solely for the use of the individual user. Content provided by EBSCO Publishing. ================================================
1 CONC ========= Philip II, King of Spain 1556-1598, (1527-1598)  son of Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor 1519-1556  and Isabella of Portugal  Born 21 May 1527 Valladolid  Died 13 September 1598 Escurial  Married 31 January 1560 Guadalajara
1 CONC   Elisabeth de Valois, Princess of France  Born 2 April 1545 Fontainebleau  Died 3 October 1568 Madrid  Buried Escorial  Married 12 November 1570 Segovia  Anna, Archduchess of Austria  Born 2 November 1549 Cigales  Died 26 October
1 CONC  1580 Madrid   If people have to be categorized, would Philip II of Spain be placed with Napoleon Bonaparte and Adolf Hitler? All three inflicted death and destruction upon numerous peoples and nations. Also like Bonaparte and Hit
1 CONC ler, Philip II was a driven and complex personality.  It was political unrest and pressure in Spain which had forced Emperor Charles V, first: to marry his first cousin, Princess Isabella of Portugal; and secondly, to have his chi
1 CONC ldren brought up in Spain. And there, on 21 May 1527, in Valladolid, Philip II was born in the presence of his father and leading nobles.  In his youth Philip II saw very little of his father who, from 1529 to 1533, was in Italy a
1 CONC nd Germany. In 1535-1536 his father went to conquer Tunis, and 1539-1541 was spent in The Netherlands. Leaving Spain in May 1543, Charles V was forced to remain in Northern Europe until 1556. As his mother had died in 1539, it was
1 CONC  mainly courtiers and tutors who prepared him for his future role on the European stage. Cristobal Calvete de Estrella taught him Latin and Greek; Honorato Juan, mathematics and architecture; Juan Gines de Sepulveda, geography and
1 CONC  history. No-one was appointed for languages and, even though in time he understood French, Italian and Portuguese, he could not speak these languages. Political education was controlled by his father through correspondence.  At a
1 CONC  young age he was expected to attend ceremonies. At the age of twelve he led the funeral cortege for his mother from Toledo, where she had died, to Granada where she was to be buried. As it had been his mother who had disciplined 
1 CONC him, this task was now taken over by his governor, Don Juan de Zuniga.  At thirteen he started collecting books which he would do all his life. Music also would always be part of his life and he would travel with minstrels, choris
1 CONC ters and organs. In 1543 Philip was made Regent of Spain. He had learned to do everything with both dignity and grace, yet at the same time displaying an air of authority. But he had also learned to conceal his feelings and emotio
1 CONC ns.  On 15 November 1543 he married Princess Maria of Portugal who was his first cousin twice over. In May 1545 European colonists in Peru led a revolt, the first in his long life. On 8 July 1545 his son, Don Carlos, was born; fou
1 CONC r days later, his wife died aged only 17. He was all of 18. In 1548 Philip was ordered by his father to come to him in The Low Countries to meet his future subjects. And so, in Bruxelles on the 1st April 1549, they were re-united.
1 CONC    His coldness and standing on protocol during his long journey had given him a reputation of arrogance which travelled ahead of him. With his father he toured The Netherlands and tried to be more pleasant, flirting with ladies a
1 CONC nd trying to outdrink the beer-swigging Dutch nobles. He stayed in The Netherlands until 1551 when he returned to Spain. Now he was treated by his father as an equal who consulted him in matters of government. This was the beginni
1 CONC ng of his father's withdrawal from the world. Philip II was made King of Naples and Sicily and his marriage to Mary Tudor was arranged. He became King-Consort of England and, in October 1555, ruler of The Netherlands. After his fa
1 CONC thers abdication in January 1556, Philip became King of Spain. Charles V's brother, Ferdinand I, became Emperor while Charles V retired to San Yuste in Spain where he died.  Philip II preferred to stay in Spain, which meant an abs
1 CONC ence from his English wife. When it became clear that she would not be able to bear children, he stayed away from England altogether. His wanting to remain in control caused a delay in the decision-making process in his distant po
1 CONC ssessions. In 1561 Philip II announced that Madrid had become the seat of his government, and there it would stay from then on. At Aranjuez he built a palace with gardens and a small zoo. At Casa de Campo was a larger zoo with ele
1 CONC phants, rhinoceros, lions and even some swans brought from The Netherlands.  He was a great patron of scholars and most arts, but he did not approve of theatre and, besides not allowing any monarch to be portrayed on stage, in 159
1 CONC 8 he banned plays in Madrid altogether. Religion was taken very seriously as he had "no desire to be the ruler of heretics". He did give some tolerance to the Calvinists in The Netherlands and made some political alliances with Pr
1 CONC otestant rulers, and he protected Elizabeth I in England against papal deposition. He even used Lutheran troops to suppress Calvinist rebels in The Netherlands. Privately he had a sincere and deep religious faith, attending Mass d
1 CONC aily. Even though a devout Catholic, he nevertheless had problems with the popes. At the beginning of his rule, Paul IV declared war on him; Gregory XIII disapproved of the annexation of Portugal in 1580; Sixtus V would not contri
1 CONC bute to the Armada's attempt to conquer England in 1588; and Clement III, at the end of his life was on the side of his enemies, the French. He tried to ignore the Calvinist emergence in The Netherlands until Lamoraal, Count of Eg
1 CONC mond, came to Madrid. Though he kept him waiting as long as possible, Egmond left with honours but was deceived in matters of religious tolerance. Egmond's verbal assurances of Philip II's tolerance were counteracted by letters in
1 CONC sisting on the burning of heretics. Egmond was discredited and chaos resulted. In 1567 preparations were made to send the Duke of Alva at the head of a large army to quell the unrest. Before Alva arrived, the Prince of Orange fled
1 CONC  to Germany. The Prince's son, Philips Willem, was then kidnapped from his University and taken prisoner to Spain. Egmond was imprisoned and, after a mockery of justice, condemned to death and executed.  Juan de Escobedo's murder 
1 CONC was probably ordered by Philip II, the murder of the Prince of Orange encouraged and rewarded, and the garrotting of Montigny was on his command. The Inquisition found Lutherans in Spain and destroyed them. The Moors living in Spa
1 CONC in were persecuted with between 80,000 and 100,000 perishing when forced to be relocated from their traditional homelands. Philip II's third marriage, to Elisabeth de Valois, appeared to have been happier than the previous two. Af
1 CONC ter a first miscarriage in 1562, she was left for death by the Spanish doctors but saved by an  Italian doctor. Throughout her recovery Philip stayed with her and, four months later, resumed sleeping with her. In 1564 there was an
1 CONC other miscarriage. In 1566 Isabella was born, Catalina Michaela in 1567, followed by another stillbirth in 1568 when Elisabeth died. Philip II had ensured that he was present at each birth and, though disappointed with daughters, 
1 CONC Isabella had come to mean more to him than anyone else in his family.  Had Elisabeth produced a son, Philip II would probably not have remarried. However, in 1570 he did marry his niece, Anna of Austria, in search of an heir as Do
1 CONC n Carlos, his son by his first wife, had died in 1568 aged 23.  In 1566 Philip ordered Juan de Ovando to inquire into the administration of America as too many problems had emerged. He codified the laws relating to "the Indies" an
1 CONC d appointed Don Martin Enriquez as viceroy in Mexico and Don Francisco de Toledo in Peru. In the 1560's and 1570's many missionaries were sent to convert the Indians. In 1578 Sebastian, King of Portugal and nephew of Philip II, we
1 CONC nt on a military expedition to North Africa where he disappeared, leaving the Portugese throne to his elderly great-uncle, Enrique, Cardinal and Archbishop of Lisbon. But Enrique I would live only another two years, in which perio
1 CONC d another nephew of Enrique I, Antonio, made claims to be legitimate and that (as his deceased father Luis, Duke of Beja, was six years older than Enrique I) he should be king instead of Enrique. Enrique I then ordered an inquiry 
1 CONC in which all papers proving Antonio's legitimacy disappeared. In 1580 Enrique I died and, even though Antonio was proclaimed king, Philip II of Spain sent in his army and, ousting Antonio, added Portugal to his domains.  By 1590, 
1 CONC most of the Philippine Islands were under Spanish control which was achieved with little bloodshed. Controlling southern and middle America was Philip's greatest success. However, the inability to retain The Netherlands was his gr
1 CONC eatest failure, for the Dutch were supported by those countries Spain tried to keep out of America. The Spanish forces butchered French Protestants in Florida and, in 1568, Sir John Hawkins's expedition was annihilated by the Span
1 CONC ish.  In 1572 the revolt in The Netherlands increased. Discredited as greedy, cruel and corrupt, Alva was recalled in 1573 to be replaced by Don Luis de Requesens. The near bankruptcy of Spain, caused by the continuous revolts and
1 CONC  wars, did not help. In 1576 Philip's illegitimate half-brother, Don Juan of Austria, was sent to restore Spain's authority and was appointed governor-general. Don Juan, assured by his initial success, suggested that he and his se
1 CONC cretary, Juan de Escobedo, together with Philip II's secretary, Antonio Perez, take control of the Spanish government as Philip II, though not yet fifty, was getting "too old". Then, in 1577, he wanted Philip II's support to invad
1 CONC e England and dethrone Elizabeth I. By then the Spanish fortunes in The Netherlands had turned and William of Orange had become the more powerful man. Don Juan sent his secretary Juan de Escobedo to Madrid to ask for support, but 
1 CONC Escobedo started to meddle with politics and threatened Philip II's secretary Antonio Perez with blackmail. On Philip II's orders Perez had Escobedo murdered and, when a court-case took place, the king assisted with the escape of 
1 CONC the  hired assassins.  The reason for Escobedo's murder was fear of his returning to Don Juan in The Netherlands where he might encourage Don Juan against Philip II; at the same time, if Escobedo were arrested, Philip II risked Do
1 CONC n Juan's fearing exposure of his plots so that he might side with the Dutch rebels. In October 1578 Don Juan died of typhoid and, hoping the Escobedo scandal would quietly fade away, Philip II remained silent.  However, Perez had 
1 CONC an enemy in Mateo Vazquez, the king's chaplain and private secretary, who openly accused Perez of the murder and left the court in protest of Philip II's inactivity, throwing the government into chaos. Perez was offered honors, a 
1 CONC diplomatic post abroad and even a lucrative retirement; but he refused for he feared even an honorable removal would seem an admission of guilt and assassins would cut short his retirement. Philip II, calling Cardinal Granvelle to
1 CONC  take over the government, asked Vazquez to return.    However, Perez refused the offer of an ambassadorship in Venice and was placed under house arrest. Three years later he was charged with accepting bribes and betraying state s
1 CONC ecrets. In 1584 one of the hired assasins turned up and accused Perez who was then imprisoned and, on 23 February 1590, tortured to confess. As he could not prove that he had been under the king's orders, he was condemned to death
1 CONC . However, he escaped with proof of the king's involvement in Escobedo's murder.  Sir Francis Drake was another source of trouble as he was sailing around the world with a small fleet from 1577 until 1589, with the plundering of S
1 CONC panish ships as his only aim. In 1581 Philip II was recognised as King of Portugal but The Netherlands declared that they had replaced him with the French Duke of Anjou; and when this Duke announced that he was engaged to Queen El
1 CONC izabeth of England, Philip II's enemies united. In 1582 and 1583, these enemies supported Don Antonio in his efforts to regain Portugal. Also in 1583 Philip II renewed his efforts in The Netherlands which would result in the murde
1 CONC r of the Prince of Orange in 1584.  In 1585 Sir Francis Drake again set out to harrass the Spaniards in the West Indies and, on the way, sacked Vigo on mainland Spain. This meant war and so Spain prepared to invade England. Asking
1 CONC  Pope Sixtus V for financial support, Philip II was refused as his aims were power and had nothing to do with religious motives. However, his invincible fleet the "Armada" was built and placed under the command of the Duke of Medi
1 CONC na Sidonia. Late in July 1588 the fleet set out; however, fireships broke its formation and the Dutch prevented the Duke of Parma from joining it. In desperation the Duke of Medina Sidonia decided that the fleet should return to S
1 CONC pain, going northwards around the British Isles. The consequence was about sixty ships being lost and fifteen thousand men killed, first by the English and later in Ireland when they landed there exhausted.  The first news Philip 
1 CONC II received was that his fleet had beaten off the English, followed by a long silence which lasted almost a month; but then the full tragedy was at last revealed. Philip II's reaction, shared by the nation's feeling of despair, wa
1 CONC s : "I sent the fleet against men, not against wind and water". His later years were filled with concern for his family but saddened by the loss of his sons, leaving only the youngest, the future Philip III. Philip II died at the 
1 CONC Escurial in 1598, aged seventy-one. Source: Leo van de Pas
