0 @N6000000004121753566@ NOTE [2713952.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Acadian Landry family originated in La Ventrouze, near Montagne-au-Perche in the moder n French department of L'Orne. Jean-Claude came to Acadia about 1640-41 with his second wife , Marei Sallee and their son, Rene Landry did 
1 CONC LeJeune
1 CONT (theYounger) who was born in 1634 in F rance.They were accompanied by three of Jean-Claude's adult children from his first marriag e toa woman whose name remains ?. One of these three children, Perrine Landry, was acc ompanied by 
1 CONC her husband,
1 CONT jacques Joffriau. The other two were fraternal twins, Rene Landry d it L'Aisne ( the elder) and Anthoinette Landry. The twins were 22 years old and unmarried a t the time of their arrival.The family also included Marie's three chi
1 CONC ldren from her
1 CONT marriag e to the deceased Martin Aucoin. The family was likely attracted to Acadia by another daugh ter ofJjean-Claude, Marguerite, who was married to Robert Martin and ?  in Acadia for se veral years prior to 1640.All of Jean-Cla
1 CONC ude's
1 CONT grandchildren were born in or around Port Roy al. However, by the1670's that region that included the small twon and the farming seetleme nts along Riviere des Dauphins was getting corwded. Availabel farmland was becoming scarce ,
1 CONC  and several
1 CONT Acadian families began looking to the east to cultivate. Pierre Melanson was a lready established on the banks of the Bassin des Mines (Minas Basin) about 60 miles east o f Port Royal. In the late summer of 1680, a group of Acadia
1 CONC ns, including two
1 CONT sons of Rene La ndry dit LeJeune, Anthoine and Claude, moved east to join Pierre Melanson on the Bassin de s Mines. The group included Pierre Theriault, who would later become the brother-in-law of A nthoine and Claude. The group 
1 CONC first built a
1 CONT house for Pierre Theriault that served as shelte r for the entire group through the first winter. Then they began clearing high ground throug h the autumn and winter in order to construct home sites for the others. In the spring, 
1 CONC the y returned to
1 CONT the Port Royal region to plant and prepare the crop. After the next harvest , the group returned to the Bassin to continue the preparatory work for the new settlement.T he work done by this small group of settlers was the foundati
1 CONC on of the
1 CONT settlement of Grand P re, which later became famous as a principal point of embarkation for Acadian settlers durin g the deportation in 1755. Anthoine and Claude Landry eventually settled at Bassin des Mine s with their families o
1 CONC n the banks of
1 CONT the riviere-des-habitants. The other married brother s and sisters of Anthoine and Claude followed them to the area.
