1 CONT Charles II Stuart, King of Great Britain was the son of Charles I Stuart, King of Great Britain <p10138.htm> and Henrietta Maria de Bourbon. He was born on 29 May 1630 at St. James's Palace, St. James's, London, England.1 He marri
1 CONC ed Catarina Henriqueta de Bragança, Princeza de Portugal <p10140.htm>, daughter of Joao IV de Bragança, Rei de Portugal <p10140.htm> and Luiza Maria de Guzman <p10140.htm>, on 21 May 1662 at St. Thomas à Becket Church, Portsmouth,
1 CONC  Hampshire, England.1 He was also reported to have been married on 3 May 1662 at Winchester, Hampshire, England. He died on 6 February 1685 at age 54 at Whitehall Palace, Whitehall, London, England, from a stroke.2 He was buried o
1 CONC n 14 February 1685 at Westminster Abbey, Westminster, London, England.2  He gained the title of Duke of Cornwall on 29 May 1630.1 He gained the title of Duke of Rothesay on 29 May 1630.1 He was invested as a Knight, Order of the G
1 CONC arter (K.G.) on 21 May 1638.1 He gained the title of King Charles II of Great Britain on 8 May 1660.3 Charles II Stuart, King of Great Britain also went by the nick-name of 'Old Rowley'. Charles II Stuart, King of Great Britain al
1 CONC so went by the nick-name of 'the Merry Monarch'. He was godfather for Charles Somerset, Marquess of Worcester <p1058.htm> at his baptism.4 He was crowned King of England, Scotland, France and Ireland, Defender of the Faith on 23 A
1 CONC pril 1661 at Westminster Abbey, Westminster, London.3 He and Catherine Pegge <p10503.htm> were associated. He and Lucy Walter <p10503.htm> were associated. He and Barbara Villiers, Duchess of Cleveland <p10503.htm> were associated
1 CONC . He and Eleanor Gywnne <p10504.htm> were associated. He and Louise Renée de Penancoët de Kérouaille, Duchess of Portsmouth <p10504.htm> were associated. He and Mary Davies <p10504.htm> were associated circa 1672. He was godfather
1 CONC  for Charles Bruce, 4th Earl of Elgin <p2486.htm> at his baptism.5  When his father's cause was lost in 1646 he went to the Isles of Scilly then Jersey and on to France. In 1650 he was crowned King of the Scots at Scone. He invade
1 CONC d England in 1651 and was beaten by Cromwell at the Battle of Worcester and after the battle hid in an oak tree. With difficulty he escaped to France. Returning to England in 1660 he was welcomed as King. Plague struck the country
1 CONC  in 1665 killing over 60,000 in London alone and in the next year the Great Fire made 200,000 homeless. That was not all, the Dutch Fleet sailed up the Medway and England had to sue for peace. After the Great Fire, Sir Christopher
1 CONC  Wren built a new and better London. Charles had St James Park re-created and built Chelsea Hospital for old soldiers. He supported the sciences, founding Greenwich Observatory and the Royal Society. Boyle Halley and Newton are il
1 CONC lustrious names of his reign and Nell Gwynn will be remembered for other reasons. His Chief Minister was Clarendon who freed the Church of its Cromwellian past. Titus Oates raised the alarm of a Popish plot and many Catholics were
1 CONC  executed. Charles himself was a Catholic, certainly just before his death and probably before. He had many mistresses and the future Duke of Monmouth was his illegitimate son. Of twenty-six dukes in England today, five are descen
1 CONC dants on the wrong side of the blanket of Charles II. His neice was married to William of Orange, as a diplomatic measure. He brought much needed elegance to the land. He has an extensive biography in the Dictionary of National Bi
1 CONC ography.6  <e77.htm> <e77.htm>
