0 @N6000000003904597577@ NOTE
1 CONT James II, King of England 1685-1688
1 CONT  James VII of Scotland and James II of England (14 October 1633  16 September 1701) became King of Scots, King of England, and King of Ireland on 6 February 1685. He was the last Roman Catholic monarch to reign over the Kingdom o
1 CONC f Scotland, Kingdom of England, and Kingdom of Ireland. Some of his subjects distrusted his religious policies and alleged despotism, leading a group of them to depose him in the Glorious Revolution. He was replaced not by his Rom
1 CONC an Catholic son, James Francis Edward, but by his Protestant daughter and son-in-law, Mary II and William III, who became joint rulers in 1689.
1 CONT  The belief that Jamesnot William III or Mary IIwas the legitimate ruler became known as Jacobitism (from Jacobus or Iacobus, Latin for James). James made one serious attempt to recover his throne, when he landed in Ireland in 1
1 CONC 689. After his defeat at the Battle of the Boyne in the summer of 1690, he returned to France, living out the rest of his life under the protection of King Louis XIV. His son James Francis Edward Stuart (The Old Pretender) and his
1 CONC  grandson Charles Edward Stuart (The Young Pretender and Bonnie Princess Charlie) attempted to restore the Jacobite line after James's death, but failed.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Early life James, the second surviving son of Charles I and Henrietta Maria of France, was born at St. James's Palace in 1633 and created Duke of York in 1644. During the English Civil War he stayed in Oxford, a Royalist strongho
1 CONC ld. When the city surrendered in 1646, the Duke of York was confined in St James's Palace by parliamentary command. In 1648, he escaped from the Palace, from where he went to The Hague in disguise. When Charles I was executed by t
1 CONC he rebels in 1649, monarchists proclaimed the Duke of York's elder brother, Charles, as King Charles II. Charles II was recognised by the Parliament of Scotland and the Parliament of Ireland, and was crowned King of Scots at Scone
1 CONC , in Scotland, in 1651. He was, however, unable to secure the Crown of England, and consequently fled to France.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT  British Royalty House of Stuart
1 CONT  James II & VII Children   Mary II   Anne   James, The Old Pretender Grandchildren   Bonnie Prince Charlie   Henry, Cardinal-Duke of York Like his brother, James sought refuge in France, serving in the French army under Turenne. I
1 CONC n 1656, when his brother, Charles, entered into an alliance with Spainan enemy of Francehe joined the Spanish army under Louis, Prince of Cond.̌
1 CONT  In 1660, with Oliver Cromwell dead, Charles II was restored to the English Throne. Though James was the heir-presumptive, it seemed unlikely that he would actually inherit the Crown, for Charles was still a young man capable of f
1 CONC athering children. In September 1660, James (who was also created Duke of Albany in Scotland) wed Lady Anne Hyde, the daughter of Charles's chief minister, Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon.
1 CONT  James was appointed Lord High Admiral and commanded the Royal Navy during the Second (16651667) and Third Anglo-Dutch Wars (16721674). Following its capture by the English in 1669, the Dutch territory of New Netherland was name
1 CONC d New York in his honour. Fort Orange, 150 miles up the River Hudson, was renamed Albany in his honour as well. James also headed the Royal African Company, which participated in the slave trade.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Religion James was admitted to the Roman Catholic Church in about 1668 or 1669, although this was kept secret for some time. However, growing fears of Catholic influence at court, led to the introduction by Parliament of a new Te
1 CONC st Act in 1673. Under this Act, all civil and military officials were required to take an bath (in which they were required not only to disavow the doctrine of transubstantiation, but also denounce certain practices of the Roman C
1 CONC atholic Church as "superstitious and idolatrous") and receive communion under the auspices of the Church of England. James refused to perform both actions, instead choosing to relinquish the post of Lord High Admiral. His conversi
1 CONC on to Catholicism was now openly known.
1 CONT  Charles II opposed the conversion, ordering that James' children be raised as Protestants. Nevertheless, in 1673, he allowed James (whose first wife had died in 1671) to marry the Catholic Mary of Modena. Many English people, dis
1 CONC trustful of Catholicism, regarded the new Duchess of York as an agent of the Pope.
1 CONT  In 1677, James attempted to appease Protestants by allowing his daughter, Mary, to marry the Protestant Prince of Orange, William III (who was also his nephew). Despite the concession, fears of a Catholic monarch persisted, inten
1 CONC sified by the failed pregnancies of Charles II's wife, Catherine of Braganza. A defrocked Anglican clergyman, Titus Oates, falsely spoke of a "Popish Plot" to kill Charles and put the Duke of York on the Throne. The fabricated plo
1 CONC t caused a wave of anti-Catholic hysteria to sweep across the nation. On the orders of the King, the Duke of York left England for Brussels. In 1680, he was appointed Lord High Commissioner of Scotland and took up his residence at
1 CONC  the Palace of Holyroodhouse in Edinburgh.
1 CONT  In England, attempts were made by Anthony Ashley Cooper, the Earl of Shaftesbury, a former government minister, and now the leading enemy of James and a Catholic succession, to have him excluded from the line of succession. Some 
1 CONC even proposed that the Crown go to Charles II's illegitimate son, James Scott, 1st Duke of Monmouth. When, in 1679, the Exclusion Bill was in danger of passing, Charles II dissolved the English Parliament. (The Exclusion Bill cris
1 CONC is contributed to the development of the English two-party system; the Whigs were those who supported the Bill, whilst the Tories were those who opposed it.) Two further Parliaments were elected in 1680 and 1681, but were dissolve
1 CONC d for the same reason.
1 CONT  The Rye House Plot of 1683, a Protestant conspiracy to assassinate both Charles and the Duke of York, failed utterly; it increased popular sympathy for the King and his brother. James once again found himself influential in gover
1 CONC nment, and his brother restored him to the office of Lord High Admiral in 1684.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Reign Monarchical Styles of King James II of England
1 CONT  Reference style: His Majesty Spoken style: Your Majesty Alternative style: Sir
1 CONT  Monarchical Styles of King James VII of Scotland
1 CONT  Reference style: His Grace Spoken style: Your Grace Alternative style: Sir
1 CONT  Statue of James II in Trafalgar Square, London. This dates from 1686 and is attributed to the studio of Grinling Gibbons. It is one of only two known public statues of the monarch - the other resides at University College, Oxford
1 CONC . (January 2006)Charles died sine prole legitima (without legitimate offspring) in 1685, converting to Roman Catholicism on his deathbed. He was succeeded by his brother, who reigned in England and Ireland as James II, and in Scot
1 CONC land as James VII. James was crowned at Westminster Abbey on 23 April 1685. At first, there was little overt opposition to the new Sovereign. The new Parliament which assembled in May 1685 seemed favourable to James, agreeing to g
1 CONC rant him a large income.
1 CONT  James, however, faced the Monmouth Rebellion (led by Charles II's illegitimate son, the Duke of Monmouth). James Scott, 1st Duke of Monmouth declared himself King on 20 June 1685, but was afterwards defeated at the Battle of Sedg
1 CONC emoor. Monmouth was executed at the Tower of London soon afterwards. The king's judgesmost notably, George Jeffreys, 1st Baron Jeffreys (the "Hanging Judge")punished the rebels brutally. Judge Jeffreys' Bloody Assizes provoked l
1 CONC ittle comment at the time and were seen by many as an appropriate response to an armed rebellion.
1 CONT  To protect himself from further rebellions, James sought to establish a large standing army. By putting Roman Catholics in charge of several regiments, the King was drawn into a conflict with Parliament. Parliament was prorogued 
1 CONC in November 1685, never to meet again during James's brief reign.
1 CONT  Religious tension intensified from 1686: James controversially allowed Roman Catholics to occupy the highest offices of the Kingdom; he received at his court the papal nuncio, the first representative from Rome to London since th
1 CONC e reign of Mary I; James's Jesuit confessor, Edward Petre, was a particular object of Protestant ire and when James ordered the suspension of several Anglicans from political office, including Henry Compton, the anti-Catholic Bish
1 CONC op of London, he lost much of his previous support.
1 CONT  In the Declaration of Indulgence (1687), also known as the Declaration for Liberty of Conscience, James suspended laws punishing Roman Catholics and Protestant dissenters. (It is unclear if James issued the Declaration to gain th
1 CONC e political support of the dissenters, or if he was truly committed to the principle of freedom of religion.) The King also provoked opposition by his policies relating to the University of Oxford. He offended Anglicans by allowin
1 CONC g Catholics to hold important positions in Christ Church and University College, two of Oxford's largest colleges. Even more unpopularly, he dismissed the Protestant Fellows of Magdalen College, appointing Roman Catholics includin
1 CONC g Bishop Parker in their place.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Glorious Revolution Half-Crown coin of James II, 1686. The inscription reads IACOBUS II DEI GRATIA (James II by the Grace of God)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT  Main article: Glorious Revolution In April 1688, James re-issued the Declaration of Indulgence, subsequently ordering Anglican clergymen to read it in their churches. When the Archbishop of Canterbury William Sancroft and six oth
1 CONC er bishops (known as the Seven Bishops) submitted a petition requesting the reconsideration of the King's religious policies, they were arrested and tried for seditious libel. Public alarm increased with the birth of a Catholic so
1 CONC n and heir, James Francis Edward, to Queen Mary in June, 1688. (Some charged that the son was "suppositious", having been substituted for a stillborn child.) Threatened by a Catholic dynasty, several influential Protestants entere
1 CONC d into negotiations with William, Prince of Orange, who was James's son-in-law.
1 CONT  On 30 June 1688 a group of Protestant nobles, known as the Immortal Seven, requested the Prince of Orange to come to England with an army. By September, it had become clear that William sought to invade. James refused the assista
1 CONC nce of Louis XIV, fearing that the English would oppose French intervention. Furthermore, he believed that his own army would be adequate. But the King was too complacent; when William arrived on 5 November 1688, many Protestant o
1 CONC fficers defected and joined William. His own daughter, Anne, left the court, leading to considerable anguish on the part of the King. On 11 December, James attempted to flee to France, first throwing the Great Seal of the Realm in
1 CONC to the River Thames. He was, however, caught in Kent. Having no desire to make James a martyr, the Prince of Orange let him escape on 23 December. James was received by Louis XIV, who offered him a palace and a generous pension.
1 CONT  William convened an irregular Convention Parliament. (The procedure of calling a Convention Parliament had been previously used when succession to the Throne was unclear; it was a Convention Parliament which restored Charles II t
1 CONC o the Throne following the English Civil War and republican Commonwealth.) The Convention declared, on 12 February 1689, that James's attempt to flee on 11 December constituted an abdication of the government, and that the Throne 
1 CONC had then become vacant (instead of passing to James II's son, James Francis Edward). James's daughter Mary was declared Queen; she was to rule jointly with her husband William III. The Scottish Estates followed suit on 11 April of
1 CONC  the same year.
1 CONT  William and Mary subsequently granted their assent to an Act commonly referred to as the Bill of Rights. The Act confirmed the earlier Declaration of Right, in which the Convention Parliament had declared that James's flight cons
1 CONC tituted an abdication, and that William III and Mary II were to be King and Queen. The Bill of Rights also charged James II with abusing his power; amongst other things, it criticised the suspension of the Test Acts, the prosecuti
1 CONC on of the Seven Bishops for merely petitioning the Crown, the establishment of a standing army and the imposition of cruel punishments. The Bill also stipulated that no Catholic would henceforth be permitted to ascend to the Engli
1 CONC sh throne, nor could any English monarch marry a Catholic. The Act, furthermore, settled the question of succession to the Crown. First in the line of succession were the children of William and Mary (if any), to be followed by th
1 CONC e Princess Anne and her children, and finally by the children of William by any subsequent marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Later years With a French army on his side, James landed in Ireland in March 1689. The Irish Parliament did not follow the example of the English Parliament; it declared that James remained King. At James' urging, the Irish Parli
1 CONC ament passed an Act for Liberty of Conscience which granted religious freedom to all Catholics and Protestants in Ireland. The king was, however, defeated at the Battle of the Boyne by William III on 1 July 1690. He fled to France
1 CONC  after the defeat departing from Kinsale, his alleged cowardice leading to the dissolution of much of his support and earning him the nickname Sǎmus  ̀Chaca ("James the Shit") in Ireland.
1 CONT  In France, James was allowed to live in the royal cht́eau of Saint-Germain-en-Laye. An attempt was made to restore him to the Throne by assassinating William III in 1696, but the plot failed. Louis XIV's offer to have James elect
1 CONC ed King of Poland in the same year was rejected, for James feared that acceptance of the Polish Crown might (in the minds of the English People) render him incapable of being King of England. Thereafter, Louis ceased to offer assi
1 CONC stance to James. During his last years, James lived as an austere penitent. He died of a brain haemorrhage in 1701 at Saint-Germain-en-Laye, where he was buried.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Legacy James's younger daughter Anne succeeded to the throne when William III died in 1702. (Mary II had died in 1694.) The Act of Settlement 1701 provided that, if the line of succession established in the Bill of Rights were to
1 CONC  be extinguished, then the Crown would go to a German cousin, Sophia, Electress of Hanover, and to her Protestant heirs. Thus, when Anne died in 1714 (less than two months after the death of Sophia), the Crown was inherited by Geo
1 CONC rge I, Sophia's son, the Elector of Hanover and Anne's second cousin.
1 CONT  The son of James II, James Francis Edward Stuart (known to his supporters as "James III and VIII" and to his opponents as the "Old Pretender"), took up the Jacobite cause. He led a rising in Scotland in 1715 shortly after George 
1 CONC I's accession, but was defeated. Further risings were also defeated and since the rising of 1745 led by Charles Edward Stuart, no serious attempt to restore the Stuart heir has been made, although some individuals still adhere to 
1 CONC the philosophy of Jacobitism.
1 CONT  James Francis Edward died in 1766, when he was succeeded by his eldest son, Charles Edward Stuart (known to his supporters as "Charles III" and to his opponents as the "Young Pretender"). Charles in turn was succeeded by his youn
1 CONC ger brother Henry Benedict Stuart, a cardinal of the Catholic Church. Henry was the last of James II's legitimate descendants. At his death in 1807 the Jacobite claim devolved upon the senior descendant of King Charles I, King Cha
1 CONC rles Emmanuel IV of Sardinia. Presently, James II's heir is Franz, Duke of Bavaria. Although the Duke of Bavaria has not claimed the throne, he is recognised by Jacobites as "Francis II".
1 CONT
1 CONT  Style and arms The official style of James II was "James the Second, by the Grace of God, King of England, Scotland, France and Ireland, Defender of the Faith, etc." (The claim to France was only nominal, and was asserted by ever
1 CONC y English King from Edward III to George III, regardless of the amount of French territory actually controlled.) His arms as King were: Quarterly, I and IV Grandquarterly, Azure three fleurs-de-lis Or (for France) and Gules three 
1 CONC lions passant guardant in pale Or (for England); II Or a lion rampant within a tressure flory-counter-flory Gules (for Scotland); III Azure a harp Or stringed Argent (for Ireland). James was created "Duke of Normandy" by King Loui
1 CONC s XIV of France, December 31, 1660. This was a few months after the restoration of his brother Charles II to the English and Irish thrones (Charles II had been crowned King of Scotland in 1651), and was probably done as a politica
1 CONC l gesture of support for the James - since his brother also would have claimed the title "Duke of Normandy."
1 CONT
