0 @N6000000000592845642@ NOTE Savage 4:640 - *EDWARD, Newbury, elder br. of Archelaus,
1 CONT came, says Coffin, with him, bring. w. and s. Edward, b. 1628,
1 CONT and John; had here Joshua, the first Eng. male ch. of the town,
1 CONT b. 1636 or 7; Sarah, 12 Jan. 1642; Jonathan, 5 Nov. 1643; Ruth,
1 CONT 28 Mar. 1646; and, Coffin adds, perhaps others; was freem. 25
1 CONT May 1636, rep. Sept. foll. and 7, and sev. yrs. later. His wid.
1 CONT or w. Joanna is ment. 9 Nov. 1653 in the rec. but the time of
1 CONT his d. is not ment. [:ITAL]
1 CONT The gravestone, erected long after his death ... reads:
1 CONT In memory of
1 CONT MR. EDWARD WOODMAN
1 CONT who came from England and
1 CONT settled in Newbury in 1635.
1 CONT "A man of talents, influence,
1 CONT firmness and decision."
1 CONT He served faithfully for many years
1 CONT as Selectman, Deputy to the General
1 CONT Court and Commissioner.
1 CONT He died about 1690.
1 CONT aboard the "James" from London, having departed 06 Apr
1 CONT served in Newbury's first board of Selectmen, then called "the
1 CONT seven men"
1 CONT Edward Woodman appears on the first known list of the settlers
1 CONT of Newbury, compiled in 1642, as does his brother Archelaus --
1 CONT they were among the original 91 grantees.
1 CONT The Woodmans of Buxton ME, pp 3-5:
1 CONT In 1855, Mr. Joshua Coffin, of Newbury, prepared and
1 CONT caused to be printed for me, "A list of some of the descendants
1 CONT of Mr. Edward Woodman," the immigrant ancestor. I follow his
1 CONT list for the names of the children of Edward,1 Joshua,2 and
1 CONT Benjamin3; adding, however, such facts as I have elsewhere
1 CONT obtained, and especially noticing any discrepancies that there
1 CONT may be between his list and the information which I have
1 CONT derived from other sources.
1 CONT EDWARD.1
1 CONT MR. COFFIN prefaced his list with the following statement:
1 CONT "The town of Newbury, Mass., was settled and incorporated
1 CONT in 1635. In that year came Mr. Edward Woodman, his wife Joanna,
1 CONT and Archelaus Woodman, to Newbury, where they resided till
1 CONT their death. Archelaus came from a place called Christian
1 CONT Malford, a parish in Wiltshire, in the North-west part of the
1 CONT county, about six miles from Malmesbury, the chief town of that
1 CONT part of the country. He came passenger in the ship James, of
1 CONT London, which sailed from Southampton for New England in April,
1 CONT 1635. His name may be found in Vol. VII. of the Third Series of
1 CONT the Mass. Historical Society's Collections, page 319. He is
1 CONT there called Hercules Woodman. A few weeks later than the James
1 CONT came the Abigail, bringing a Richard Woodman, only 9 years old.
1 CONT Whether Mr. Edward Woodman, who was probably brother to
1 CONT Archelaus Woodman, came in the James or Abigail, or some other
1 CONT vessel, no record informs us. Both of them came to Newbury at
1 CONT the same time. Richard died in Lynn in 1647, aged 21.
1 CONT Archelaus, in 1635, is styled a 'mercer.' His first wife,
1 CONT Elizabeth, died 17th December, 1677. His second wife, Dorothy
1 CONT Chapman, whom he married 12th November, 1678, died in 1706. In
1 CONT the Newbury records he is styled Lieutenant, and died 14th
1 CONT October, 1702, aged about 90. He left no children. Of the
1 CONT ninety-one grantees who settled Newbury, fifteen were entitled
1 CONT to the appellation of 'Mr.' One of these fifteen was Mr. Edward
1 CONT Woodman. He was a man of influence, decision and energy, and
1 CONT opposed with great zeal the attempt made by the Rev. Thomas
1 CONT Parker to change the mode of church government from
1 CONT Congregationalism to something like Presbyterianism. [See
1 CONT History of Newbury, under the years 1669, 1670, &c.] Mr.
1 CONT Woodman was a deputy to the General Court in 1636, '37, '39 and
1 CONT '43. In 1638, '41, '45 and '46, he was one of the three
1 CONT commissioners to end small causes in Newbury, and at various
1 CONT times held other offices of profit and trust in town and State.
1 CONT Among his other commissions he had one from the State, 'to see
1 CONT people marry,' of which, in 1681, he thus speaks: 'An
1 CONT unprofitable commission; I quickly laid aside the worke, which
1 CONT has cost me many a bottle of sacke and liquor, where friends
1 CONT and acquaintances have been concerned.' He and his wife Joanna
1 CONT were living in February, 1687-8. She was then 74. He died prior
1 CONT to 1694. His age is not known."
1 CONT He was mistaken if he meant to say that Lieut. Archelaus1
1 CONT had no children. From a deed made by Archelaus1 to Archelaus
1 CONT Adams, dated Jan. 6, 1698-9, it appears that this Adams was his
1 CONT grandson, and that he had other grandchildren.
1 CONT From the will of John Brocklebank, of Rowley, dated Nov.
1 CONT 30, 1665, it appears that his wife Sarah was a daughter of
1 CONT Archelaus1 Woodman. They were married Sept. 26, 1657. Mr.
1 CONT Brocklebank, at his death (April, 1666), left two children,
1 CONT daughters.
1 CONT The birth of the children of Archelaus1 is not recorded in
1 CONT the Newbury records. Mr. Coffin says that he was about 90 when
1 CONT he died, in 1702, and that he and Edward1 came to Newbury in
1 CONT 1635. If Mr. Coffin states his age correctly when he died, then
1 CONT he was born about 1612, and came to this country when he was
1 CONT about 23 years of age. In 1678 he took the oath of allegiance,
1 CONT and is then stated to have been 60 years old, which makes his
1 CONT birth to have been in or about 1618, and his age 17 when he
1 CONT arrived in New England. It thus appears that he had two
1 CONT daughters married. As there is no record found of the birth of
1 CONT his children, I infer that they were few in number, consisting
1 CONT perhaps of two daughters only. There is no evidence that he had
1 CONT any male children. It is probable that all the male children
1 CONT born in Newbury were either sons or descendants of Edward.1
1 CONT Mr. Coffin says that Archelaus1 came from Christian
1 CONT Malford in England, which is probably correct, though I have
1 CONT not found his authority for the statement. His birth is not
1 CONT found recorded at Christian Malford, for "the registers" there
1 CONT "were all destroyed by lightning at the period required till
1 CONT 1702."
1 CONT Mr. Edward1 Woodman probably came from Corsham, a village
1 CONT in Wiltshire, about eleven miles from Christian Malford.
1 CONT There is on record (Vol. XIV. p. 57) in the Registry of
1 CONT Deeds, a long deed and agreement between Lieut. Archelaus1 and
1 CONT his grandson above mentioned; from which, by tracing down the
1 CONT title to the present day, one may be able to determine,
1 CONT perhaps, where his house stood. The deed is dated Jan. 6,
1 CONT 1698-9.
1 CONT On the 25th of March, 1681, Edward1 conveyed to his son
1 CONT Jonathan, "my now dwellinghouse, houses and barns and orchard
1 CONT and pasture and all my plow land lying by and adjoining to the
1 CONT said houses, as also all the plow lands upon the northwest side
1 CONT of the street lying upon the westward side of my house, the
1 CONT said street being vulgarly called the new street." The
1 CONT consideration for this conveyance was "natural and fatherly
1 CONT love and affection" and "twenty pounds which is yearly to be
1 CONT paid during the time of my own and my wife's natural life."
1 CONT This was in his old age, and was evidently an arrangement under
1 CONT which he and his wife were to have a home with and be provided
1 CONT for by their son Jonathan during their lives. He disposed of
1 CONT his property before his death, for there was no will proved and
1 CONT no letters of administration taken.
1 CONT Where his dwelling-house thus conveyed was situated I do
1 CONT not know; but a mortgage made by Jonathan2 in 1695, conveys
1 CONT "all my housing and land and orchard where I now dwell in
1 CONT Newbury." One of the bounds mentioned in the mortgage is
1 CONT "Woodman's lane," a name which is not yet entirely forgotten in
1 CONT Newburyport; within the limits of which town Edward1 and
1 CONT Archelaus1 both dwelt, as I suppose. I am inclined to the
1 CONT belief that the premises so mortgaged, covered the same or a
1 CONT portion of the same property which was given to him by his
1 CONT father, and that the "new street" and "Woodman's lane" were
1 CONT identical. If so, Edward1 lived in 1681, and probably for years
1 CONT before and at the time of his death, in what was afterwards and
1 CONT for a long time known as "Woodman's lane."
1 CONT The place where his house stood may, perhaps, be
1 CONT ascertained very nearly by any one who will trace Jonathan's
1 CONT title down to the present day.(*)
1 CONT He was one of the first selectmen of Newbury, having been
1 CONT elected in 1636, and his name heads the list as given. by Mr.
1 CONT Coffin. Thereafter, for many years, he was one of the leading
1 CONT men in town. In Coffin's History of Newbury, p. 73, he is
1 CONT called "a man of talents, influence, firmness and decision."
1 CONT I have not ascertained the date of his death nor the place
1 CONT of his burial. He was probably buried in the grave-yard
1 CONT opposite the old Coffin mansion, in Newburyport.
1 CONT The children of Edward,1 according to Coffin's list, were:
1 CONT i.  EDWARD,2 b. (???), 1628; m. Mary Goodridge, 20 Dec. 1653.
1 CONT ii.  JOHN,2 b. about 1630; m. Mary Field, 15 July, 1656.
1 CONT iii.  JOSHUA,2 b. (???), 1636; m. Elizabeth Stevens, 23 Jan.
1 CONT 1666. She died in 1714.
1 CONT iv.  MARY,2 b. (???); m. John Brown, 20 Feb. 1660.
1 CONT v.  SARAH,2 b. 12 Jan. 1642; m. John Kent, 12 March, 1666.
1 CONT vi.  JONATHAN,2 b. 8 Nov. 1643; m. Hannah Hilton, 2 July,
1 CONT 1668.(+)
1 CONT vii.  RUTH,2 b. 28 March, 1646; m. Benjamin Lowle, 17 Oct.
1 CONT 1666.
1 CONT This list is correct, so far as I know. Edward and John,
1 CONT it seems, were born before their father came to this country.
1 CONT The date of Joshua's birth is not found in the town records,
1 CONT nor of Mary or Jonathan.
1 CONT The date of Sarah's birth in the records in 1641. It was
1 CONT doubtless 1641-2. He is not known to have had any trade. In a
1 CONT deed dated in 1687, he is styled husbandman. Of his personal
1 CONT appearance nothing is known.
1 CONT (*)Since writing the above, I have learned that Woodman's Lane
1 CONT is now known in Newburyport as Kent Street.
1 CONT (+)1668, in Coffin's list, is misplaced, but here placed
1 CONT correctly. [:ITAL]
1 CONT was usually styled "Mr.," denoting a good position in the
1 CONT community -- only 15 of the original 91 grantees of Newbury
1 CONT were entitled to be called "Mr." in 1636.
1 CONT was licensed to sell strong drink
1 CONT was "conspicuous and active in the affairs of the Newbury
1 CONT Church;" "active in church agitation"
1 CONT applied to aid the magistrates in the execution of court
1 CONT decrees
1 CONT Lieutenant in a company sent against the Pequods
1 CONT served as Selectman of Newbury
1 CONT led the opposition to the Rev. Thomas Parker's attempt to
1 CONT change the church government; Rev. Parker wanted to change the
1 CONT church government from Congregationalism to something akin to
1 CONT Presbyterianism.
1 CONT and his wife Joanna were members of the church in Newbury
1 CONT took the Oath of Allegiance, giving his age as 60 years
1 CONT was commissioned by the state to "see people marry."; In that
1 CONT period in Massachusetts, people were not married by ministers
1 CONT in the church.  Edward wrote in 1681: "An unprofitable
1 CONT commission; I quickly laid aside the worke, which has cost me
1 CONT many a bottle of sacke and liquor, where friends and
1 CONT acquaintances have been concerned."
1 CONT deeded land to son Sgt. Jonathan Woodman on Woodman's Lane in
1 CONT Newbury; "My now dwelling-house, houses and barns and orchard
1 CONT and pasture, and all my plow land lying by and adjoining to the
1 CONT said houses, as also all the plow lands upon the northwest side
1 CONT of the street lying upon the westward side of my house, the
1 CONT said street being vulgarly called the Newstreet."  The
1 CONT consideration for this conveyance was "natural and fatherly
1 CONT love and affection" and "twenty pounds which is yearly to be
1 CONT paid during the time of my own and my wife's natural life."
1 CONT This street is now called Kent Street in Newbury.
1 CONT Seven Hundred Ancestors, p 90:
1 CONT Edward Woodman, bapt. Dec.27, 1606, was one of the 91
1 CONT grantees who settled Newbury, Massachusetts. He and his younger
1 CONT brother, Archelaus, settled in Newbury in 1635. He married
1 CONT Joanna Salway of Malford in Wiltshire, England. She was born in
1 CONT 1614.
1 CONT On May 25, 1636 Edward became a freeman and was a deputy
1 CONT in the general court in 1636, 1637, 1639 and 1643. In 1638,
1 CONT 1641, 1645 and 1646 he was one of three commissioners to end
1 CONT small causes in Newbury. He held other local and state offices
1 CONT at various times and is not known to have had a trade. In a
1 CONT deed dated 1687 he is referred to as a "husbandman" and was a
1 CONT man of decision, influence and energy. On Jan.12, 1637/8 he was
1 CONT licensed to sell strong drink. He died at Newbury in 1694 and
1 CONT his wife died there in 1687.
1 CONT The children of Edward and Joanna Salway Woodman were:
1 CONT 1. Edward, b. about 1628, Corsham; d. 1694, m. Dec.20, 1653,
1 CONT Mary Goodridge.
1 CONT 2. Jane, b. about 1630, Corsham; d. July 4, 1633 Corsham.
1 CONT 3. John, b. about 1634, Corsham; d. Sept. 17, 1706, m.  July
1 CONT 15, 1656, Mary Field, moved to Dover, N.H.
1 CONT 4. Joshua, b. about 1636 in Newbury; d. May 30, 1703, m.
1 CONT Jan.22, 1665, Elizabeth Stevens.
1 CONT 5. Mary, b. about 1640 in Newbury; m. Feb.20, 1659/60, John
1 CONT Browne.
1 CONT 6. Sarah, b. Jan.12, 1641 in Newbury.
1 CONT 7. Jonathan, b. Nov.5, 1643 in Newbury; d. Nov.21, 1706, m.
1 CONT July 2, 1668, Hanna Hilton.
1 CONT 8. Ruth, b. Mar.28, 1646 in Newbury; m. Oct.17, 1666,
1 CONT Benjamin Lowell. [:ITAL]
1 CONT Pope, Pioneers in Massachusetts, p.513 - Edward, mercer,
1 CONT merchant, of Malford, Eng. came in the James April 5, 1635;
1 CONT settled at Newbury frm. May 25, 1636. Licensed to sell wine and
1 CONT strong water 12 March, 1637. App. to aid the magistrates in
1 CONT execution of court decrees 6 (7) 1638. Deputy, town officer;
1 CONT active in church agitation. Wife Joanna. Either he or son
1 CONT Edward arranged with John Hull of Newbury for payment of an
1 CONT annuity. [Es. Files.] Ch. Edward, John, Joshua, Mary, (m. John
1 CONT Brown,) Sarah b. 12 Jan. 1641, (m. John Kent, Jr.,) Jonathan b.
1 CONT 5 Nov. 1643, Ruth b. 28 March, 1646, (m. Benjamin
1 CONT Lowell).[:ITAL]
1 CONT Ancestors of Raymond James Lowell:  Edward Woodman came
1 CONT from Southhampton, England 3 June 1635 in the ship "James" of
1 CONT London, England. Edward was a wealthy and prominent man. He and
1 CONT his brother Archelaus came over together and settled at
1 CONT Newbury, MA. (D. R. Lowell. 1899. The Historic Genealogy of the
1 CONT Lowells of America from 1639-1899)  D. R. Lowell (1899) showed
1 CONT seven children for Edward and Joanna.
1 CONT Edward Woodman moved to New England and settled at Newbury
1 CONT about 1635, where he was admitted a freeman on 25 May 1636. He
1 CONT was conspicuous and active in the affairs of the Newbury
1 CONT Church, and was usually styled "Mr.", thereby indicating his
1 CONT good position in the community. On 8 September 1636 he was
1 CONT chosen the deputy from Newbury to the General Court, and was
1 CONT thereafter frequently the deputy from that town. On March 17,
1 CONT 1637 he was a lieutenant of the Newbury company; on 6 September
1 CONT 1638 he was a commissioner to end small causes; and thereafter
1 CONT he frequently held these offices. On 12 January 1637/38 he was
1 CONT licensed to sell strong drink. As his name last appears in the
1 CONT Colonial records on 11 May 1670 as the deputy from Newbury, he
1 CONT evidently died not long afterwards. (Moriarty, G. A. English
1 CONT Origins of New England Families)
1 CONT Genserv page1 database says he died May 11, 1670.
1 CONT Genserv rainre1 database has birth before 1606 in Malford,
1 CONT England and death after 1688
1 CONT Genserv mccc6ba database has death date as 1692
1 CONT Genserv jarrell database has death date as 1694
1 CONT Edward came to America on the ship "James", embarking
1 CONT April 6 and arriving June 3, 1635. He settled in Newbury, MA,
1 CONT and was one of the prominent men in the history of that town,
1 CONT representing it several times in the General Court (Genserv
1 CONT mccc6ba database quoting from History of Durham, NH)
1 CONT Mercer, merchant, of Malford, England came in the James
1 CONT April 5, 1635; settled at Newbury; from May 26, 1636. Licensed
1 CONT to sell wine and strong water 12 March, 1637. Applied to aid
1 CONT the magistrates in execution of court decrees 6 (7) 1638.
1 CONT Deputy, town officer; active in church agitation. Either he or
1 CONT his son Edward arranged with John Hull of Newbury for payment
1 CONT of an annuity. He was a wealthy and prominent man; was
1 CONT Lieutenant in a company sent against the Pequods in April,
1 CONT 1637. He and his brother Archelaus d. without issue. Edward was
1 CONT living 1687, but date of d. not known. (Genserv rainre1
1 CONT database)
1 CONT Genserv jarrell database has birth date as 1614
1 CONT Marriage Notes:  Genserv teague1 database has marriage date
1 CONT as 1628[:ITAL]
1 CONT Allan's Genealogical Index:  John Woodman, Genealogy &
1 CONT History of the Descendants of Edward Woodman (1995); bapt. 27
1 CONT Dec 1606, Corsham, England; d. 11 May 1670, Newbury, MA;
1 CONT ancestor of Robert Frost[:ITAL]
1 CONT The Landing at Parker River (Newbury)
1 CONT [http://engc.bu.edu/~dcm/pr_history/newbhist.htm] From: "Ould
1 CONT Newbury": Historical and Biographical Sketches by John J.
1 CONT Currier (1896), Damrell and Upham, Boston:
1 CONT The settlers of Newbury were much like those of much of
1 CONT what is now northern Essex county. They were not religious
1 CONT enthusiasts or pilgrims who fled from religious persecution in
1 CONT England. They were substantial, law abiding, loyal English
1 CONT tradesmen, of that staunch middle class that was the backbone
1 CONT of England.
1 CONT Those that settled Newbury came at different times and on
1 CONT different ships, between the end of April, 1634 and July, 1635.
1 CONT In one of the first ships arriving in 1635, came Thomas Parker
1 CONT a minister along with a small company of settlers. They went
1 CONT first to Agawam (Ipswich) and later along with their
1 CONT countrymen, who came from Wiltshire, England, to Newbury.
1 CONT On May 6, 1635, before the settlers had moved from Ipswich
1 CONT to Newbury, the House of Deputies passed a resolution that
1 CONT Quascacunquen was to be established as a plantation and its
1 CONT name was to be changed to Newbury. So Newbury was named before
1 CONT the first settlers arrived, interestingly Thomas Parker had
1 CONT taught school in Newbury, Berkshire, England before coming to
1 CONT America.
1 CONT The first settlers came by water from Ipswich, through
1 CONT Plum Island Sound, and up the Quascacunquen River, which was
1 CONT later renamed the Parker River. There had been a few fisherman
1 CONT occupying the banks of the Merrimack and Parker rivers before
1 CONT this, but they were not permanent settlers. These settlers came
1 CONT to Newbury in May or June of 1635. Ships from England began to
1 CONT arrive almost immediately with cattle and more settlers.
1 CONT Governor Winthrop, in his history of New England under the date
1 CONT of June 3, 1635, records the arrival of two ships with Dutch
1 CONT cattle along with the ship "James", from Southampton bringing
1 CONT more settlers.
1 CONT Newbury was, therefore, begun as a stock raising
1 CONT enterprise and the settlers came to engage in that business and
1 CONT to establish homes for themselves. In total fifteen ships came
1 CONT in June and one each in August, November and December bringing
1 CONT still more families to the settlement.
1 CONT There is no record of how many families arrived in the
1 CONT first year. Houses were erected on both sides of the Parker
1 CONT River. The principal settlement was around the meeting house on
1 CONT the lower green. The first church in Newbury could not have
1 CONT been formed before June, as some of those recorded at its
1 CONT formation are not recorded as having arrived until June.
1 CONT In the division of land the first settlers recognized the
1 CONT scripture rule, "to him that hath shall be given," and the
1 CONT wealth of each grantee can be estimated by the number of acres
1 CONT given him.
1 CONT The reason for establishing Newbury, as stated above, was
1 CONT not in fleeing from religious persecution but to utilize vacant
1 CONT lands and to establish a profitable business for the members of
1 CONT a stock-raising company.
1 CONT When they arrived in Massachusetts, the settlers found
1 CONT that the state had established the Congregational form of
1 CONT religion. Everyone was taxed to support the Congregational
1 CONT Society and was commanded to attend worship at the meeting
1 CONT house. The Reverend Thomas Parker was a member of the stock
1 CONT raising company and was also the minister of the settlers.
1 CONT The outlying settlers had a long journey to the meeting
1 CONT house. The congregations were in danger of attacks from Indians
1 CONT and wild beasts on their way to and from worship. There was a
1 CONT constant dread of attack during the time of services and all
1 CONT able bodied inhabitants were required to bring their weapons to
1 CONT church. Sentinels were posted at the doors.
1 CONT In spite of the hardship and danger, the population
1 CONT steadily increased in number and gradually improved its worldly
1 CONT condition. Being cramped for room, the settlers moved up to the
1 CONT upper or training green. This was in order to get tillable land
1 CONT and engage in commercial pursuits. This movement began in 1642.
1 CONT Each had been allotted half an acre for a building lot on the
1 CONT lower green, on the upper green each was to have four acres for
1 CONT a house lot. Also on the upper green a new pond was
1 CONT artificially formed for watering cattle.
1 CONT The new town gradually extended along the Merrimack River
1 CONT to the mouth of the Artichoke River. It appears that all
1 CONT desirable land in this region was apportioned among the
1 CONT freeholders by October 1646. The land beyond was ordered to lie
1 CONT perpetually common. This tract of common land was a part of
1 CONT Newbury and what is now West Newbury. The Indian threat had
1 CONT disappeared as most of the Indians in the region had been
1 CONT exterminated by an epidemic. The first record of an Indian
1 CONT living in Newbury is in January 1644, when a lot was granted to
1 CONT "John Indian."
1 CONT Over the following years some notable, though not earth
1 CONT shaking events occurred in Newbury. In 1639, Edward Rawson
1 CONT began the manufacture of gun powder in what was probably
1 CONT America's first powder mill.
1 CONT Newbury had a trial for witchcraft thirteen years before
1 CONT the trials in Salem. In 1679, Elizabeth Morse was accused. She
1 CONT was condemned three times to die, but was reprieved and spent
1 CONT her last years in her home, at what is now Market square in
1 CONT Newburyport.
1 CONT The first American born silversmith was Jeremiah Dummer of
1 CONT Newbury who apprenticed to John Hull, an Englishman. He
1 CONT practiced his trade in what is now Newburyport. Jeremiah was
1 CONT the father of Governor William Dummer the founder of Gov.
1 CONT Dummer Academy. Jeremiah's brother-in-law, John Coney, engraved
1 CONT the plates for the first paper money made in America.
1 CONT In 1686, when the upper Commons (West Newbury) were
1 CONT divided among the freeholders of the town of Newbury, Pipestave
1 CONT Hill was covered with a dense forest of oak and birch. These
1 CONT trees were cut and used to make staves for wine casks and
1 CONT molasses hogsheads. For many years, this industry, the first of
1 CONT its kind in America, flourished and the place is still called
1 CONT Pipestave Hill.
1 CONT Limestone was discovered in Newbury in 1697. Previous to
1 CONT this all the lime used for building was obtained from oyster
1 CONT and clam shells. Mortar made from this lime was very durable
1 CONT and came, in time, to be almost as hard as granite. This
1 CONT business prospered for many years until a superior quality of
1 CONT lime was discovered elsewhere.
1 CONT The first toll bridge and shipyard in America were also in
1 CONT Newbury. The latter giving rise to the ship building industry
1 CONT which was to determine the prosperity of Newburyport in the
1 CONT coming centuries.
1 CONT (source;  NEWBURY - A Brief History
1 CONT [http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Plains/4028/history.htm])
1 CONT "In February, 1633-34 the Council for New England,
1 CONT assembled at Whitehall, England, adopted an order placing
1 CONT certain restrictions on the transportation of passengers and
1 CONT merchandise to the colony of Massachusetts Bay; and before the
1 CONT ship "Mary and John" and eight other vessels, then lying in the
1 CONT river Thames, were allowed to sail, instructions were issued
1 CONT expressly providing
1 CONT that the captains in command of these vessels "shall cause the
1 CONT Prayers contained in the Book of Common Prayers, established in
1 CONT the Church
1 CONT of England, to be said daily at the usual hours of Morning and
1 CONT Evening Prayers, and that they cause all persons on board said
1 CONT ships to be present at the same."
1 CONT In the ship "Mary and John" cam Thomas Parker, James
1 CONT Noyes, John Spencer, Henry Short,Henry Lunt, John Bartlett, and
1 CONT many others, who ultimately settled in Newbury. Upon their
1 CONT arrival in New England most of these passengers went to Agawam,
1 CONT now Ipswich, Mass., where they remained until the spring of
1 CONT 1635.
1 CONT Meanwhile Sir Richard Saltonstall, Henry Sewall, Richard
1 CONT and Stephen Dummer, with others from Wiltshire, England had
1 CONT organized a company for the purpose of stock-raising at a time
1 CONT when the prices for cattle, horses, and sheep were at their
1 CONT highest. They added to their own domestic herds some imported
1 CONT Flemish stock, and persuaded John Spencer, Henry Short, Richard
1 CONT Kent,Thomas Parker, and others to join them in the enterprise,
1 CONT and establish a settlement on the river Quascacunquen, now
1 CONT Parker River.
1 CONT Sept. 3, 1633, the General Court granted "John Winthrop,
1 CONT junior, and his assignes" permission to set up a trading house
1 CONT on the Merrimack River; and under date of May 6, 1635, the
1 CONT House of Deputies passed the following order:-
1 CONT Quascacunquen is allowed by the court to be a plantation, and
1 CONT it is referred to Mr. (John) Humphrey, Mr. (John) Endicott,
1 CONT Captain (Nathaniel) Turner, and Captain (William) Trask, or any
1 CONT three of them, to set out the bounds of Ipswich and
1 CONT Quascacunquen, or so much thereof as they can; and the name of
1 CONT said plantation shall be changed, and shall hereafter be called
1 CONT Newberry.
1 CONT Further, it is ordered that it shall be in the power of
1 CONT the court to take order that the said plantation shall receive
1 CONT a sufficient company to make a competent towne.
1 CONT Previous to this date, undoubtedly, a few venturesome
1 CONT fishermen had built temporary residences on the banks of the
1 CONT Merrimack and Quascacunquen rivers; but they were looked upon
1 CONT as trespassers and intruders, for the General Court had
1 CONT forbidden all persons from settling within their jurisdiction
1 CONT without leave.
1 CONT Rev. Thomas Parker and those associated with him, having
1 CONT obtained permission to begin a plantation "to be called
1 CONT Newberry", made preparations to remove from Ipswich early in
1 CONT the spring. There were no roads through the trackless forest,
1 CONT and the transportation of women and children and household
1 CONT goods overland was impracticable. Tradition asserts that they
1 CONT came by the way of Plum Island Sound, in open boats, and
1 CONT landed, in the month of May or June, 1635, on the north shore
1 CONT of what is now the river Parker, in a little cover about one
1 CONT hundred rods below the bridge; Nicholas Noyes, the brother of
1 CONT Rev. James Noyes, being the first to leap ashore.
1 CONT Near this secluded spot a number of summer cottages have
1 CONT recently been erected, giving to the place a pleasant,
1 CONT home-like look; but two centuries and a half ago the prospect
1 CONT was less agreeable and inviting.
1 CONT "...Eastward, cold, wide marshes stretched
1 CONT away,
1 CONT Dull, dreary flats without a bush or
1 CONT tree,
1 CONT O'ercrossed by winding creeks, where twice a
1 CONT day
1 CONT Gurgled the waters of the moon-struck sea;
1 CONT And faint with distance came the stifled
1 CONT roar,
1 CONT The melancholy lapse of waves on the low
1 CONT shore."
1 CONT Inland hills rising above hills stood like sentinels over
1 CONT the almost unbroken wilderness. Centuries before this memorable
1 CONT landing Indians had hunted in these forests and fished in the
1 CONT placid stream that ebbs and flows to the falls of Newbury; but
1 CONT only a few of that race remained to resist the encroachments of
1 CONT the white-faced strangers. Dismal and gloomy must have been the
1 CONT outlook as these brave pioneers gathered together at the close
1 CONT of the first day, and contemplated the prospect before them.
1 CONT They knew that wild beasts were roaming through the forests,
1 CONT and whether the red men would welcome them as friends or foes
1 CONT was as yet uncertain.
1 CONT "Their descendants can have but a faint idea of the
1 CONT difficulties they encountered, and of the dangers that
1 CONT continually hung over their heads, threatening every moment to
1 CONT overwhelm them like a torrent, and sweep the, with those who
1 CONT they dearly loved, to the silent tomb."
1 CONT Undismayed by these difficulties and dangers, the new
1 CONT settlers instinctively turned their attention to the
1 CONT cultivation of the soil and the development of the resources of
1 CONT nature. Here and there along the winding river they
1 CONT appropriated the few clear spots where the natives had formerly
1 CONT planted corn, and promptly took possession of the neighboring
1 CONT marshes where the growing crop of salt grass promised an
1 CONT abundant harvest. There was no lack of work; no room for idle
1 CONT dreamers. Houses had to be built, land ploughed and tilled, and
1 CONT sheds erected for the protection of cattle before winter set
1 CONT in. House lots, planting lots, and meadow lots were laid out
1 CONT and granted to individual members of the community, and the
1 CONT original entries, giving names and dates, can still be seen on
1 CONT the old records of the town; but how many houses were erected
1 CONT or how many families settled in Newbury during the first year
1 CONT of its existence it is impossible to state with exactness.
1 CONT Governor Winthrop, in his History of New England, under
1 CONT date of June 3, 1635, records the arrival of two ships with
1 CONT Dutch cattle; and the same day the ship "James" arrived from
1 CONT Southampton, bringing, among other passengers, John Pike,
1 CONT father of the famous Robert Pike, of Salisbury, and one Thomas
1 CONT Coleman, who had been employed b the projectors of the
1 CONT stock-raising company to provide food for the cattle and take
1 CONT care of them for a specified term of years.
1 CONT In the Massachusetts Colony Records, under date of July 8,
1 CONT 1635:- It is ordered that there shall be a convenient quantity
1 CONT of land set out by Mr. Dumer and Mr. Bartholemewe, within the
